LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t1",title:"Test 1",topics:"Notices and messages · Summer activities · A repair café · Life on a canal boat · The oldest trees · Learning to cook",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`SPORTS CENTRE
The swimming pool will close at 6 pm on Friday for cleaning.
The gym stays open as usual.`,o:["You can't use the gym on Friday evening.","Swimmers should come before 6 pm on Friday.","The whole sports centre closes early on Friday."],a:1,x:"Only the pool closes at 6 pm, so swimmers need to come earlier. The gym is ‘open as usual’, so A and C are wrong."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Mia – Mum needs the car tomorrow, so she can't drive us to the concert. Could your dad take us? If not, there's a bus at 7.15. Jess",q:"Why has Jess sent this message?",o:["to explain why she can't go to the concert","to suggest leaving for the concert earlier","to ask if Mia's father can help"],a:2,x:"‘Could your dad take us?’ Jess still plans to go – by bus if necessary – so C is wrong, and nothing says the bus is earlier than planned (B)."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`To: Year 10 students
From: Mr Harris
The school trip to the science museum is now full. If you have paid but can no longer go, please tell me by Wednesday so another student can take your place.`,o:["Students who can't go on the trip must pay for someone else.","There are still a few places left on the trip.","Mr Harris needs to know soon if anyone wants to cancel."],a:2,x:"‘Please tell me by Wednesday’ – he needs to know soon. The trip is full (B), and nobody has to pay for another student (A)."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`FRESH ORANGE JUICE
Keep in the fridge.
Once opened, drink within three days.
Shake before pouring.`,o:["The juice stays fresh for three days after you buy it.","You should drink the juice within three days of opening it.","The juice only needs to be kept cold after it is opened."],a:1,x:"‘Once opened, drink within three days’ – the three days start when you open it, not when you buy it (A). It must always be kept in the fridge (C)."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`GUITAR FOR SALE
Only two years old – hardly used.
Comes with a case and a beginner's book.
£60, or I'll swap it for a keyboard.
Call Tom: 07700 900123`,o:["Tom would accept a different instrument instead of money.","The guitar has been played a lot.","The book has to be bought separately."],a:0,x:"‘I'll swap it for a keyboard’ = he would take a keyboard instead of £60. The guitar is ‘hardly used’ (B) and the book comes with it (C)."}]},2:{title:"Summer activities",intro:"descriptions of eight summer activities",who:"activities",stem:"Which activity would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"Green Hands",text:["Join our team repairing footpaths and building stone walls in the national park. No experience needed – our rangers will teach you how to use the tools safely. Sessions run every Saturday and Sunday from 9 am to 3 pm, and lunch is provided."]},{name:"Sketch the City",text:["A two-week course for 14–18-year-olds. Every morning the group goes out with a professional artist to draw streets, markets and buildings. On the last day, everyone's best pictures go on display in the town library for the public to see."]},{name:"Riverside Rowing Club",text:["Rowing is a great way to build strength. Beginners train as part of a crew of eight, and crews race against other clubs every month. Training is on Tuesday and Thursday evenings. Membership costs £40 a month."]},{name:"Night Runners",text:["A free running group for all levels. We meet outside the town hall at 7 pm on Mondays and Wednesdays and run 5 km together at a comfortable speed. Just bring your trainers – there's no need to book."]},{name:"Paws Animal Rescue",text:["We're looking for volunteers aged 16 and over to help look after the dogs and cats at our centre. You'll work alongside our vet nurses, and after 30 hours we'll give you a certificate describing the work you've done."]},{name:"City Farm Open Days",text:["Come and meet the goats, pigs and rabbits at our farm in the middle of town! Visitors can feed the animals and take a tractor ride. Open every day in August. Entry £3."]},{name:"World Kitchen",text:["Spend five afternoons cooking with teenagers from all over town. Each day a different chef shows the group how to make a meal from their home country – from Mexican tacos to Japanese noodles – and then everyone sits down to eat together."]},{name:"Art in the Park",text:["Bring your paints and join our outdoor painting weekend. Adults and teenagers are welcome, and tickets include all materials. There's no exhibition at the end – it's just for fun!"]}],qs:[{q:"Carlos wants to spend time outdoors and learn a practical skill. He can only take part at weekends because he works in his uncle's shop during the week.",a:0,x:"Green Hands: outdoors, rangers teach you to use tools (a practical skill), Saturdays and Sundays. Art in the Park is also at the weekend, but it's ‘just for fun’."},{q:"Aisha loves drawing and wants to improve. She'd like to work with other young people and have her work seen by the public at the end.",a:1,x:"Sketch the City: a professional artist, only 14–18-year-olds, and the pictures go on display. Art in the Park has adults too and no exhibition."},{q:"Ben wants to get fitter but doesn't enjoy team sports. He'd like something he can do in the evenings, and it mustn't cost much.",a:3,x:"Night Runners: free, in the evenings, and not a team sport. Rowing is in the evenings but you are part of a crew and it costs £40 a month."},{q:"Lena hopes to become a vet. She wants some real experience with animals that she can mention on her college application.",a:4,x:"Paws Animal Rescue: working with vet nurses and a certificate describing the work – useful for an application. At the City Farm you are only a visitor."},{q:"Ryan wants to make new friends before he starts at a new school. He enjoys cooking and would like to learn to make dishes from other countries.",a:6,x:"World Kitchen: teenagers from all over town, and chefs show dishes from their home countries."}]},3:{title:"The girl who fixes things",intro:"an article in which a teenager describes how she started a repair café",paras:["When my grandmother's old radio stopped working last year, my parents wanted to throw it away and buy a new one. I couldn't believe it. The radio had been in our kitchen for as long as I could remember, and I was sure it could be saved. So I took it to a small shop in town, where an elderly man called Mr Novak fixed it in twenty minutes – for just five pounds.","That got me thinking. How many other things end up in the bin just because people don't know how to repair them? I'd read about ‘repair cafés’ online – places where volunteers fix broken things for free while people have a coffee and watch. I decided our town needed one.","Finding a place was easier than I expected. The community centre agreed to let us use their hall on the first Saturday of every month without charging us anything. The hard part was finding people who could actually fix things. I put up posters and asked on social media, but at first only two people offered to help. Then Mr Novak said he would come, and he brought three friends from his old workplace.","Our first repair café was in March. I was worried nobody would turn up, but by ten o'clock there was a queue outside the door. People brought toasters, lamps, bikes and even a sewing machine. Not everything could be fixed, but about three quarters of the things went home working again. What surprised me most was how much people wanted to learn. Instead of just leaving their things with us, most of them sat down next to the volunteers and asked questions.","Since then, the café has grown a lot. We now have fifteen volunteers, including several people my age who are learning from the older ones. I've learnt to fix lamps and bike brakes myself, although I'm still not allowed near anything electrical without Mr Novak watching! My advice to anyone with a similar idea? Just start. You don't need to know everything – you just need to find the people who do."],qs:[{q:"Why did the writer take the radio to Mr Novak's shop?",o:["Her parents had asked her to get it repaired.","She didn't want it to be replaced.","She wanted to find out how much it was worth.","She had broken it herself."],a:1,x:"Her parents wanted to throw it away, but ‘I was sure it could be saved’."},{q:"Where did the idea of a repair café come from?",o:["Mr Novak suggested it.","She had visited one in another town.","She had read about them on the internet.","Her grandmother told her about one."],a:2,x:"‘I'd read about repair cafés online.’"},{q:"What was the most difficult part of setting up the café?",o:["finding somewhere to hold it","paying for the hall","getting people to bring things to repair","finding volunteers with the right skills"],a:3,x:"‘The hard part was finding people who could actually fix things.’ The hall was free and easy to find."},{q:"What surprised the writer at the first repair café?",o:["how many things could not be repaired","how interested visitors were in learning","how long people had to wait","how old some of the things were"],a:1,x:"‘What surprised me most was how much people wanted to learn.’"},{q:"What would the writer say to someone who wants to start a similar project?",o:["“Learn how to repair things yourself before you begin.”","“Make sure you have enough money first.”","“Don't wait until you're an expert – find people who can help.”","“Ask your local council for permission.”"],a:2,x:"‘Just start. You don't need to know everything – you just need to find the people who do.’"}]},4:{title:"Home is where the water is",intro:"an article about a family who live on a canal boat",paras:["Three years ago, Sam and Priya Clarke sold their flat in Birmingham and moved with their two children onto a twenty-metre canal boat. {1} But the family say it is the best decision they have ever made.","Space, of course, is the biggest challenge. The boat has a tiny kitchen, a living area and two small bedrooms, and everything has to have its place. {2} ‘We had to decide what really mattered to us,’ says Priya. ‘Now we own far fewer things, and honestly, we don't miss them.’","Life on the water also means doing jobs that most people never think about. The family have to fill up the water tank every few days and take their rubbish to special collection points along the canal. {3} Sam admits that this was hard at first, especially in winter.","The children, Leo, 12, and Anya, 9, go to a school near the marina where the boat stays during term time. {4} During the holidays, the family travel along the canals, and they have visited more than forty towns and villages so far.","Would they ever go back to living in a house? ‘Maybe one day,’ says Sam. {5} ‘For now, we can't imagine waking up anywhere else.’"],options:["Before they moved, they had to give away or sell most of their belongings.","Their friends thought they were crazy to give up a comfortable home.","In the evenings they do their homework at a little table next to the window.","They also have to keep a stove burning to heat the boat when it gets cold.","Then he looks out at the ducks on the water and smiles.","The boat was built more than fifty years ago in Scotland.","They had always dreamed of travelling abroad with the children.","It took them six months to find a buyer for the boat."],key:[1,0,3,2,4],x:["‘Crazy to give up a comfortable home’ explains the ‘But’ that follows: the family think it was the best decision.","‘Give away or sell most of their belongings’ leads to Priya's comment: ‘Now we own far fewer things.’","‘Also’ adds a second job to the ones before, and ‘when it gets cold’ links to ‘especially in winter’.","Homework in the evenings follows school, before the holidays in the next sentence.","‘Maybe one day,’ he says – ‘Then he looks out at the ducks… and smiles’ before saying he can't imagine living anywhere else."]},5:{title:"The world's oldest trees",text:`High in the White Mountains of California, there are trees that {1} growing for almost 5,000 years. These bristlecone pines {2} in a place where very few other plants can survive: the soil is poor, there is little rain and the winters are extremely cold.

Scientists believe that these difficult conditions are actually the {3} the trees live so long. Because they grow so slowly, their wood becomes very hard, and insects cannot easily {4} it. To protect the trees, the park does not {5} visitors exactly where the oldest one is. People can still walk among the pines, but they are asked to stay on the paths and not to {6} any wood away as a souvenir.`,gaps:[{o:["were","are","have been","had"],a:2,x:"Present perfect continuous with ‘for’: the trees started long ago and are still growing now."},{o:["raise","rise","grow","build"],a:2,x:"Plants grow somewhere. ‘Raise’ needs an object, and ‘rise’ means go up."},{o:["cause","purpose","result","reason"],a:3,x:"the reason (why) something happens: the reason the trees live so long."},{o:["hurt","damage","break","injure"],a:1,x:"You damage things like wood. ‘Hurt’ and ‘injure’ are used for people and animals."},{o:["say","speak","tell","explain"],a:2,x:"tell somebody something. ‘Say’ and ‘explain’ can't be followed directly by the person."},{o:["take","bring","carry","fetch"],a:0,x:"take something away = remove it from a place. ‘Bring’ means towards the speaker."}]},6:{title:"My cooking challenge",text:`Last month I decided to learn how to cook properly. Before that, the only thing I {0} make was toast! My plan was simple: I would cook one new dish every weekend.

The first week I tried to make pancakes, but I forgot {1} add any sugar and they tasted terrible. My brother laughed so much {2} he almost fell off his chair. Things got better after that. My aunt, {3} works as a chef, sent me some easy recipes, and I watched lots of videos online.

Now I can make pasta, soup and even a chocolate cake, and I'm really proud {4} myself. Next month I'm going to cook dinner for the whole family. I hope they enjoy it {5} much as I've enjoyed learning! If you've never cooked before, why not give it a try? It's much easier {6} you think.`,example:{a:"could"},gaps:[{a:["to"],x:"forget to do something = not remember to do it."},{a:["that"],x:"so + adjective/adverb + that: he laughed so much that he almost fell."},{a:["who"],x:"A relative pronoun for a person, between commas: my aunt, who works as a chef."},{a:["of"],x:"be proud of somebody/yourself."},{a:["as"],x:"as much as – a comparison of two equal things."},{a:["than"],x:"A comparative adjective is followed by ‘than’: easier than you think."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t2",title:"Test 2",topics:"Notices and messages · Weekend courses · A young inventor · Our school garden · The story of jeans · A trip to the mountains",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`LIBRARY
Books returned after the due date: 20p per day.
Return boxes are outside the main door.`,o:["You can leave books outside the library.","Late books are free to return on Mondays.","You must pay 20p to borrow each book."],a:0,x:"‘Return boxes are outside the main door’ – you can return books there. The 20p is only for late books (C), and nothing mentions Mondays (B)."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Hi Sam – football practice is at the park, not the sports centre, this Thursday because the pitch is being repaired. Same time. Coach Rob",q:"What is Coach Rob telling Sam?",o:["Practice will start later than usual.","Practice has moved to a different place.","Thursday's practice has been cancelled."],a:1,x:"‘At the park, not the sports centre … Same time.’ The place changes, not the time (A), and practice still happens (C)."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`To: Club members
From: Kasia
The photography trip on Saturday is still happening, even if it rains. Bring a waterproof bag for your camera – we'll have lunch in a café.`,o:["The trip will be cancelled if the weather is bad.","Members need to bring their own lunch.","Members should protect their cameras from the rain."],a:2,x:"‘Bring a waterproof bag for your camera.’ The trip happens even in rain (A), and lunch is in a café (B)."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`SUN CREAM
Put on 15 minutes before going outside.
Use again after swimming.
Not for children under 3.`,o:["Wait until you are outside before putting it on.","Put the cream on again after you swim.","The cream is only for young children."],a:1,x:"‘Use again after swimming.’ You put it on before going outside (B), and it's not for children under 3 (C)."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`BIKE WANTED
Looking for a second-hand mountain bike.
Must be in good condition – colour not important.
Up to £150. Contact Maya, Room 12.`,o:["Maya wants to sell her mountain bike.","Maya will pay no more than £150.","Maya only wants a bike in one colour."],a:1,x:"‘Up to £150’ is the most she'll pay. She's buying, not selling (A), and the colour isn't important (C)."}]},2:{title:"Weekend courses",intro:"descriptions of eight weekend courses",who:"courses",stem:"Which course would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"Spanish on the Road",text:["Going to Spain or Latin America? In six Saturday afternoons you'll learn the words and phrases you need for hotels, restaurants, trains and shops. Classes have no more than six students, so everyone gets plenty of time to speak."]},{name:"Spanish Grammar Intensive",text:["For learners who already know some Spanish and want to pass an exam. Two full weekends of grammar practice in a class of up to twenty students, with homework every evening."]},{name:"Clay Weekends",text:["Leave your laptop behind and make something with your hands! Our friendly teachers show you how to make bowls, cups and plates on the potter's wheel. No drawing or painting skills needed. Saturdays, 10 am – 4 pm."]},{name:"Good Food, Small Budget",text:["Learn to cook tasty, healthy meals for four people for under £10. Every Saturday morning we shop together at the market and then cook three simple dishes you can make again at home."]},{name:"Painting in Watercolour",text:["A relaxing Sunday course for anyone who loves art. Bring your own brushes and discover how to paint landscapes and flowers. Some painting experience is useful, as we move quickly."]},{name:"Better Photos on Your Phone",text:["You don't need an expensive camera to take great pictures. In three Sunday-morning sessions (9 am – 12 pm) you'll learn about light, angles and editing – all on the phone you already have."]},{name:"Camera Skills",text:["Take control of your camera! This Saturday course covers shutter speed, lenses and night photography. Please bring your own digital camera – phones are not suitable for this course."]},{name:"Pool Lifeguard Award",text:["Aged 16 or over and a strong swimmer? This eight-weekend course teaches rescue skills and first aid. Everyone who passes gets a certificate recognised by pools across the country – ideal for holiday work."]}],qs:[{q:"Olivia works in an office all week and wants to relax at the weekend. She'd like to make something creative with her hands, but she can't paint.",a:2,x:"Clay Weekends: making things with your hands, ‘no drawing or painting skills needed’. The watercolour course needs painting experience."},{q:"Jamal is travelling around Spain next summer. He wants to learn useful everyday phrases quickly and would prefer a small group.",a:0,x:"Spanish on the Road: phrases for hotels, restaurants and trains, no more than six students. The grammar course is for exams, with twenty students."},{q:"Hana wants to take better pictures. She only has a phone, and she is only free on Sunday mornings.",a:5,x:"Better Photos on Your Phone: Sunday mornings, on the phone you already have. Camera Skills is on Saturday and needs a digital camera."},{q:"Leo, 17, wants a qualification that will help him get a summer job at a swimming pool.",a:7,x:"Pool Lifeguard Award: for 16 and over, with a certificate recognised by pools – ‘ideal for holiday work’."},{q:"Mrs Dunn wants to learn to cook healthy meals for her family without spending much money.",a:3,x:"Good Food, Small Budget: healthy meals for four people for under £10."}]},3:{title:"A better bin",intro:"an article about a teenager who invented a new kind of bin",paras:["When fifteen-year-old Tomás Rivera noticed that most people in his block of flats threw everything into the same bin, he was annoyed. ‘There were recycling bins right next to it,’ he says. ‘But people were in a hurry, or they didn't know which bin was which.’ Instead of complaining, he decided to do something about it.","Tomás had always enjoyed taking things apart to see how they worked. His grandfather, an electrician, had given him an old toolbox when he was eight, and since then he had built a remote-control car and a lamp that switched itself on when it got dark. A bin, he thought, couldn't be much harder.","It was. His first design used a camera to recognise different kinds of rubbish, but it was far too slow, and it often mistook plastic bottles for glass. ‘I nearly gave up,’ he admits. ‘I spent three months on something that didn't work.’ Then his science teacher suggested using weight and simple sensors instead of a camera. The new version was cheaper, quicker and right almost every time.","Last spring, Tomás entered his bin in a national competition for young inventors. He didn't expect to win – the other finalists were mostly university students – but the judges were impressed by how simple and practical his idea was. He came second and won £2,000, which he has spent on better parts.","Now the building manager has asked him to put three of his bins in the flats. Tomás is excited but also realistic. ‘It still needs work,’ he says. ‘Sometimes it gets confused by wet paper. But the best thing is that my neighbours have started talking about recycling. Even if my bin isn't perfect, that's a start.’"],qs:[{q:"Why did Tomás decide to make a new kind of bin?",o:["The recycling bins in his building were too small.","People weren't using the recycling bins properly.","His neighbours asked him to help them recycle.","He wanted to enter a competition."],a:1,x:"People ‘threw everything into the same bin’ although recycling bins were right next to it."},{q:"What do we learn about Tomás in the second paragraph?",o:["He already had some experience of making things.","His grandfather trained him as an electrician.","He found it easy to design a bin.","He was given his tools as a prize."],a:0,x:"He had built a remote-control car and a lamp. The next paragraph shows the bin was not easy (C)."},{q:"What was the problem with Tomás's first design?",o:["It was too expensive to build.","It was too heavy to move.","It didn't recognise rubbish correctly.","His teacher didn't like it."],a:2,x:"‘It often mistook plastic bottles for glass’ – and it was slow."},{q:"How did Tomás feel about the competition?",o:["He was sure he would win.","He didn't think he had much chance.","He was disappointed to come second.","He thought the other ideas were too simple."],a:1,x:"‘He didn't expect to win – the other finalists were mostly university students.’"},{q:"What does Tomás say about his bin now?",o:["It works perfectly in every situation.","It is too expensive for his building.","It has made people more interested in recycling.","He is going to stop working on it."],a:2,x:"‘The best thing is that my neighbours have started talking about recycling.’ It still gets confused by wet paper (A)."}]},4:{title:"Our rooftop garden",intro:"an article by a student about a garden on a school roof",paras:["Two years ago, the flat roof of our school was an empty grey space that nobody used. Today it is covered in vegetables, flowers and even a small pond. {1} It all started with an idea from our geography teacher, Ms Okafor.","She wanted to show us how food is grown, but our school is in the middle of the city and there is no field nearby. {2} The head teacher agreed, as long as the roof was checked by an engineer first.","The first few months were hard work. We had to carry forty bags of soil up four floors, because the building doesn't have a lift. {3} By the end of the first term, though, we had planted tomatoes, beans and lettuce.","Now about thirty students look after the garden, taking turns to water the plants during lunch breaks. {4} In the summer holidays, when the school is closed, some parents come in to help.","Last month we held our first market in the school car park and sold our vegetables to parents and neighbours. {5} We've decided to spend the money on a greenhouse, so that we can grow food all year round."],options:["It has become one of the most popular places in the school.","Some students wanted to build a football pitch there.","So she suggested using the roof instead.","The engineer said the roof was too weak to use.","Everyone was exhausted after each trip.","There is a list on the classroom wall showing whose turn it is.","Vegetables from supermarkets often travel thousands of kilometres.","We made almost £300 in just two hours."],key:[0,2,4,5,7],x:["After describing the garden today, A says what it means for the school now, before the story of how it started.","There's no field nearby, so she suggested the roof – and ‘The head teacher agreed’.","Carrying soil up four floors → ‘Everyone was exhausted after each trip.’","Students take turns to water the plants → a list shows whose turn it is.","They sold vegetables → made almost £300 → ‘spend the money on a greenhouse’. D contradicts the rest of the story."]},5:{title:"The story of jeans",text:`Jeans are one of the most popular items of clothing in the world, but they were not {1} designed to be fashionable. In the 1870s, a tailor called Jacob Davis was asked to make strong trousers for a man who {2} wood for a living. He added metal pieces to the pockets so that they would not {3} easily.

Davis worked with a businessman, Levi Strauss, who {4} him with the cloth, and together they produced the first jeans. For many years, jeans were worn mainly by farmers and factory workers. It was only in the 1950s, when film stars began wearing them, that they became {5} with young people. Today, jeans are sold in almost every country, and they are {6} by people of all ages.`,gaps:[{o:["originally","firstly","early","previously"],a:0,x:"originally = at first, in the beginning."},{o:["broke","cut","made","hit"],a:1,x:"cut wood – the man was a woodcutter."},{o:["hurt","damage","tear","ruin"],a:2,x:"Cloth tears. ‘Damage’ and ‘ruin’ need an object; ‘hurt’ is for people."},{o:["gave","sold","offered","supplied"],a:3,x:"supply somebody with something – the only one that takes ‘with’."},{o:["famous","popular","liked","known"],a:1,x:"popular with somebody."},{o:["dressed","put on","worn","carried"],a:2,x:"You wear clothes → they are worn."}]},6:{title:"A trip to the mountains",text:`Hi Chris,

I've just got back {0} a week in the mountains with my family, and I had a brilliant time! We stayed in a small wooden house {1} was right next to a lake. Every morning we went walking, and one day we climbed all the {2} to the top of a mountain – it took us five hours!

The view was amazing, although I was {3} tired that I fell asleep on the way down in the cable car. My sister spent most of her time fishing, but she didn't catch {4} fish at all. We're planning to go again next year, and my parents say you can come with us {5} you want. It would be great {6} see you there!

Love, Sam`,example:{a:"from"},gaps:[{a:["which","that"],x:"A relative pronoun for a thing: a house which / that was next to a lake."},{a:["way"],x:"all the way to = the whole distance to."},{a:["so"],x:"so + adjective + that: so tired that I fell asleep."},{a:["any"],x:"not … any in negative sentences: didn't catch any fish."},{a:["if"],x:"if you want = in case you'd like to."},{a:["to"],x:"It would be great + to-infinitive."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t3",title:"Test 3",topics:"Notices and messages · Holiday camps · Raising a guide dog · A town without cars · Busy bees · My first job",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`LIBRARY
From Monday, you can borrow up to eight books at a time instead of five.
DVDs: still two per person.`,o:["The library will be closed on Monday.","You can now take out more books than before.","The number of DVDs you can borrow has changed."],a:1,x:"Eight books ‘instead of five’ – more than before. DVDs are ‘still two’, and nothing says the library is closed."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Sam, I left my maths book at your house! Can you drop it at mine on your way home? I need it for tonight's homework. If you can't, just bring it to school tomorrow. Leo",q:"What would Leo prefer Sam to do?",o:["take the book to Leo's house today","help him with his maths homework","bring the book to school tomorrow"],a:0,x:"He needs it ‘for tonight's homework’, so he asks Sam to drop it at his house. School tomorrow is only if Sam can't."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`To: All team members
From: Coach Davies
Saturday's match has moved from the school field to the sports centre because the field is too wet. It still starts at 10.30. Please arrive half an hour early to warm up.`,o:["The match has been cancelled because of the weather.","The match will now start at 10.00.","Players should be at the sports centre by 10.00."],a:2,x:"It ‘still starts at 10.30’ and players must arrive half an hour early – at 10.00. Only the place has changed."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`VITAMIN C TABLETS
Adults: one tablet a day with food.
Not suitable for children under 12.
Store in a cool, dry place.`,o:["The tablets must be kept in the fridge.","You should take a tablet when you eat a meal.","Children under 12 should take half a tablet."],a:1,x:"‘One tablet a day with food’ = with a meal. They are not for children under 12 at all, and a ‘cool, dry place’ is not the same as the fridge."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`BIKE REPAIRS
Students get 20% off every Wednesday – bring your student card.
Most repairs done while you wait!`,o:["You have to leave your bike at the shop for a day.","Students can get a cheaper repair on one day a week.","Only students can use this service."],a:1,x:"20% off ‘every Wednesday’ for students. Most repairs are done ‘while you wait’, and anyone can use the shop."}]},2:{title:"Holiday camps",intro:"descriptions of eight summer camps",who:"camps",stem:"Which camp would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"Wild Woods",text:["Spend a week sleeping in tents deep in the forest. Our guides teach you how to build a fire, find clean water and use a map and compass. For 12–16-year-olds. Please note: phones stay at home!"]},{name:"Code Club Summer",text:["Five days of building your own computer game with help from professional game designers. Every day runs from 9 am to 4 pm, so you sleep at home. Laptops are provided. Ages 13–17."]},{name:"Sea Explorers",text:["Sail, kayak and swim along the coast for a week. Campers stay in a youth hostel by the beach. You must be able to swim 200 metres before you join."]},{name:"Stage School",text:["Two weeks of acting, singing and dancing with teachers from the theatre world. On the last evening, everyone performs in a real theatre, and families are invited to watch."]},{name:"Farm Stay",text:["Families live together on a working farm for a week, feeding the animals and helping with the harvest. A great holiday for parents and children to share."]},{name:"Sports Mix",text:["A different sport every day – football, tennis, basketball and climbing. Beginners are very welcome, and our day camp costs just £10 a day, lunch included."]},{name:"Language Adventure",text:["Ten days in France with French lessons every morning and trips every afternoon. Students live with French families who have teenagers of their own."]},{name:"Film Makers",text:["Stay at our countryside centre for a week and make a short film using phones – writing, filming and editing. The best films will be shown on our website."]}],qs:[{q:"Maya is 15 and loves being outdoors. She wants to learn how to look after herself in the wild and doesn't mind being without her phone for a week.",a:0,x:"Wild Woods: tents in the forest, fire, water and map skills, for 12–16-year-olds, and no phones. Sea Explorers is outdoors too, but it's about sailing."},{q:"Tom wants to improve his French before his exams next year. He'd also like to get to know young people from another country.",a:6,x:"Language Adventure: French lessons, and he lives with French families who have teenagers."},{q:"Lucy dreams of being a performer, and she'd like her parents to see what she has learnt at the end of the course.",a:3,x:"Stage School: acting, singing and dancing, and families are invited to the final show. Film Makers' films go online, not on stage."},{q:"Josh is 14 and loves computers. He wants to make something of his own, but his parents want him to come home every night.",a:1,x:"Code Club Summer: build your own game, and ‘you sleep at home’. Film Makers is residential – you stay at the centre."},{q:"Ella hasn't done much sport before, but she'd like to try several different ones. She can't afford an expensive camp.",a:5,x:"Sports Mix: a different sport every day, beginners welcome and only £10 a day."}]},3:{title:"Walking with Hope",intro:"an article in which a teenager describes looking after a young guide dog",paras:["When I was fifteen, our family agreed to become ‘puppy walkers’ for a guide dog charity. That meant looking after a young Labrador called Hope for her first year, before she went away to be trained as a guide dog for a blind person. My mum had wanted to do it for years, but my dad was worried about the carpets. In the end it was my little brother who persuaded him, by promising to clean up after her every day. (He didn't, of course.)","Hope arrived when she was eight weeks old, and she was much smaller than I had expected. The charity gave us a long list of rules. She wasn't allowed on the sofa, she could never eat food from the table, and we had to take her everywhere with us – on buses, into shops, even to the dentist – so that she would get used to noise and crowds. At first I found this embarrassing, because people kept stopping us to ask questions. After a while, though, I started to enjoy explaining what we were doing.","The hardest part was teaching her to stay calm. Hope loved everyone, and whenever she saw another dog, she wanted to play. A trainer from the charity visited us every month, and she showed me how to use my voice to control Hope instead of pulling on the lead. It took weeks, but slowly Hope learnt to walk quietly beside me, even past the park.","Everyone had warned us that saying goodbye would be difficult, and they were right. On the day the charity came to collect her, I tried to be cheerful, but as soon as the van drove away I burst into tears. What helped was a letter we received six months later. Hope had passed all her tests and was now working with a woman called Margaret, who had lost her sight in her forties. Margaret wrote that, for the first time in years, she could go into town on her own.","We've looked after two more puppies since then, and it doesn't get much easier. But whenever I think about Margaret walking down the high street by herself, I know it's worth it. If your family has the time and the space, I'd really recommend it – just don't expect your carpets to stay clean."],qs:[{q:"Who finally persuaded the writer's father to agree?",o:["the writer","the writer's mother","the writer's younger brother","someone from the charity"],a:2,x:"‘In the end it was my little brother who persuaded him.’ Her mum had wanted it, but she didn't manage to persuade him."},{q:"How did the writer feel at first about taking Hope everywhere?",o:["annoyed that the rule seemed unnecessary","proud to be seen with such a special dog","uncomfortable when strangers asked her questions","worried that Hope would get lost in crowds"],a:2,x:"‘At first I found this embarrassing, because people kept stopping us to ask questions.’"},{q:"What did the trainer teach the writer?",o:["how to walk Hope through the park","how to stop Hope eating from the table","how to control Hope without pulling on the lead","how to let Hope play safely with other dogs"],a:2,x:"‘She showed me how to use my voice to control Hope instead of pulling on the lead.’"},{q:"What made saying goodbye easier for the writer?",o:["meeting Margaret in town","hearing how Hope was helping someone","getting a new puppy straight away","a visit from the charity's trainer"],a:1,x:"‘What helped was a letter’ – Hope was working with Margaret, who could now go into town on her own."},{q:"What is the writer's opinion of being a puppy walker?",o:["It gets much easier the second time.","It is only suitable for families with young children.","It is too expensive for most families.","It is hard, but it is worth doing."],a:3,x:"‘It doesn't get much easier. But… I know it's worth it.’ She recommends it to families with time and space."}]},4:{title:"The town that switched off its cars",intro:"an article about a town that closes its centre to traffic every Sunday",paras:["Every Sunday morning in the small town of Kellby, something unusual happens: the cars disappear. From eight o'clock until six in the evening, the town centre is closed to traffic, and the streets fill with bikes, skateboards and people on foot. {1}","The idea came from a group of parents who were worried about the busy road outside the primary school. {2} To everyone's surprise, the council agreed to try it for three months, and it has now been running for four years.","Not everybody was happy at first. Some shop owners believed they would lose customers if people couldn't park outside. {3} Many shops now say that Sunday is their busiest day of the week.","The quiet streets have also changed the way people spend their free time. Families bring picnics, older residents sit and chat on benches in the middle of the road, and there is a market in the main square. {4}","Other towns have started to take notice. Last year, visitors from more than twenty councils came to Kellby to see how it works. {5} ‘The streets belong to people,’ says one of the parents who started the campaign. ‘We just reminded everyone.’"],options:["In fact, the opposite happened.","They asked if the road could be closed for just one day a week.","Some of them have already decided to try the same thing.","Local musicians often play there in the afternoon.","For many people who live there, it has become the favourite day of the week.","The school has since moved to a new building outside the town.","Parking in the town centre is free on Saturdays.","Experts believe cars will disappear from all towns within ten years."],key:[4,1,0,3,2],x:["‘It has become the favourite day of the week’ sums up the scene of streets full of bikes and people.","‘They asked if the road could be closed’ explains what the parents wanted – and then ‘the council agreed to try it’.","‘In fact, the opposite happened’ answers the shop owners' fear, and ‘Sunday is their busiest day’ shows it.","‘Local musicians often play there’ – ‘there’ is the main square with the market.","‘Some of them’ = the visitors from other councils, who have decided to try the same thing."]},5:{title:"Busy bees",text:`Honeybees are among the hardest-working insects on the planet. A single bee may {1} as many as 5,000 flowers in one day while it collects food for its colony. And bees do not {2} this alone: when a bee discovers a good place to find flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a special ‘dance’ that tells the other bees which {3} to fly in.

Without bees, many of the fruits and vegetables we eat would be much harder to {4}, because plants need bees to carry pollen from one flower to another. Sadly, bee numbers have fallen in many countries in recent years. Scientists think this is {5} to a mix of reasons, including disease and the loss of wild flowers. The good news is that everyone can help. Even planting a few flowers on a balcony can {6} a real difference.`,gaps:[{o:["arrive","travel","go","visit"],a:3,x:"visit a place (here, flowers). ‘Travel’, ‘go’ and ‘arrive’ would need a preposition."},{o:["make","have","do","take"],a:2,x:"do something alone. ‘Make’ is for creating or producing something."},{o:["road","path","side","direction"],a:3,x:"fly in a direction. Bees don't follow a road or a path."},{o:["rise","grow","raise","develop"],a:1,x:"Farmers grow fruit and vegetables. ‘Raise’ is for animals or children; ‘rise’ means go up."},{o:["thanks","because","due","result"],a:2,x:"due to = because of. ‘Because’ would need ‘of’, and ‘thanks to’ is for something good."},{o:["put","give","make","do"],a:2,x:"make a difference – a fixed phrase."}]},6:{title:"My first job",text:`Last summer I got my first job {0} a waiter in a café near the beach. I was really nervous on my first day because I had {1} had a job before. The owner, Mrs Lane, showed me {2} to carry three plates at once, but I still dropped a whole tray of cups! Luckily, she just laughed and said it happened to everyone.

The work was hard. I had to stand for eight hours a day, and by the evening my feet were {3} tired that I could hardly walk home. But I met lots of interesting people, and I earned enough money {4} buy a new laptop.

The best thing about the job was that I learnt to talk to people I didn't know. I used to be quite shy, but now I find it much easier. I'm going to work there again next summer, {5} Mrs Lane asks me back. I really hope she {6}!`,example:{a:"as"},gaps:[{a:["never"],x:"had never + past participle = not at any time before then."},{a:["how"],x:"show somebody how to do something."},{a:["so"],x:"so + adjective + that: so tired that I could hardly walk."},{a:["to"],x:"enough + noun + to + infinitive: enough money to buy."},{a:["if"],x:"A condition: I'll work there if she asks me back."},{a:["does"],x:"The auxiliary replaces the verb: I hope she does (= asks me back)."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t4",title:"Test 4",topics:"Notices and messages · Books for teenagers · A young chess champion · The smallest school · The story of chocolate · A music festival",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`SWIMMING POOL
Children under 8 must be with an adult in the water at all times.`,o:["Adults can't swim at the same time as children.","An adult must be in the water with young children.","Children under 8 cannot use the pool."],a:1,x:"Young children must be ‘with an adult in the water’. They can use the pool (A), and nothing stops adults swimming (C)."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Mum, the train's late – there's a problem on the line. Don't wait for me for dinner. I'll get a sandwich at the station and should be home by 9. Ana",q:"What is Ana telling her mother?",o:["She has missed her train.","She wants her mother to collect her from the station.","She will eat before she gets home."],a:2,x:"‘Don't wait for me for dinner. I'll get a sandwich at the station.’ The train is late – she hasn't missed it – and she doesn't ask to be collected."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`To: Film club
From: Kate
Next week's film is two and a half hours long, so we'll start at 6.30 instead of 7. Snacks are welcome, but please no hot food in the hall.`,o:["Members can bring any food they like.","The film club will meet earlier than usual next week.","Next week's meeting will be shorter than usual."],a:1,x:"6.30 ‘instead of 7’ – earlier. Hot food is not allowed (B), and the film is long, so the meeting won't be shorter (C)."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`WOOL JUMPER
Wash by hand in cold water.
Do not put in the dryer.
Dry flat.`,o:["You can wash this jumper in a washing machine on a cool setting.","You should hang the jumper up to dry.","The jumper must not be dried in a machine."],a:2,x:"‘Do not put in the dryer’ = don't dry it in a machine. It must be washed by hand (A) and dried flat, not hung up (C)."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`ROOM TO RENT
Large bedroom in a shared flat, 10 minutes from the university.
Bills included. Non-smokers only.
Available from 1 September.`,o:["The rent does not include electricity and water.","People who smoke cannot rent the room.","Only students can rent the room."],a:1,x:"‘Non-smokers only’. Bills are included (A), and it's near the university but not only for students (B)."}]},2:{title:"Books for teenagers",intro:"descriptions of eight books",who:"books",stem:"Which book would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"The Silent Island",text:["A mystery full of surprises. When Isla spends the summer with her grandfather on a Scottish island, she discovers that he has been hiding a secret for fifty years. At 400 pages, it's a book for long holidays."]},{name:"Road to Tokyo",text:["The true story of a 17-year-old who cycled from London to Tokyo, told by the cyclist himself. Full of photos and maps, it ends with a chapter of advice on planning your own long journey."]},{name:"Kitchen Science",text:["Thirty experiments you can do at home using everyday things like vinegar, balloons and paper. Clear pictures show every step."]},{name:"Starfall",text:["An exciting science fiction adventure set on a spaceship in the year 3000. It's the first in a series of five books about the same crew."]},{name:"Laugh Out Loud",text:["A collection of thirty funny short stories by young writers. Each one is only a few pages long – perfect for a short journey – and guaranteed to put you in a good mood."]},{name:"The Last Game",text:["Sam wants to be a professional footballer, but a serious injury changes everything. A moving novel about football, friendship and how Sam's relationship with his brother grows stronger."]},{name:"Great Lives",text:["Short, lively biographies of fifty scientists, artists and athletes who changed the world, and what helped each of them succeed. Every chapter stands on its own, so you can start anywhere."]},{name:"Ghost Town",text:["A frightening story about an empty village where strange things happen at night. Not recommended for younger readers."]}],qs:[{q:"Karim loves true adventure stories and is planning a long trip himself next year. He'd like some practical ideas to help him.",a:1,x:"Road to Tokyo: a true story, with maps and a chapter of advice on planning your own long journey."},{q:"Sophie spends twenty minutes on the bus to school every day and wants something to read that will cheer her up.",a:4,x:"Laugh Out Loud: funny, only a few pages each – perfect for a short journey – and it puts you in a good mood."},{q:"Oliver enjoys stories set in the future, and when he likes a book, he wants to read more about the same characters.",a:3,x:"Starfall: science fiction in the year 3000, the first of five books about the same crew."},{q:"Nadia is interested in famous people and how they became successful. She likes books she can dip in and out of.",a:6,x:"Great Lives: fifty biographies, what helped each person succeed, and every chapter stands on its own."},{q:"Leo plays in his school football team. He likes stories about sport, but he also wants a story about family and friends.",a:5,x:"The Last Game: football, friendship and Sam's relationship with his brother."}]},3:{title:"Checkmate at fourteen",intro:"an article in which a young chess player describes how she became a champion",paras:["I learnt to play chess when I was six, from an old book I found at my grandmother's house. Nobody else in my family played, so for the first year I played against myself, moving round the table to sit on each side. My parents thought it was a bit strange, but when my teacher saw me playing in the school library, she told them about a chess club in town.","The club met on Thursday evenings in a room above a café. I was the youngest member by about thirty years, and at first the other players didn't take me very seriously. Then I beat the club champion, a retired bus driver called Frank. He wasn't angry at all – he was delighted, and from then on he met me every week to help me study the game.","People imagine that chess is all about being clever, but I think it's more about staying calm. In my first big competition, I lost three games in a row because I was so nervous that I couldn't think clearly. Frank taught me some breathing exercises, and now I always take a minute before each game to relax.","Last year I won the national under-14 championship, which meant I could represent my country abroad. I travelled to Greece with my dad and played against children from forty countries. I didn't win, but I finished in the top ten, and I made friends with a girl from Brazil who I still play online every weekend.","My school friends sometimes ask me why I spend so much time on a board game. I tell them it's the same as any other sport: you practise, you lose, you learn and you get better. And it's much more exciting than it looks!"],qs:[{q:"How did the writer first learn to play chess?",o:["She joined a chess club.","Her teacher showed her at school.","Her grandmother taught her.","She learnt from a book."],a:3,x:"‘I learnt to play chess… from an old book I found at my grandmother's house.’ Nobody in her family played."},{q:"How did Frank react when the writer beat him?",o:["He was upset about losing to a child.","He said she had been lucky.","He suggested she joined a different club.","He was pleased and offered to help her."],a:3,x:"‘He wasn't angry at all – he was delighted… he met me every week to help me study the game.’"},{q:"According to the writer, what is most important in chess?",o:["studying famous games","playing as often as possible","not letting yourself get nervous","being very intelligent"],a:2,x:"‘I think it's more about staying calm.’ She lost when she was nervous."},{q:"What happened at the competition in Greece?",o:["She played against her father.","She finished near the top and made a new friend.","She won the championship.","She lost every game because she was nervous."],a:1,x:"‘I finished in the top ten, and I made friends with a girl from Brazil.’"},{q:"What does the writer tell her school friends?",o:["Chess is like any other sport.","Chess is easier than it looks.","They should join her chess club.","Chess is only for clever people."],a:0,x:"‘It's the same as any other sport: you practise, you lose, you learn and you get better.’"}]},4:{title:"Six pupils and the sea",intro:"an article about a very small school on a Scottish island",paras:["On the island of Carrick, off the west coast of Scotland, the school bell rings every morning at nine o'clock for just six pupils. They are aged between five and eleven, and they all learn together in one classroom with one teacher, Mrs Fraser. {1}","With so few pupils, lessons are very different from those in a big school. The older children often help the younger ones with their reading and maths. {2} ‘Everyone has to be patient,’ says Mrs Fraser. ‘But they learn so much from each other.’","The island itself is also an important part of their education. On sunny days, lessons often move outside. The children study the birds on the cliffs, collect shells on the beach and measure the height of the waves. {3}","Of course, there are some disadvantages. There is no swimming pool or sports hall, and it can be hard to find enough players for a football team. {4} Once a month, the pupils take the ferry to the mainland to join a larger school for sport and music.","When they are twelve, the children have to leave the island to go to secondary school, and they live with families on the mainland during the week. {5} But many say that the island school has given them something special: confidence, independence and a love of nature."],options:["This helps the older pupils to remember what they have learnt, too.","Last year, they even helped scientists to count the seals that live in the bay.","For some of them, it is the first time they have lived away from home.","However, the school has found a way to solve this problem.","There are only about eighty people living on the whole island.","Most teachers in Scotland work in large city schools.","Most of the pupils want to become teachers when they grow up.","The ferry to the island only runs in the summer months."],key:[4,0,1,3,2],x:["Six pupils, one teacher – and ‘only about eighty people’ on the whole island explains why the school is so small.","Older children help younger ones, and ‘this helps the older pupils… too’ shows why it works for both.","Birds, shells, waves – and ‘even helped scientists to count the seals’ adds one more outdoor lesson.","‘However, the school has found a way to solve this problem’ – the monthly trip to the mainland is the solution.","Living with families on the mainland during the week is ‘the first time they have lived away from home’. (The ferry runs once a month all year, so the last option is wrong.)"]},5:{title:"The story of chocolate",text:`Chocolate has a long history. More than 3,000 years ago, people in Central America were already {1} drinks from the beans of the cacao tree. These drinks were bitter, not sweet, and were often {2} with chilli. The Aztecs valued cacao beans so {3} that they even used them as money.

When chocolate arrived in Europe in the 1500s, people began to add sugar, and it quickly became {4} among rich families. However, it was not until the 1800s that a Dutch scientist found a way to {5} chocolate into a powder, and soon after that the first chocolate bars appeared. Today, the world {6} around seven million tonnes of chocolate every year.`,gaps:[{o:["doing","cooking","building","making"],a:3,x:"make a drink. We ‘cook’ food, and ‘do’ is not used for producing things."},{o:["put","mixed","added","joined"],a:1,x:"mixed with something. ‘Added’ would need ‘to’ (chilli was added to them)."},{o:["many","lot","much","more"],a:2,x:"so much that – with a verb (valued). ‘Many’ goes with plural nouns."},{o:["pleasant","delicious","favourite","popular"],a:3,x:"become popular among a group of people. ‘Favourite’ would need ‘a’, and ‘pleasant’ and ‘delicious’ describe the taste, not how many people like it."},{o:["develop","become","grow","turn"],a:3,x:"turn something into something else. ‘Become’ has no object."},{o:["swallows","bites","tastes","eats"],a:0,x:"The world eats chocolate – how much chocolate people eat in total."}]},6:{title:"Mud, music and friends",text:`Last August, my best friend and I went to our first music festival. We had been looking forward {0} it since January. We took the train {1} Friday morning and arrived just in time to put up our tent before the first band started.

The music was amazing, but the weather was terrible. It rained {2} much that the whole field turned into mud, and my new trainers were completely ruined. We didn't care, though – everyone was singing and dancing in {3} of the rain.

The best moment was on Saturday night, when our favourite band played. We managed to get right to the front, {4} we could see everything. By Sunday we were so tired that we fell asleep as {5} as we got on the train home. I'd definitely go again next year, but this time I'm going to take boots {6} of trainers!`,example:{a:"to"},gaps:[{a:["on"],x:"on + day: on Friday morning."},{a:["so"],x:"so much that – the result of the rain."},{a:["spite"],x:"in spite of = despite."},{a:["where","so"],x:"where = at the front (or ‘so’ = with the result that)."},{a:["soon"],x:"as soon as = immediately when."},{a:["instead"],x:"instead of = in place of."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t5",title:"Test 5",topics:"Notices and messages · Museums for a day out · Sailing around Britain · Volunteering with animals · Why we sleep · Learning Spanish",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`CAFÉ
Sorry – our card machine isn't working today.
Cash only. There's a cash machine next to the bank opposite.`,o:["You can only pay by card today.","The café is closed until the machine is repaired.","You can get money from a machine across the road."],a:2,x:"‘There's a cash machine next to the bank opposite’ = across the road. It's cash only (A), and the café is open (C)."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Hi Grandma! Thanks for the birthday money. I've used it to buy the headphones I wanted – they're great! See you on Sunday. Love, Max",q:"Why has Max sent this message?",o:["to invite his grandmother to his birthday party","to thank his grandmother for a present","to ask his grandmother for some money"],a:1,x:"‘Thanks for the birthday money.’ He has already spent it, and Sunday is a visit, not a party."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`To: Parents of Year 8
From: Ms Patel
The school photographer is coming on Tuesday. Students should wear full school uniform. Brothers and sisters can have a photo together at lunchtime – please tell me by Friday if you'd like one.`,o:["Parents should contact Ms Patel if they want a family photo.","Parents must pay for photos by Friday.","Students can wear their own clothes on Tuesday."],a:0,x:"Brothers and sisters can have a photo together – ‘please tell me by Friday’. Uniform must be worn (A), and payment isn't mentioned (B)."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`TOMATO SOUP
Ready in 3 minutes.
Heat in a pan, stirring all the time.
Not suitable for the microwave.`,o:["The soup takes a long time to prepare.","You can heat the soup in a microwave.","The soup needs to be stirred while it heats."],a:2,x:"‘Stirring all the time’. It's ‘not suitable for the microwave’ (A) and it's ready in 3 minutes (C)."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`DOG WALKER WANTED
For our friendly dog, Bruno, on weekday afternoons.
Must be over 16 and love animals.
£8 an hour. Call Emma.`,o:["Emma will pay £8 for each walk, however long it is.","Young teenagers cannot apply for the job.","The job is at the weekend."],a:1,x:"‘Must be over 16’ – younger teenagers can't apply. It's on weekday afternoons (A) and paid £8 an hour, not per walk (B)."}]},2:{title:"A day at the museum",intro:"descriptions of eight museums",who:"museums",stem:"Which museum would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"The Science Hall",text:["Over 200 hands-on experiments, from building bridges to making lightning. Every Saturday, scientists give free talks for teenagers about their work. Entry £12."]},{name:"The Toy Museum",text:["Toys from the last 200 years, from wooden trains to the first video games. Younger children love the play area on the top floor."]},{name:"Riverside Gallery",text:["Paintings by local artists, with a new exhibition every month. On Sunday mornings, visitors can join a free drawing class with one of the artists. Entry is free."]},{name:"The Transport Museum",text:["Old buses, trams and trains – and you can climb inside most of them. The museum café is inside a 1950s railway carriage."]},{name:"Castle House",text:["Visit the rooms where a rich family lived 300 years ago. Guides dressed as servants tell you what life was like – and our garden is famous for its roses. Entry £15."]},{name:"The Football Museum",text:["Everything about the history of football, with shirts, balls and trophies. Test your skills in the interactive area, where you can take penalties against a virtual goalkeeper."]},{name:"The Space Centre",text:["See a real rocket and walk through a model of the International Space Station. The planetarium show takes you on a journey to the stars. Open until 9 pm on Fridays."]},{name:"The Food Museum",text:["Discover what people ate in the past and how our food is produced today. At weekends, visitors can take part in a cooking class – booking is essential."]}],qs:[{q:"Hannah loves art and would like to improve her own drawing. She doesn't have much money to spend.",a:2,x:"Riverside Gallery: a free drawing class with an artist, and entry is free."},{q:"Jack is interested in how things work and would like to hear experts talk about their jobs. He can only go at weekends.",a:0,x:"The Science Hall: hands-on experiments and free talks by scientists every Saturday."},{q:"Mr and Mrs Carter want to take their grandchildren, aged 4 and 6, somewhere with a place where the children can play.",a:1,x:"The Toy Museum: younger children love the play area on the top floor."},{q:"Priya enjoys history and wants to find out how people lived in the past. She also loves gardens.",a:4,x:"Castle House: rooms from 300 years ago, guides explaining life then, and a famous rose garden."},{q:"Dan works during the day, so he'd like to visit in the evening. He is fascinated by the planets.",a:6,x:"The Space Centre: open until 9 pm on Fridays, with a planetarium show about the stars."}]},3:{title:"Around Britain in a small boat",intro:"an article in which a young woman describes sailing around Britain",paras:["When I told my friends I was going to sail around Britain on my own, most of them laughed. I was nineteen, and my boat, the Kittiwake, was only seven metres long. But I had been sailing since I was eight, and I had spent two years planning the trip. What I didn't have was enough money, so I worked in a supermarket every weekend for a year to pay for the equipment.","I left from Plymouth in May, heading west. The first week was the hardest. The wind was stronger than the forecast had said, and I was sick for three days. I seriously thought about giving up, but my mum, who was following my progress on her computer, sent me a message every evening. Her words kept me going.","People often ask if I felt lonely. Actually, I was hardly ever alone for long. In every harbour I stopped at, someone would come and talk to me, and several families invited me to their homes for a meal. In one small Scottish village, a fisherman even fixed a broken part of my boat and refused to take any money.","The most beautiful part of the trip was the north coast of Scotland. I sailed past cliffs full of birds, and one morning a group of dolphins swam beside the boat for almost an hour. I tried to film them, but my camera battery was flat, so I just sat and watched. I think I enjoyed it more that way.","I arrived back in Plymouth after eleven weeks. The friends who had laughed were waiting on the harbour wall with a banner. The trip taught me that most people are kind, and that you can do far more than you think. Next year, I'd like to try crossing the Atlantic – though maybe not on my own."],qs:[{q:"How did the writer pay for her trip?",o:["Her parents gave her the money.","She sold her old boat.","She did a part-time job.","A sailing club helped her."],a:2,x:"‘I worked in a supermarket every weekend for a year to pay for the equipment.’"},{q:"What helped the writer during the first difficult week?",o:["stopping in a harbour","messages from her mother","the weather forecast","medicine for seasickness"],a:1,x:"‘My mum… sent me a message every evening. Her words kept me going.’"},{q:"What does the writer say about the people she met?",o:["They were surprised that she was alone.","They often asked her for help.","Few of them spoke to her.","Many of them were very generous."],a:3,x:"Families invited her for meals, and a fisherman fixed her boat and ‘refused to take any money’."},{q:"Why didn't the writer film the dolphins?",o:["The dolphins swam away too quickly.","She decided it was better just to watch.","Her camera wasn't working.","She was too busy sailing the boat."],a:2,x:"‘My camera battery was flat.’ She enjoyed watching, but that wasn't the reason she didn't film."},{q:"What does the writer say she learnt from the trip?",o:["People are capable of more than they believe.","Sailing alone is too dangerous.","Her friends did not really believe in her.","She should have taken a friend with her."],a:0,x:"‘You can do far more than you think.’"}]},4:{title:"A day at the animal hospital",intro:"an article about a student who volunteers at a wildlife hospital",paras:["Every Saturday, seventeen-year-old Ruby Evans gets up at six o'clock and cycles to a small wildlife hospital just outside her town. The hospital looks after injured wild animals – mostly birds, hedgehogs and foxes – and returns them to nature when they are well again. {1}","Ruby started volunteering there two years ago, after she found a baby owl that had fallen from a tree. {2} ‘They were so kind and calm,’ she says. ‘I knew straight away that I wanted to help.’","Her day usually begins with cleaning out the cages and preparing food. It isn't always pleasant work. {3} ‘You get used to it,’ Ruby laughs. ‘And when you see an animal getting stronger every day, you forget about the smell.’","The best moment, she says, is when an animal is ready to go back to the wild. Last spring, she helped to release a family of hedgehogs in a quiet corner of a nature reserve. {4}","Ruby now hopes to study to become a vet. {5} ‘I've learnt so much here,’ she says. ‘Not just about animals, but about being patient and working in a team.’"],options:["She took it to the hospital, where the staff looked after it until it could fly.","Some of the foxes, for example, have to be fed dead mice.","They ran off into the long grass and disappeared within seconds.","Last year it treated more than two thousand animals.","Her experience at the hospital should help her to get a place at university.","Unfortunately, the owl died a few days later.","She has always been afraid of foxes.","The nature reserve is closed to the public in winter."],key:[3,0,1,2,4],x:["‘Last year it treated more than two thousand animals’ – ‘it’ is the hospital, and the number adds to what the hospital does.","She found a baby owl, then ‘took it to the hospital’, and ‘they were so kind’ refers to the staff.","‘It isn't always pleasant work’ – feeding foxes dead mice is an example, and then comes the smell.","Releasing the hedgehogs – ‘they ran off into the long grass’.","She wants to become a vet, and her experience will help her get a university place."]},5:{title:"Why do we sleep?",text:`Most people spend about a third of their lives asleep, but scientists still do not fully {1} why we need so much sleep. What they do know is that sleep is {2} for both our bodies and our brains.

While we sleep, the brain sorts through the information it has collected during the day and decides what to {3}. This is why students who get a good night's sleep before an exam often remember more than those who stay up late studying. Sleep also gives the body time to {4} itself, and people who sleep badly are more likely to catch colds.

Teenagers need more sleep than adults – around nine hours a night – but many {5} much less, often because they look at their phones in bed. Experts {6} people to switch off all screens at least an hour before going to sleep.`,gaps:[{o:["understand","notice","recognise","realise"],a:0,x:"understand why = know the reason. ‘Realise’, ‘notice’ and ‘recognise’ are about becoming aware of something."},{o:["serious","strict","urgent","essential"],a:3,x:"essential for = absolutely necessary for."},{o:["stay","keep","last","hold"],a:1,x:"keep information = not forget it. ‘Stay’ has no object."},{o:["service","correct","repair","mend"],a:2,x:"The body repairs itself. We mend clothes, correct mistakes and service cars."},{o:["do","get","pass","make"],a:1,x:"get (an amount of) sleep – the usual collocation."},{o:["suggest","insist","advise","say"],a:2,x:"advise somebody to do something. ‘Suggest’, ‘insist’ and ‘say’ can't be followed by a person + to."}]},6:{title:"¡Hola! My Spanish year",text:`At the beginning of this year I decided {0} learn Spanish. My family goes to Spain every summer, and I was tired of pointing at things in shops because I couldn't say what I wanted.

I started with an app, {1} I soon realised that I needed to practise speaking, too. So I joined an evening class at the college. There are twelve of us in the group, and our teacher, Carlos, makes every lesson fun. {2} week he brings Spanish snacks for us to try.

The most useful thing I've done, though, is find a language partner. Lucía is a student from Madrid {3} is learning English. We meet online twice a week: we speak Spanish for half an hour and then English for half an hour. We've become good friends, and she has invited me to visit her next summer.

I still make lots of mistakes, but I'm not {4} worried about them as I used to be. My advice to anyone learning a language is to start speaking as soon as you {5}. Don't wait until you're perfect – you'll be waiting {6} ever!`,example:{a:"to"},gaps:[{a:["but"],x:"Contrast: I started with an app, but I needed more."},{a:["every","each"],x:"every week = each week."},{a:["who","that"],x:"A relative pronoun for a person: a student who is learning English."},{a:["as"],x:"not as + adjective + as: not as worried as I used to be."},{a:["can"],x:"as soon as you can = as early as possible."},{a:["for"],x:"for ever = always, with no end."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t6",title:"Test 6",topics:"Notices and messages · After-school clubs · Life as a zookeeper · A library in a garage · The story of the bicycle · A new country",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`PLEASE DO NOT FEED THE DUCKS BREAD
It makes them ill.
Duck food is on sale at the café.`,o:["The café sells bread for the ducks.","Ducks are not allowed near the café.","You can buy special food for the ducks."],a:2,x:"‘Duck food is on sale at the café.’ Bread makes the ducks ill, so the café doesn't sell bread for them (B)."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Dad – I'm staying at Lily's to finish our project. Her mum says she'll drive me home at about ten. Is that OK? Zoe",q:"What does Zoe want to know?",o:["if her dad can collect her at ten","if she can come home later than usual","if she can spend the night at Lily's house"],a:1,x:"She's asking if it's OK to come home at about ten – later than usual. Lily's mum will drive her, and she isn't staying the night."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`To: All staff
From: Reception
The lift will be out of order all day on Thursday. Visitors who can't use the stairs can meet staff in the meeting room on the ground floor.`,o:["The stairs will be closed for repairs.","Visitors can't come to the building on Thursday.","Some meetings may have to take place downstairs."],a:2,x:"Visitors who can't use the stairs can meet staff in the ground-floor room – downstairs. It's the lift, not the stairs, that isn't working."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`SUN CREAM SPF 50
Put on 15 minutes before going out in the sun.
Use again after swimming.`,o:["You should put the cream on before you go outside.","The cream is not suitable for swimming.","You only need to use this once a day."],a:0,x:"‘Put on 15 minutes before going out.’ You use it again after swimming, so A and C are wrong."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`LOST: black and white cat called Pepper.
Last seen near Hill Park on Monday.
Reward for any information! Call 07700 900456`,o:["Pepper has been missing for a long time.","Pepper's owner will pay for news of the cat.","The cat was found in Hill Park."],a:1,x:"‘Reward for any information’ = the owner will pay. Pepper was last seen near the park, not found, and only since Monday."}]},2:{title:"After-school clubs",intro:"descriptions of eight after-school clubs",who:"clubs",stem:"Which club would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"Chess Club",text:["A quiet club for players of all levels, every Monday. The best players take part in tournaments against other schools."]},{name:"Eco Team",text:["Plant trees around the school grounds, look after the vegetable garden and run the school's recycling. We meet outdoors every Tuesday – come and make a real difference!"]},{name:"Robotics",text:["Design and build robots and enter the national schools competition. We meet on Wednesdays after school and on Saturday mornings. You'll need to be strong at maths and science."]},{name:"Drama Club",text:["We put on a play every term. Not everyone wants to act – we also need people to look after the lights, the sound and the costumes behind the stage. Thursdays."]},{name:"Homework Club",text:["A quiet room where teachers are ready to help with any subject. Open every school day from 3.30 until 5 pm."]},{name:"Dance Crew",text:["Learn street dance and perform at school events and parties. No experience needed. Fridays after school."]},{name:"The School News",text:["Write articles and take photos for our online school newspaper, which comes out every month. Members interview teachers, visitors and local people with interesting stories."]},{name:"Cooking Club",text:["Learn to cook quick, healthy meals to take home to your family. Tuesdays. There's a small charge each week for the ingredients."]}],qs:[{q:"Freya wants to be a journalist when she leaves school. She enjoys writing and meeting new people.",a:6,x:"The School News: writing articles and interviewing people with interesting stories."},{q:"Omar is shy and doesn't want to be on stage, but he'd like to help with a school show.",a:3,x:"Drama Club: people are needed for lights, sound and costumes ‘behind the stage’."},{q:"Isabel cares about the environment and wants to do something practical outdoors.",a:1,x:"Eco Team: planting trees, the vegetable garden and recycling, and they meet outdoors."},{q:"Kieran is brilliant at maths and science and loves competitions. He's free at weekends.",a:2,x:"Robotics: strong maths and science, a national competition, and Saturday mornings."},{q:"Mia finds some of her subjects difficult and would like extra help on most days of the week.",a:4,x:"Homework Club: teachers help with any subject, every school day."}]},3:{title:"Looking after giraffes",intro:"an article in which a young zookeeper describes his job",paras:["People often think my job must be like a holiday – spending all day with animals in the sunshine. The reality is rather different. My day starts at 7.30, and the first two hours are spent cleaning. Giraffes produce an enormous amount of mess, and it all has to be cleared away before the visitors arrive at ten.","I've wanted to work with animals since I was a child, but getting a job at a zoo isn't easy. After school I studied animal care at college, and I spent every holiday volunteering at a farm park. Even so, I applied for fourteen jobs before I was offered one. What made the difference, my manager told me later, was that I had so much practical experience.","Each giraffe has its own personality. Mara, the oldest female, is calm and gentle, but her son Kito is curious and sometimes naughty – he once took a visitor's hat and refused to give it back! Getting to know them as individuals is the best part of the job, because it means I notice immediately if one of them is unwell.","Part of my work is teaching visitors about giraffes in the wild, where their numbers have fallen by around forty per cent in thirty years. I give a short talk at feeding time every afternoon. At first I was terrified of speaking in front of crowds, but now I really enjoy it, especially when children ask questions.","The job isn't well paid, and I work most weekends and holidays. But I can't imagine doing anything else. Last month Mara had a baby, and I was there to watch it take its first steps. Moments like that make all the early mornings worth it."],qs:[{q:"What does the writer say about the start of his working day?",o:["He meets visitors at the entrance.","He spends it relaxing in the sun.","He has to do a lot of cleaning.","He prepares food for the animals."],a:2,x:"‘The first two hours are spent cleaning.’"},{q:"Why was the writer finally offered a job?",o:["because he had applied to the same zoo many times","because he knew the manager","because he had done a lot of practical work with animals","because he had a college qualification"],a:2,x:"‘What made the difference… was that I had so much practical experience.’"},{q:"What does the writer say about the giraffes' personalities?",o:["Kito is calmer than his mother.","Knowing each one well helps him to care for them.","They all behave in a very similar way.","Mara doesn't like visitors."],a:1,x:"Knowing them as individuals means ‘I notice immediately if one of them is unwell’."},{q:"How does the writer feel about giving talks now?",o:["He still feels very nervous.","He enjoys it.","He prefers talking to adults.","He doesn't think it should be part of his job."],a:1,x:"‘At first I was terrified… but now I really enjoy it.’"},{q:"What is the writer's attitude to his job?",o:["He would like a better-paid job.","He is tired of working at weekends.","He would prefer to work with different animals.","He loves it, even though it has disadvantages."],a:3,x:"It isn't well paid and he works weekends, ‘but I can't imagine doing anything else’."}]},4:{title:"The garage library",intro:"an article about a girl who opened a library in her family's garage",paras:["When twelve-year-old Leah Moss moved to a new town, the first thing she looked for was the library. She was disappointed to discover that the nearest one had closed two years earlier. {1}","So Leah decided to start her own. She cleared out her parents' garage, borrowed some shelves from her uncle and put up a sign: ‘Free books – take one, leave one.’ {2}","At first only a few neighbours came. But then a local newspaper wrote about the garage library, and within a week Leah had received more than three hundred books from people all over the town. {3}","Leah now opens the library every Saturday and Sunday afternoon. As well as lending books, she runs a reading club for younger children, who sit on cushions on the floor while she reads stories aloud. {4}","Last month, the town council contacted Leah's family. They were so impressed that they have promised to find the money to reopen a proper library in the town centre. {5} ‘I just wanted somewhere to read,’ Leah says. ‘I never expected all this!’"],options:["Her own books were the first ones on the shelves.","There were so many that her dad had to build extra shelves.","The next closest one was forty minutes away by bus.","Some of the parents stay and read too.","They also want Leah to help them choose the books.","Her parents had never read many books themselves.","The garage was later turned into a bedroom.","Newspapers are no longer delivered to her street."],key:[2,0,1,3,4],x:["The nearest library had closed, and ‘the next closest one was forty minutes away’ explains the problem.","She set up the garage, and ‘her own books were the first ones on the shelves’.","More than three hundred books – ‘so many that her dad had to build extra shelves’.","Children sit and listen, and ‘some of the parents stay and read too’.","The council will reopen a library, and ‘they also want Leah to help them choose the books’."]},5:{title:"Two wheels good",text:`The first bicycles appeared in Germany in 1817. They had no pedals, so riders had to {1} themselves along with their feet. Later designs added pedals to the front wheel, which was made very large so that the bike could {2} faster. These ‘penny-farthings’ were difficult to ride, and falls were very {3}.

The modern bicycle, with two wheels of the same size and a chain, was {4} in the 1880s. It was safer and more comfortable, and it soon became hugely popular. For the first time, ordinary people could travel long distances without {5} for a horse or a train ticket. Today there are more than a billion bicycles in the world, and many cities are building special lanes to {6} more people to cycle.`,gaps:[{o:["press","throw","push","carry"],a:2,x:"push yourself along = move forward by pushing with your feet."},{o:["run","go","walk","drive"],a:1,x:"A bike goes fast. We don't say a bike runs, walks or drives."},{o:["general","normal","common","usual"],a:2,x:"common = happening often. ‘Usual’ and ‘normal’ mean what is expected, and ‘general’ means including most things."},{o:["found","explored","discovered","invented"],a:3,x:"invent a machine that didn't exist before. We discover or find things that already exist."},{o:["costing","spending","buying","paying"],a:3,x:"pay for something. ‘Spend’ would need money as its object."},{o:["succeed","encourage","let","make"],a:1,x:"encourage somebody to do something. ‘Make’ and ‘let’ are followed by a verb without ‘to’."}]},6:{title:"A new start",text:`When I was thirteen, my family moved from Poland to Ireland because my dad got a job in Dublin. I didn't want to leave my friends, and I was worried {0} going to a new school where I didn't know anyone.

My first day was difficult. I hardly understood {1} the teachers said because they spoke so fast. But a girl called Aoife asked me to sit with her at lunch, and she introduced me {2} her friends. We've been best friends ever {3} then.

Little by little, my English improved. I joined the school football team, {4} helped me to make more friends. Now, three years later, I feel at home here. I still miss Poland sometimes, and we go back every summer to visit my grandparents.

If you ever have to move to a new country, don't worry too much. It's hard at the beginning, but it gets easier {5} time goes on. And who knows – you might find a best friend on your {6} day!`,example:{a:"about"},gaps:[{a:["what","anything"],x:"understand what somebody says (or ‘hardly understood anything’)."},{a:["to"],x:"introduce somebody to somebody."},{a:["since"],x:"ever since then = from that time until now."},{a:["which"],x:"A relative pronoun referring to a whole idea (joining the team)."},{a:["as"],x:"as time goes on = while time passes."},{a:["first"],x:"on your first day."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t7",title:"Test 7",topics:"Notices and messages · Films to watch · A young wildlife photographer · The man who planted a forest · Plastic and the ocean · Grandad's garden",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`CITY PARK
Open from 7 am until sunset.
No cycling on the grass.
Dogs must be kept on a lead near the playground.`,o:["You can ride your bike anywhere in the park.","Dogs are not allowed in the park.","The time the park closes changes during the year."],a:2,x:"It closes at sunset, and sunset is at a different time in summer and winter. Bikes can't go on the grass, and dogs are allowed on a lead."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Hi Tom, sorry – Saturday's match has sold out! But my uncle has two spare tickets for Sunday's game. Do you want to go instead? Let me know by tonight. Ben",q:"What does Ben want Tom to do?",o:["buy tickets for Saturday's match","tell him if he wants to go on Sunday","ask Ben's uncle for tickets"],a:1,x:"‘Do you want to go instead? Let me know by tonight.’ Saturday has sold out, and Ben's uncle already has the tickets."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`Dear customer,
Your order has been sent and should arrive within three working days. You don't need to be at home – our driver can leave it with a neighbour.`,o:["The customer must collect the order.","The order may be left with someone who lives nearby.","The order will definitely arrive tomorrow."],a:1,x:"‘Our driver can leave it with a neighbour.’ It arrives within three working days, and nobody has to collect it."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`CHICKEN SANDWICH
Best before: 12 May
Keep in the fridge.
Contains: wheat, eggs, milk.`,o:["The sandwich should be eaten after 12 May.","The sandwich can be kept anywhere.","People who can't eat eggs shouldn't choose this sandwich."],a:2,x:"It ‘contains… eggs’. It's best before 12 May, not after, and it must be kept in the fridge."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`GUITAR LESSONS
First lesson free!
All ages and levels.
Lessons at my house or yours – you choose.
Call Maria: 07700 900789`,o:["Lessons are only for beginners.","Maria can come to your home to teach.","You have to pay for your first lesson."],a:1,x:"‘Lessons at my house or yours’. The first lesson is free, and all levels are welcome."}]},2:{title:"Films to watch",intro:"descriptions of eight films",who:"films",stem:"Which film would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"The Long Walk",text:["The true story of a family who escape a war by walking across the mountains. Serious and very emotional, and nearly three hours long."]},{name:"Robot Friends",text:["A colourful cartoon comedy about two little robots who get lost in a big city. A perfect choice for families with young children."]},{name:"Deep Blue",text:["This documentary about life in the oceans has amazing underwater photography, and a famous scientist explains everything you see along the way."]},{name:"The Heist",text:["An action thriller full of fast car chases and dangerous plans. Not suitable for anyone under 15."]},{name:"Laugh Track",text:["A light and funny romantic comedy set in a TV studio, with a great cast. The perfect way to relax."]},{name:"Voices",text:["A musical about a school choir that enters a national competition – with more than twenty songs, most of them written for the film."]},{name:"Mars Base",text:["Science fiction about the first people to live on Mars. Scientists helped to make sure everything in the film could really happen."]},{name:"Old Masters",text:["A documentary about famous painters from the past and the techniques they used to create their greatest works."]}],qs:[{q:"Lena loves nature and wants to learn something while she watches.",a:2,x:"Deep Blue: ocean life, and a scientist explains everything."},{q:"The Brown family want a film that their children, aged 5 and 7, will enjoy.",a:1,x:"Robot Friends: a cartoon comedy that is perfect for families with young children."},{q:"Sam sings in a band and enjoys films with lots of music.",a:5,x:"Voices: a musical with more than twenty songs."},{q:"Aisha is interested in space, and she likes stories that could really happen.",a:6,x:"Mars Base: space, and scientists made sure everything could really happen."},{q:"Marco wants something fun and relaxing after a hard week, but not a cartoon.",a:4,x:"Laugh Track: light, funny and ‘the perfect way to relax’. Robot Friends is a cartoon."}]},3:{title:"Behind the lens",intro:"an article in which a teenager describes becoming a wildlife photographer",paras:["My grandad gave me his old camera when I was eleven. It was heavy and complicated, and at first I only took photos of our cat. Then one morning I noticed a robin sitting on the garden fence. I spent twenty minutes getting closer, one step at a time, and finally took a picture I was really proud of. That was the moment I knew what I wanted to do.","Wildlife photography is mostly about waiting. I often sit in a small tent, called a hide, for four or five hours without seeing anything. My friends think that sounds boring, but I love it. You notice things – the sound of the wind, the way the light changes. And when an animal finally appears, the excitement is incredible.","Last year I entered a national competition for young photographers with a photo of a fox in the snow. I'd taken it early on a winter morning in the field behind our house. I didn't expect to get anywhere, so when the organisers phoned to say I'd won my age group, I thought it was a joke. The prize was a week at a nature reserve with a professional photographer.","That week taught me more than two years of practising alone. The photographer, Anna, showed me how to use light better and how to tell a story with a single picture. But the most important thing she said was that a good wildlife photographer always puts the animal first. If getting a photo means frightening or disturbing an animal, you should walk away.","I'm now saving up for a new lens, and I sell some of my photos as cards at the local market. One day I'd like to photograph wildlife all over the world. But I've learnt that you don't need to travel far to find something amazing – sometimes it's sitting on your garden fence."],qs:[{q:"What made the writer decide to become a wildlife photographer?",o:["a present he got for his birthday","taking photos of his cat","taking a good photo of a bird","his grandfather's advice"],a:0,x:"After photographing the robin: ‘That was the moment I knew what I wanted to do.’"},{q:"What does the writer say about waiting in a hide?",o:["His friends often wait with him.","He enjoys noticing small things around him.","He finds it boring.","He never waits for more than an hour."],a:1,x:"‘I love it. You notice things – the sound of the wind, the way the light changes.’"},{q:"How did the writer feel when he heard he had won?",o:["He was sure he deserved it.","He couldn't believe it.","He was disappointed with the prize.","He wished he had sent a different photo."],a:1,x:"‘I didn't expect to get anywhere… I thought it was a joke.’"},{q:"What was the most important thing Anna taught him?",o:["how to tell a story in one photo","how to use light","how to sell his photos","not to disturb animals to get a picture"],a:3,x:"‘The most important thing she said was that a good wildlife photographer always puts the animal first.’"},{q:"What does the writer say in the last paragraph?",o:["He doesn't earn any money from his photos.","He has already travelled around the world.","You can find amazing wildlife close to home.","He is going to stop photographing local wildlife."],a:2,x:"‘You don't need to travel far to find something amazing.’ He sells cards, so D is wrong."}]},4:{title:"George's Wood",intro:"an article about a man who planted a forest",paras:["Forty years ago, the hill above the village of Aldby was bare. The trees had been cut down long before, and nothing grew there except grass. Then a retired teacher called George Hill decided to change that. {1}","At first, people in the village thought he was wasting his time. Young trees need years to grow, and George was already sixty-five. {2} ‘I'm not doing it for me,’ he told them. ‘I'm doing it for the children.’","Every morning, whatever the weather, George walked up the hill with a bag of young trees and a spade. He planted oak, birch and cherry trees – about ten every day. {3}","Slowly, the hill began to change. Birds that had not been seen in the area for years came back, and deer began to appear in the evenings. {4}","George died last year at the age of ninety-eight, but his forest lives on. More than fifty thousand trees now cover the hill, and the village has named it ‘George's Wood’. {5} Every spring, the children plant one new tree for each baby born in the village that year."],options:["Some of them told him he would never see the results.","He planted his first tree on his sixty-fifth birthday.","When some were eaten by rabbits, he simply planted more.","Families now come from miles away to walk there at weekends.","The local school looks after it now.","He had always hated living in the countryside.","Cutting down trees is now against the law in many countries.","His children moved to Australia many years ago."],key:[1,0,2,3,4],x:["He decided to change the bare hill, and ‘he planted his first tree on his sixty-fifth birthday’ – which matches ‘already sixty-five’ later.","People thought he was wasting his time: ‘some of them told him he would never see the results’ – and he answers them.","About ten trees a day, and ‘when some were eaten by rabbits, he simply planted more’.","Birds and deer come back, and people follow: ‘families now come from miles away’.","‘The local school looks after it now’ leads to ‘every spring, the children plant one new tree’."]},5:{title:"Plastic and the ocean",text:`Every year, millions of tonnes of plastic {1} up in the world's oceans. Some of it is carried there by rivers, and some is {2} away by people on beaches. Because plastic takes hundreds of years to break {3}, it can harm sea animals for a very long time. Turtles, for example, often mistake plastic bags for jellyfish and try to eat them.

There are many ways we can {4} the amount of plastic we use. Many countries have already stopped shops from giving away free plastic bags, and some supermarkets now let customers bring their own containers. Individuals can help too, by {5} a reusable water bottle and saying no to plastic straws. Small changes like these, if enough people make them, can have a big {6}.`,gaps:[{o:["stop","close","finish","end"],a:3,x:"end up somewhere = arrive there in the end, often without planning to."},{o:["put","dropped","thrown","fallen"],a:2,x:"throw something away = get rid of it. ‘Put away’ means tidy something."},{o:["off","along","over","down"],a:3,x:"break down = slowly separate into very small parts."},{o:["decline","reduce","fall","drop"],a:1,x:"reduce something = make it smaller. ‘Fall’ and ‘decline’ have no object."},{o:["using","making","spending","wasting"],a:0,x:"using a reusable bottle instead of buying new ones."},{o:["result","change","affect","effect"],a:3,x:"have an effect. ‘Affect’ is a verb, and we don't ‘have a big result’."}]},6:{title:"Grandad's garden",text:`My grandad has a small garden behind his house, and it is {0} favourite place in the world. Every summer since I {1} five, I've spent a week there helping him.

Grandad knows the name of every plant, and he's taught me how to grow tomatoes, beans and strawberries. He says the secret is patience: you can't hurry nature. {2} year we grew a pumpkin that was so big we couldn't lift it on {3} own! We had to use a wheelbarrow to take it into the kitchen.

What I love most is that nothing is wasted. Old vegetables go into a compost bin, {4} they slowly turn into soil for next year. Grandad even collects rainwater in a barrel so he doesn't {5} to use the tap.

This year I've started my own small vegetable patch at home. It isn't as good {6} Grandad's yet, but I'm learning!`,example:{a:"my"},gaps:[{a:["was"],x:"since + past simple: since I was five."},{a:["last","one"],x:"Last year / One year – a time in the past."},{a:["our"],x:"on our own = without help."},{a:["where"],x:"where = in the compost bin."},{a:["have","need"],x:"doesn't have to / doesn't need to = it isn't necessary."},{a:["as"],x:"not as good as – a comparison."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t8",title:"Test 8",topics:"Notices and messages · Places to eat · Climbing Kilimanjaro · A day with a firefighter · The first mobile phones · A surprise party",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`MUSEUM SHOP
Bags must be left in the lockers.
Lockers take £1 coins – you get your money back when you return the key.`,o:["The museum shop sells bags.","You can take your bag into the shop.","Using a locker costs nothing in the end."],a:2,x:"You put in £1, but ‘you get your money back’, so it costs nothing in the end. Bags must go in the lockers (B)."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Anna – I can't meet you at 4 as we planned, my piano lesson has moved. Can we meet at 5.30 in the same place? Sorry! Kate",q:"Why has Kate written to Anna?",o:["to suggest meeting later","to cancel their meeting completely","to change the place where they're meeting"],a:0,x:"‘Can we meet at 5.30 in the same place?’ – later, same place, and still meeting."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`To: Club members
We're looking for someone to design posters for our summer concert. You don't need to be an expert – just creative! Reply to this email if you're interested.`,o:["Members must have experience of design.","Any creative member can offer to help.","The club is paying an artist to design the posters."],a:1,x:"‘You don't need to be an expert – just creative!’ Nothing is said about payment."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`WALL PAINT
Apply two coats.
Wait four hours between coats.
Keep away from children.`,o:["Children can use this paint safely.","You should paint the wall twice, with a break in between.","The paint dries in two hours."],a:1,x:"Two coats, with four hours between them. Children should be kept away from it (C)."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`SWAP SHOP
Bring clothes you no longer wear and take something new home – no money needed!
Saturday 10–2, Church Hall`,o:["The event lasts all weekend.","You can buy second-hand clothes cheaply.","You can exchange your old clothes for different ones."],a:2,x:"Bring clothes and take something home – ‘no money needed’. It's on Saturday from 10 to 2 only."}]},2:{title:"Places to eat",intro:"descriptions of eight places to eat",who:"places",stem:"Which place would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"Casa Rosa",text:["A busy Italian family restaurant with big tables, a children's menu and a play corner. Open for lunch and dinner every day."]},{name:"The Green Leaf",text:["A peaceful vegetarian café where everything is made with vegetables from local farms. Free Wi-Fi, and nobody minds if you stay all afternoon."]},{name:"Sushi Go",text:["Fresh Japanese food to eat in or take away. Open until midnight."]},{name:"The Old Mill",text:["An elegant restaurant beside the river, with beautiful views from every table. Dinner only. It isn't cheap, but it's perfect for a special occasion – book early."]},{name:"Burger Barn",text:["Big burgers at low prices, loud music and a great atmosphere. Popular with students, who get 10% off with a student card."]},{name:"Café Nova",text:["Delicious coffee and home-made cakes, and live jazz on Friday evenings."]},{name:"Spice Route",text:["Excellent Indian food. On Sunday mornings the chef runs cooking classes where you learn to make three dishes."]},{name:"The Picnic Box",text:["We prepare picnic baskets full of sandwiches, salads and cakes for you to take to the park. Order online the day before and collect in the morning."]}],qs:[{q:"Paul and Jane want to celebrate their wedding anniversary somewhere special with a view.",a:3,x:"The Old Mill: river views and ‘perfect for a special occasion’."},{q:"Ravi is a student without much money. He likes lively places.",a:4,x:"Burger Barn: low prices, a student discount and a great atmosphere with loud music."},{q:"Clara is vegetarian and needs a quiet place where she can work on her laptop.",a:1,x:"The Green Leaf: vegetarian, peaceful, free Wi-Fi and you can stay all afternoon."},{q:"Tom wants to learn how to cook food from another country.",a:6,x:"Spice Route: cooking classes on Sunday mornings."},{q:"The Silvas want to eat outdoors with friends on Sunday, but they don't want to cook.",a:7,x:"The Picnic Box: ready-made picnic baskets to take to the park."}]},3:{title:"To the roof of Africa",intro:"an article in which a teenager describes climbing Mount Kilimanjaro",paras:["When my aunt suggested that we climb Mount Kilimanjaro together, I laughed. At 5,895 metres, it's the highest mountain in Africa, and I got out of breath walking up the stairs at school. But she was serious. She wanted to raise money for the hospital that had treated my cousin, and she needed a climbing partner. Before I knew it, I had agreed.","We had nine months to prepare. Every weekend we walked in the hills near our town, carrying heavy backpacks, and I started running three times a week. The fitness wasn't the hardest part, though. What worried me most was the altitude – the higher you go, the less oxygen there is, and nobody can tell in advance how their body will react.","The climb took seven days. Our guide, Joseph, set a very slow pace – ‘pole pole’, he kept saying, which means ‘slowly, slowly’ in Swahili. I felt impatient at first, because we could have walked much faster. But he explained that going slowly gives the body time to get used to the altitude, and I soon understood why it mattered.","On the last night we started walking at midnight so that we could reach the top at sunrise. It was freezing, my head ached, and every step was an effort. Twice I wanted to turn back. My aunt didn't say much – she just walked beside me and held my arm when I slipped. When the sun came up and we saw the snow on the summit, I cried.","Together we raised over £6,000 for the hospital. People ask me if I'd do it again. Honestly? Not yet – my legs still remember it! But it taught me that I'm much stronger than I thought, and that the best way to do something difficult is one small step at a time."],qs:[{q:"Why did the writer agree to climb Kilimanjaro?",o:["Her school had organised the trip.","She wanted to get fitter.","Her aunt needed someone to go with her.","She had always wanted to climb a high mountain."],a:2,x:"Her aunt ‘needed a climbing partner’. The writer had never planned it – she laughed at first."},{q:"What worried the writer most before the climb?",o:["carrying a heavy backpack","getting fit enough","being away from home","how the altitude would affect her"],a:3,x:"‘What worried me most was the altitude.’ Fitness ‘wasn't the hardest part’."},{q:"How did the writer feel about Joseph's slow pace at first?",o:["too tired to notice","grateful","impatient","frightened"],a:2,x:"‘I felt impatient at first, because we could have walked much faster.’"},{q:"How did the writer's aunt help her on the last night?",o:["She persuaded her to turn back.","She told her stories to keep her going.","She carried her backpack for her.","She stayed close and supported her."],a:3,x:"‘She just walked beside me and held my arm when I slipped.’"},{q:"What does the writer say she learnt?",o:["that she wants to climb another mountain soon","that difficult things are possible if you take them step by step","that she should have trained harder","that raising money is easier than she thought"],a:1,x:"‘The best way to do something difficult is one small step at a time.’ She doesn't want to do it again yet (C)."}]},4:{title:"A day with a firefighter",intro:"an article about the work of a firefighter",paras:["Sarah Jones has been a firefighter for twelve years. Most people imagine that her job is all about running into burning buildings. {1} ‘Real fires are only a small part of what we do,’ she says.","A typical shift starts at eight in the morning with a check of all the equipment. Every hose, ladder and breathing mask has to be tested. {2} ‘If something doesn't work when we need it, people could die,’ Sarah explains.","After that, the team trains together. They practise rescuing people from cars, climbing ladders while carrying heavy equipment and working in rooms full of smoke. {3}","In the afternoon, Sarah often visits local schools to teach children about fire safety. {4} ‘The children love trying on our helmets,’ she laughs. ‘And they always ask if we've ever rescued a cat from a tree.’","When the alarm goes off, everything changes. The team has just ninety seconds to put on their uniforms and get into the fire engine. {5} ‘You never know what you'll find,’ says Sarah. ‘That's what makes the job exciting.’"],options:["She shows them how to check smoke alarms at home and what to do if there is a fire.","Most fire stations have a kitchen where the team cooks meals.","This can take up to two hours.","Within minutes, they could be on their way to a car accident, a flood or a fire.","In fact, she spends much more time preventing fires than fighting them.","Cats are the animals that firefighters rescue most often.","It is hard work, but it means they are ready for anything.","Fire engines were first used more than two hundred years ago."],key:[4,2,6,0,3],x:["‘In fact’ corrects what most people imagine, and ‘real fires are only a small part’ agrees with it.","Every piece of equipment is tested – ‘this can take up to two hours’.","After all that training: ‘it is hard work, but it means they are ready for anything’.","Teaching fire safety: ‘she shows them how to check smoke alarms’ – and ‘them’ is the children.","Ninety seconds to get ready, then ‘within minutes, they could be on their way’ – and ‘you never know what you'll find’."]},5:{title:"The first mobile phones",text:`The first mobile phone call was {1} in 1973 by an engineer in New York. The phone he used weighed more than a kilogram, and its battery {2} for only about twenty minutes. It took another ten years before mobile phones went on {3}, and even then they were very expensive – around $4,000 each.

In the 1990s, phones became smaller and cheaper, and people began to send text messages. At first, a message could {4} no more than 160 characters, so people invented short forms like ‘CU L8R’. Today's smartphones do far more than make calls: we use them to take photos, find our way and {5} in touch with friends all over the world. It is hard to believe how quickly they have {6} our lives.`,gaps:[{o:["made","done","had","given"],a:0,x:"make a phone call."},{o:["lasted","continued","kept","stayed"],a:0,x:"A battery lasts for a length of time."},{o:["shop","market","sale","buy"],a:2,x:"go on sale = become available to buy. ‘Market’ would need ‘the’."},{o:["consist","involve","belong","contain"],a:3,x:"contain = have inside. ‘Consist’ would need ‘of’."},{o:["have","keep","take","hold"],a:1,x:"keep in touch with somebody."},{o:["developed","turned","changed","moved"],a:2,x:"change somebody's life. ‘Turn’ would need ‘around’."}]},6:{title:"Surprise!",text:`Last month it was my mum's fiftieth birthday, and my dad, my sister and I decided to organise a surprise party for {0}. The hardest part was keeping it a secret. We sent invitations to all her friends {1} asked them not to say anything.

On the day of the party, my aunt took Mum shopping {2} that we could get the house ready. We put up balloons, made a huge cake and hid in the living room with the lights off. When we heard her key in the door, everyone went completely silent.

As soon {3} Mum walked in, we switched on the lights and shouted ‘Surprise!’ She was so shocked that she dropped all her shopping bags! Then she started laughing – and crying at the same time. She said it was the best birthday she had {4} had.

We danced until midnight, and there was so {5} food left that we were still eating cake a week later. Now Mum says she wants to organise a surprise party for Dad. I don't think it will be a surprise, {6}!`,example:{a:"her"},gaps:[{a:["and"],x:"and joins two actions: sent invitations and asked them."},{a:["so"],x:"so that = in order that."},{a:["as"],x:"as soon as = immediately when."},{a:["ever"],x:"the best… she had ever had – superlative + ever."},{a:["much"],x:"so much + uncountable noun (food)."},{a:["though","however"],x:"though at the end of a sentence = however."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t9",title:"Test 9",topics:"Notices and messages · Sports clubs · Growing up on a farm · A robot in the classroom · The Great Wall · A lost dog",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`TRAIN STATION
The 8.15 to Leeds will leave from Platform 4 today, not Platform 2.
We apologise for any inconvenience.`,o:["There is no train to Leeds today.","Passengers for Leeds need to go to a different platform.","The Leeds train will leave later than usual."],a:1,x:"It leaves ‘from Platform 4 today, not Platform 2’. The time is still 8.15."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Hi Jo! I've got a spare ticket for the theatre on Friday – my sister can't come. It starts at 7.30. Want to join me? I'll pay for dinner if you pay for the taxi! Mel",q:"What is Mel offering Jo?",o:["a free place at the theatre","a lift to the theatre in her sister's car","a ticket that Jo has to pay for"],a:0,x:"She has a spare ticket because her sister can't come – and asks Jo to join her. Jo only pays for the taxi."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`Hi everyone,
Thanks for coming to the beach clean-up on Sunday! We filled forty bags with rubbish. Next month we're cleaning the river – more details soon.
Maya`,o:["Maya is asking for help with Sunday's clean-up.","Maya is complaining about the rubbish on the beach.","Maya is thanking people and telling them about a future event."],a:2,x:"‘Thanks for coming’ – the clean-up has happened – and ‘next month we're cleaning the river’."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`HAIR DRYER
Do not use near water.
Switch off and unplug after use.
The dryer gets hot – let it cool before putting it away.`,o:["You can use the dryer in the bath.","You should wait before you put the dryer away.","The dryer switches off by itself."],a:1,x:"‘Let it cool before putting it away.’ It must not be used near water, and you have to switch it off yourself."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`BABYSITTER AVAILABLE
Experienced 18-year-old student, first-aid trained.
Evenings and weekends. Happy to help with homework!
Call Sophie.`,o:["Sophie is still at school.","Sophie can only work during the day.","Sophie can help children with schoolwork."],a:2,x:"‘Happy to help with homework!’ She works evenings and weekends, and she's a student, not at school."}]},2:{title:"Sports clubs",intro:"descriptions of eight sports clubs",who:"clubs",stem:"Which club would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"City Swimmers",text:["Training for competitive swimmers aged 12–18, five mornings a week before school. You must already swim at club level."]},{name:"Climb Up",text:["An indoor climbing wall with instructors on hand every evening. Beginners are welcome, and all equipment is included in the price."]},{name:"Park Tennis",text:["Free tennis in the park every Saturday morning. Rackets and balls are provided – just turn up."]},{name:"Riverside Canoe Club",text:["Paddle along the river and learn to handle a canoe safely. Sessions on summer evenings. You must be able to swim."]},{name:"Mixed Hockey",text:["A friendly team for men and women of all ages. We play matches against other clubs every Sunday and go out for a meal afterwards."]},{name:"Yoga for All",text:["Gentle yoga classes to improve flexibility and reduce stress. Perfect if you are recovering from an injury."]},{name:"Street Football",text:["Five-a-side football for 14–17-year-olds on Friday nights. Our coaches have played professionally."]},{name:"Mountain Bikers",text:["Weekend rides through the forest and hills, from easy to extreme. Bring your own bike and helmet."]}],qs:[{q:"Hugo has never tried climbing, but he'd love to. He doesn't have any equipment, and he's free after work.",a:1,x:"Climb Up: beginners welcome, equipment included and instructors every evening."},{q:"Lisa hurt her back last year. Her doctor has told her to do some gentle exercise to help it get stronger.",a:5,x:"Yoga for All: gentle classes, perfect if you are recovering from an injury."},{q:"Anton has just moved to the area. He wants to play a team sport and make new friends of different ages.",a:4,x:"Mixed Hockey: all ages, and a meal together after matches."},{q:"Rosa is 16 and wants to get better at football with help from experienced coaches.",a:6,x:"Street Football: 14–17-year-olds, and coaches who have played professionally."},{q:"Jamie can't afford to pay for a club and doesn't have any sports equipment of his own.",a:2,x:"Park Tennis: free, and rackets and balls are provided."}]},3:{title:"A childhood on the farm",intro:"an article in which a young woman describes growing up on a farm",paras:["I grew up on a sheep farm in Wales, two miles from the nearest village and twenty from the nearest town. When I was little, I thought every child's day started at six o'clock with feeding the animals. It was only when I started school that I realised my life was a bit different from my classmates'.","The best time of year was spring, when the lambs were born. For about six weeks my parents hardly slept, and my brother and I helped as much as we could. I'll never forget the first time I helped a lamb that wasn't breathing. My dad showed me what to do, and when it finally stood up on its wobbly legs, I felt like a hero.","Of course, farm life had its difficulties. We couldn't go on holiday, because someone always had to look after the animals, and in winter the road to the village was sometimes blocked by snow for days. There was no bus, so if I wanted to see my friends at the weekend, my mum had to drive me – and she was usually too busy.","As a teenager, I sometimes felt jealous of my friends in town, who could go to the cinema or the shops whenever they liked. But looking back, I think the farm taught me things they never learnt. I knew how to work hard, I wasn't afraid of getting dirty, and I understood where our food came from.","I now live in a city and work as a nurse. People are often surprised when I tell them about my childhood. I still go home to help with the lambs every spring, and I've noticed something: whenever I'm stressed at work, I think about those quiet fields, and I feel calm again."],qs:[{q:"When did the writer realise her life was unusual?",o:["when she first fed the animals","when she became a teenager","when she started going to school","when she moved to the city"],a:2,x:"‘It was only when I started school that I realised my life was a bit different.’"},{q:"How did the writer feel after helping the lamb?",o:["exhausted","frightened","very proud","worried that it was still ill"],a:2,x:"‘I felt like a hero.’"},{q:"What was a problem for the writer at weekends?",o:["The bus to the village was always late.","Her friends didn't want to visit the farm.","She had to work on the farm all day.","It was difficult to get to see her friends."],a:3,x:"There was no bus, so her mum had to drive her – ‘and she was usually too busy’."},{q:"What does the writer think now about growing up on a farm?",o:["It gave her useful skills and attitudes.","Her friends in town learnt more than she did.","She would have preferred to live in town.","She missed out on too many things."],a:0,x:"‘The farm taught me things they never learnt’: working hard, not being afraid of getting dirty, knowing where food comes from."},{q:"What does the writer say in the last paragraph?",o:["She never goes back to the farm.","Thinking about the farm helps her relax.","She wants to become a farmer.","Her job as a nurse is very similar to farming."],a:1,x:"‘Whenever I'm stressed at work, I think about those quiet fields, and I feel calm again.’"}]},4:{title:"Meet Max, our new classmate",intro:"an article about a school that uses a robot in lessons",paras:["When the students of Class 6B came back after the summer holidays, they found a new classmate waiting for them. Max is about a metre tall, has big round eyes and can speak twelve languages. {1}","The school's head teacher, Mr Ali, bought Max to help students who find some subjects difficult. {2} ‘Some children are too shy to ask a teacher to explain something again,’ he says. ‘But they don't mind asking a robot.’","Max has quickly become popular. He can answer questions about maths and science, tell jokes and even dance. {3} ‘He's funny,’ says eleven-year-old Priya. ‘But sometimes he doesn't understand what we're saying.’","Not everyone is sure about the idea. Some parents worry that children will spend less time talking to real people. {4} ‘Max will never replace our teachers,’ says Mr Ali. ‘He's just another way to help.’","So far, the results look good. Students' maths marks have improved, and the shyest children are asking more questions in class. {5} ‘Next year, we'd like a second robot,’ says Mr Ali. ‘Max needs a friend!’"],options:["The school says this won't happen.","He is also a robot.","The students taught him to do that during the first week.","Max repeats explanations as many times as a student needs.","Even the teachers have started to enjoy working with him.","Robots are already used in many hospitals.","Max cost less than a new computer room.","Priya wants to become a scientist when she grows up."],key:[1,3,2,0,4],x:["A classmate with big round eyes who speaks twelve languages – ‘He is also a robot.’","Max helps students who find subjects difficult: he ‘repeats explanations as many times as a student needs’ – which is why shy children ask him.","Max can even dance, and ‘the students taught him to do that during the first week’.","Parents worry that children will talk less to real people – ‘The school says this won't happen.’","Good results for the students, ‘and even the teachers have started to enjoy working with him’."]},5:{title:"The Great Wall of China",text:`The Great Wall of China is one of the most famous buildings in the world. It was built over many centuries to {1} the country from attack. Parts of the wall are more than 2,000 years old, and the whole structure {2} for over 20,000 kilometres, although much of it is now in ruins.

The wall was built by millions of workers, including soldiers and farmers. The work was extremely hard, and many people lost their lives. Today, the wall is one of China's most popular tourist {3}, and around ten million people visit it every year. Some sections are so busy that visitors can hardly {4}.

It is often said that the Great Wall can be seen from space, but this is not {5}. Astronauts say that it is too narrow to see without special equipment. Even so, it is an {6} achievement, and a visit is something most people never forget.`,gaps:[{o:["keep","protect","hold","cover"],a:1,x:"protect something from attack. ‘Keep’ would need ‘safe’."},{o:["widens","lengthens","stretches","grows"],a:2,x:"stretch for + a distance. ‘Lengthen’ and ‘widen’ mean become longer or wider."},{o:["visits","attractions","tours","trips"],a:1,x:"a tourist attraction – a fixed phrase."},{o:["go","stay","last","move"],a:3,x:"can hardly move = there's almost no space to move."},{o:["true","real","sure","honest"],a:0,x:"this is not true – about a statement people make."},{o:["amazed","amazing","amazingly","amazement"],a:1,x:"an adjective before a noun: an amazing achievement. ‘Amazed’ describes how a person feels."}]},6:{title:"The day Biscuit went missing",text:`Last Saturday was one of the worst days of my life. I took our dog, Biscuit, for a walk in the woods, and I let him off his lead {0} usual. Suddenly he saw a rabbit and ran off after it. I called and called, {1} he didn't come back.

I searched for two hours before I went home to tell my parents. We all went back to the woods and looked everywhere, but there was no sign of him. That evening, my sister put a message on social media with a photo of Biscuit, asking people to call us {2} they saw him. I couldn't sleep at all that night.

The next morning, the phone rang. A farmer {3} lived five kilometres away had found Biscuit asleep in his barn! He was tired and dirty, but he wasn't hurt. When we arrived, Biscuit jumped up and licked my face as {4} nothing had happened.

From now on, I'm going to keep him on his lead near rabbits. I've also bought him a collar {5} our phone number on it, just {6} case he ever runs away again.`,example:{a:"as"},gaps:[{a:["but"],x:"Contrast: I called, but he didn't come back."},{a:["if"],x:"if = in the case that."},{a:["who","that"],x:"A relative pronoun for a person: a farmer who lived five kilometres away."},{a:["if","though"],x:"as if / as though + clause."},{a:["with"],x:"a collar with our phone number on it."},{a:["in"],x:"just in case = because it might happen."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.b1=LZ.data.papers.b1||[]).push({id:"b1t10",title:"Test 10",topics:"Notices and messages · Part-time jobs · A street musician · Our treehouse · Why cats purr · Camping in the rain",parts:{1:{title:"Short texts",items:[{kind:"Notice",look:"sign",text:`SCHOOL OFFICE
Lost property is kept for one month.
After that, it is given to charity.`,o:["You can collect lost things at any time.","Things nobody collects will be given away.","The school sells lost property after a month."],a:1,x:"After a month ‘it is given to charity’ – given away, not sold. You only have a month to collect it."},{kind:"Text message",look:"phone",text:"Leo – I've booked the tennis court for 5 pm tomorrow. Bring your own racket – mine's broken, so I'm borrowing my brother's. I'll bring the balls. Sam",q:"What must Leo bring?",o:["some tennis balls","a racket for Sam","his own racket"],a:2,x:"‘Bring your own racket.’ Sam is borrowing his brother's racket and bringing the balls."},{kind:"Email",look:"mail",text:`Dear Mr Brown,
Thank you for your application. We would like to invite you for an interview next Tuesday at 11 am. Please let us know if this time is not convenient.
Kind regards,
Lisa Grant`,o:["Mr Brown must reply by Tuesday.","Mr Brown has got the job.","Mr Brown can ask for a different interview time."],a:2,x:"‘Please let us know if this time is not convenient’ – he can ask for another time. He only has an interview so far."},{kind:"Label",look:"label",text:`SLEEPING BAG
For temperatures down to 5°C.
Machine wash at 30°C.
Carry bag included.`,o:["This sleeping bag is ideal for camping in the snow.","You can wash the sleeping bag in a machine.","The carry bag must be bought separately."],a:1,x:"‘Machine wash at 30°C’. It's only for temperatures down to 5°C – not snow – and the bag is included."},{kind:"Advert",look:"board",text:`FREE KITTENS
Three healthy kittens need loving homes.
Ready to leave their mother in two weeks.
Phone Jan after 6 pm.`,o:["You should phone Jan in the evening.","Jan is selling the kittens.","You can take a kitten home today."],a:0,x:"‘Phone Jan after 6 pm.’ The kittens are free, and they can't leave for two weeks."}]},2:{title:"Part-time jobs",intro:"eight advertisements for part-time jobs",who:"jobs",stem:"Which job would be most suitable for…",sections:[{name:"Café Assistant",text:["Serve customers in our busy seaside café on Saturdays and Sundays. Must be 16 or over. Free lunch every day you work."]},{name:"Dog Walker",text:["Walk three friendly dogs in the park on weekday afternoons, whatever the weather. You must love animals!"]},{name:"Library Helper",text:["Help children choose books and read with them on Saturday mornings. This is a volunteer job, so it's unpaid, but you'll get a certificate describing your experience."]},{name:"Swimming Teacher Assistant",text:["Help our swimming teachers with children's classes on Wednesday evenings. A lifesaving certificate is essential. Excellent pay."]},{name:"Shop Assistant",text:["Work in our clothes shop at weekends. Staff get 30% off everything we sell and first choice of the new collections."]},{name:"Online Tutor",text:["Help younger students with their maths homework in online lessons from your own home. Choose the hours that suit you."]},{name:"Kitchen Porter",text:["Wash dishes and keep the kitchen clean in a busy restaurant. Evenings only – you may not finish until midnight."]},{name:"Newspaper Delivery",text:["Deliver newspapers to homes in your area between 6 and 7 am, before school. You'll need your own bike."]}],qs:[{q:"Sara is very good at maths and wants to work from home when it suits her.",a:5,x:"Online Tutor: maths, from home, and you choose your hours."},{q:"Ben loves animals and being outside. He's free after school.",a:1,x:"Dog Walker: animals, outdoors, weekday afternoons."},{q:"Chloe wants experience of working with children for her university application. She doesn't mind not being paid.",a:2,x:"Library Helper: working with children, unpaid, with a certificate for her application. The swimming job needs a lifesaving certificate."},{q:"Mo is a strong swimmer with a lifesaving qualification, and he wants a job that pays well.",a:3,x:"Swimming Teacher Assistant: lifesaving certificate essential and excellent pay."},{q:"Yasmin loves fashion and would like to save money on clothes.",a:4,x:"Shop Assistant: a clothes shop with 30% off for staff."}]},3:{title:"Music on the street",intro:"an article in which a young violinist describes playing music in the street",paras:["I started busking – playing music in the street for money – when I was seventeen. I'd been playing the violin since I was six, but I was always too nervous to play in front of people. My teacher suggested busking as a way to get over my fear. ‘Nobody's listening properly,’ she said. ‘They're all busy shopping.’ She was right – and it worked.","The first time, I stood in the high street for an hour and made just two pounds. I nearly gave up. But the second week, an old man stopped and listened to three whole pieces. When I finished, he told me he used to play the violin himself, and he asked me to play a piece his wife had loved. After that, I knew I'd keep going.","Busking has taught me a lot about people. Children are the best audience – they stop, stare and sometimes dance. Businesspeople in a hurry rarely look up, but on Friday afternoons they're more relaxed and often put money in my case. Rainy days are hopeless, of course: nobody wants to stand still in the rain, and it's bad for the violin.","I've had some surprising moments, too. Last summer, a woman filmed me and posted the video online. It was watched thousands of times, and a week later I was asked to play at a wedding. Since then, I've played at several private parties, which pay much better than the street.","I'm now studying music at college, but I still busk most Saturdays. It's not really about the money any more. I love the fact that I never know who's going to stop and listen, and every week I meet someone new."],qs:[{q:"Why did the writer start busking?",o:["because her friends did it","to earn money for college","to practise new pieces of music","to help her stop feeling nervous"],a:3,x:"Her teacher suggested it ‘as a way to get over my fear’."},{q:"What made the writer decide to continue busking?",o:["earning a lot of money","a successful first day","a conversation with a listener","advice from her teacher"],a:2,x:"The old man who listened and talked to her: ‘After that, I knew I'd keep going.’ Her first day went badly."},{q:"What does the writer say about her audiences?",o:["She enjoys playing in the rain.","Children usually walk past without looking.","Businesspeople never give her money.","People are more generous at the end of the week."],a:3,x:"‘On Friday afternoons they're more relaxed and often put money in my case.’"},{q:"What happened after the video was posted online?",o:["She appeared on television.","She stopped busking.","She was invited to play at a wedding.","She started a music course."],a:2,x:"‘A week later I was asked to play at a wedding.’"},{q:"Why does the writer still busk?",o:["Her teacher wants her to.","She enjoys meeting different people.","She needs the money for college.","She hopes to become famous."],a:1,x:"‘It's not really about the money any more… every week I meet someone new.’"}]},4:{title:"Our treehouse",intro:"an article in which a teenager describes building a treehouse with her family",paras:["Last spring, my dad promised my little brother and me that we could have a treehouse in the big oak tree at the bottom of our garden. Neither of us believed him. {1}","Our first job was to design it. My brother wanted a slide, a rope bridge and a secret door. Dad said we had to be realistic. {2} In the end, we agreed on a platform with walls, a roof and a rope ladder.","Building it took every weekend for two months. Dad did the difficult parts, like fixing the platform to the tree, but we helped to measure, carry wood and paint. {3} Luckily, it wasn't serious, and Mum put a plaster on it.","The most important rule was that we couldn't hurt the tree. {4} Instead, Dad used special metal pieces that let the branches keep growing.","When it was finally finished, we slept in the treehouse on the first warm night of summer. {5} Now it's our favourite place to read, play and watch the birds – and Dad says it was the best project he's ever done."],options:["So we didn't hammer any nails into the trunk.","He had promised lots of things before and never done them.","Oak trees can live for more than five hundred years.","I hit my thumb with a hammer on the very first day.","The wood cost much more than we had expected.","Even so, we managed to include a secret door.","We didn't get much sleep because of the owls!","Our neighbours helped us to carry the heavy pieces."],key:[1,5,3,0,6],x:["Neither of them believed Dad – because ‘he had promised lots of things before and never done them’.","Dad wanted them to be realistic – ‘Even so, we managed to include a secret door.’","‘I hit my thumb’ explains ‘it wasn't serious, and Mum put a plaster on it’.","They couldn't hurt the tree, ‘so we didn't hammer any nails into the trunk. Instead…’","Sleeping in the treehouse – ‘we didn't get much sleep because of the owls!’"]},5:{title:"Why do cats purr?",text:`Most people think that cats purr because they are happy, and this is often {1}. A cat sitting on your knee and purring loudly is probably feeling calm and relaxed. However, scientists have {2} that cats also purr when they are frightened, hurt or even giving birth.

One theory is that purring helps cats to {3} themselves when they are stressed, in the same way that people might hum a song. Another is that the vibrations of a purr may help bones and muscles to {4} more quickly after an injury.

Kittens start purring when they are only a few days old, and they do it while their mother is feeding them. This probably tells the mother that everything is {5}. So next time your cat purrs, it might be saying ‘I'm happy’ – or it might be asking for {6}!`,gaps:[{o:["true","certain","real","sure"],a:0,x:"this is true = this is a correct idea."},{o:["invented","discovered","created","explored"],a:1,x:"discover that… = find out a fact. We invent or create new things."},{o:["sleep","stay","rest","calm"],a:3,x:"calm yourself = make yourself less stressed."},{o:["cure","treat","heal","fix"],a:2,x:"Bones and injuries heal. Doctors cure or treat illnesses."},{o:["nice","kind","fine","pleasant"],a:2,x:"everything is fine = there is no problem."},{o:["rescue","aid","service","help"],a:3,x:"ask for help."}]},6:{title:"Camping in the rain",text:`Last summer, my friends and I went camping in the mountains for the first time. The weather forecast said it would be sunny all week, so we didn't bother {0} bring waterproof jackets. That was a big mistake!

On the first night, it started raining at about midnight. Within an hour, water was coming {1} the tent, and our sleeping bags were soaking wet. We tried to sleep in the car, {2} there wasn't enough room for four people.

The next morning, we went to the nearest village and bought the cheapest raincoats we could find. They were bright yellow and looked terrible, but {3} least they kept us dry. For the rest of the week it rained almost every day, but we still had fun. We swam in a lake, cooked on a small stove and played cards for hours.

The lesson I learnt is never to trust the weather forecast completely. Next time, I'll pack for every kind of weather, {4} matter what the forecast says. And I'll make sure our tent is {5} better quality {6} the one we took this time!`,example:{a:"to"},gaps:[{a:["into","through"],x:"water coming into (or through) the tent."},{a:["but"],x:"Contrast: we tried, but there wasn't enough room."},{a:["at"],x:"at least = the one good thing is…"},{a:["no"],x:"no matter what = whatever."},{a:["of"],x:"be of better quality."},{a:["than"],x:"A comparative + than: better quality than the one we took."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t1",title:"Test 1",topics:"Boredom · Larks and owls · Laughter · Wildlife sound · The homework debate · Dry-stone walls · Changing careers",parts:{1:{title:"In praise of boredom",text:`For most of us, boredom is something to be avoided at all {0}. The moment it {1} in, we reach for our phones, and the empty minutes are quickly filled with messages, videos and news. Yet a growing body of research suggests that we may be doing ourselves a disservice.

Psychologists who study boredom argue that it serves a useful {2}. The uncomfortable feeling of having nothing to occupy us signals that our current situation is not meeting our needs, and this {3} rise to a desire for something more meaningful. In one well-known experiment, participants who had first been given a deliberately dull task went on to {4} up with more creative ideas than those who had not.

The problem, researchers say, is that we rarely allow boredom to run its {5}. Smartphones offer instant relief, so the restless state that might once have {6} to daydreaming, reflection or a new project is cut {7}. For this reason, some experts recommend building ‘unplugged’ time into our daily routines – a walk without headphones, say, or a train journey spent simply looking out of the window. It may feel strange at first, but it could turn out to be time well {8}.`,example:{o:["charges","prices","costs","fees"],a:2},gaps:[{o:["falls","sets","gets","comes"],a:1,x:"Something unpleasant sets in = it begins and seems likely to continue (boredom, winter, rain)."},{o:["aim","target","purpose","intention"],a:2,x:"serve a (useful) purpose – a fixed collocation."},{o:["brings","makes","takes","gives"],a:3,x:"give rise to something = cause it to appear."},{o:["come","make","put","think"],a:0,x:"come up with an idea. ‘Think up’ has no ‘with’; ‘put up with’ and ‘make up with’ mean something else."},{o:["path","course","route","way"],a:1,x:"let something run its course = allow it to continue naturally until it ends."},{o:["caused","brought","led","resulted"],a:2,x:"lead to something. ‘Result’ needs ‘in’; ‘cause’ and ‘bring’ take a direct object."},{o:["off","down","back","short"],a:3,x:"cut something short = end it sooner than expected."},{o:["served","used","passed","spent"],a:3,x:"time well spent – a fixed phrase for time used in a worthwhile way."}]},2:{title:"Larks and owls",text:`Whether you leap out of bed at dawn or struggle to function before ten o'clock is, {0} a large extent, determined by your genes. Scientists have identified dozens of genes linked to our ‘chronotype’ – our natural tendency to be a morning or an evening person – and it seems that no {1} how hard night owls try, they cannot simply train themselves to become larks.

This matters because {2} of modern life is organised around the early riser. School and working days typically start at a time that suits larks perfectly but leaves owls feeling as {3} they were permanently jet-lagged. Teenagers in particular are biologically programmed to fall asleep and wake up later, which is {4} some schools have experimented with later start times.

The results have been encouraging: where lessons began an hour later, pupils were not only more alert but also less likely to miss classes. {5} the evidence, however, many schools have been slow to change, largely because of the practical difficulties involved. Buses, sports fixtures and parents' working hours would all have to be rearranged, and few head teachers are willing to take on such a challenge {6} their own. Until attitudes shift, owls will simply have to make the {7} of a world that was not designed {8} them in mind.`,example:{a:"to"},gaps:[{a:["matter"],x:"no matter how + adjective/adverb = however much."},{a:["much","most"],x:"much/most of + uncountable noun: much of modern life."},{a:["if","though"],x:"feel as if / as though + clause."},{a:["why"],x:"which is why = and that is the reason that."},{a:["despite"],x:"Despite + noun = although there is… (‘In spite of’ would be three words)."},{a:["on"],x:"on your own = alone, without help."},{a:["best"],x:"make the best of something = deal as well as you can with a situation that isn't ideal."},{a:["with"],x:"with somebody in mind = thinking about them when you plan something."}]},3:{title:"No laughing matter",text:`Laughter is one of the most {0} of human behaviours,
and yet it remains surprisingly poorly understood. One common {1}
is that we laugh mainly at jokes. In fact, researchers have found that most laughter
occurs during {2} ordinary conversation, and that speakers themselves
laugh more than their listeners. This suggests that laughter is less a {3}
to something funny than a way of signalling friendliness and {4}.

Laughter also appears to have real health benefits. A good laugh
reduces stress hormones and may even increase our {5} to pain.
However, claims that it can cure serious illness are {6}, and most
scientists urge {7} when interpreting such research.
What is clear is that laughter is highly {8}: we are far more likely
to laugh when we are with other people than when we are alone.`,example:{w:"UNIVERSE",a:"universal"},gaps:[{w:"CONCEIVE",a:["misconception"],x:"Noun with a negative prefix: a misconception = a wrong belief."},{w:"ENTIRE",a:["entirely"],x:"Adverb before an adjective: entirely ordinary."},{w:"RESPOND",a:["response"],x:"Noun after ‘a’: a response to something."},{w:"AGREE",a:["agreement"],x:"Noun, parallel with ‘friendliness’: signalling friendliness and agreement."},{w:"TOLERATE",a:["tolerance"],x:"Noun after ‘our’: tolerance to/of pain."},{w:"FOUND",a:["unfounded"],x:"Negative adjective: unfounded claims = not based on evidence."},{w:"CAUTIOUS",a:["caution"],x:"Noun after ‘urge’: urge caution = advise people to be careful."},{w:"CONTAGION",a:["contagious"],x:"Adjective after ‘highly’: contagious = spreading easily from person to person."}]},4:{example:{s1:"James started learning the piano five years ago.",key:"BEEN",pre:"James",ans:"has been learning the piano",post:"for five years."},items:[{s1:"I'm certain it wasn't Jack who broke the window – he was away all weekend.",key:"HAVE",pre:"It",post:"who broke the window – he was away all weekend.",a:[["can't have been","cannot have been","couldn't have been","could not have been"],["jack"]],x:"Certainty about the past (negative): can't have / couldn't have + past participle."},{s1:"Without Maria's help, we would never have finished the project on time.",key:"IT",pre:"If",post:"Maria's help, we would never have finished the project on time.",a:[["it had not been","it hadn't been"],["for"]],x:"Third conditional: if it hadn't been for + noun = without."},{s1:"People say that the castle was built in the twelfth century.",key:"SAID",pre:"The castle",post:"in the twelfth century.",a:[["is said"],["to have been built"]],x:"Impersonal passive about the past: is said + to have been + past participle."},{s1:"I didn't realise how difficult the exam was until I started it.",key:"ONLY",pre:"It was",post:"I realised how difficult the exam was.",a:[["only when i started it","only once i started it","only after i started it"],["that"]],x:"Cleft sentence for emphasis: It was only when… that…"},{s1:"Tom regrets not taking the job in Canada.",key:"WISHES",pre:"Tom",post:"the job in Canada.",a:[["wishes he had","wishes that he had","wishes he'd","wishes that he'd"],["taken","accepted"]],x:"Regret about the past: wish + past perfect."},{s1:"It's pointless trying to persuade him to change his mind.",key:"POINT",pre:"There is",post:"him to change his mind.",a:[["no point in","no point"],["trying to persuade","trying to convince","persuading","convincing"]],x:"There is no point (in) + -ing = it's useless to do something."}]},5:{title:"Listening to the wild",intro:"an article about a wildlife sound recordist",paras:["Nadia Ferreira spends much of her working life lying perfectly still in places most people would pay good money to avoid. She has spent nights in waterlogged hides in the Scottish Highlands and whole days crouched beneath the canopy of the Brazilian rainforest, headphones clamped to her ears, waiting for a sound that may never come. ‘People assume the job is about equipment,’ she says. ‘Really, it's about patience – and a certain tolerance for discomfort.’","Ferreira is one of a small number of specialist sound recordists working in wildlife film-making. While camera operators tend to get the credit for the breathtaking images that make these documentaries so popular, it is often the soundtrack that makes a scene feel real. Much of what viewers hear, however, was not recorded at the same moment as the pictures. A camera with a long lens may be filming an animal half a kilometre away, far beyond the reach of any microphone, so the sound has to be captured separately, sometimes weeks later.","This is where things become controversial. Critics argue that adding sound after the event amounts to a kind of deception, and that audiences are entitled to know what is authentic and what has been assembled. Ferreira has some sympathy with this view, but only up to a point. ‘Every sound I record is real – the animal, the place, the time of year,’ she says. ‘What I won't do is invent something that doesn't happen in nature. If a bird doesn't sing at night, I'm not going to put it in a night scene because it sounds pretty.’ Other recordists, she admits, are less strict.","Her route into the profession was far from conventional. She trained as a music teacher and spent six years in a secondary school before a chance encounter changed her direction. On a camping holiday in Portugal, she met a recordist who was trying to capture the dawn chorus and spent a morning helping him carry his equipment. ‘I came home and couldn't stop thinking about it,’ she recalls. ‘Teaching had become a routine, and here was something that demanded complete concentration. I handed in my notice at the end of that term – which, looking back, was probably reckless.’","The first two years were difficult. Work was scarce, and the few jobs she was offered were poorly paid. She supported herself by giving piano lessons in the evenings and spent her savings on microphones. The turning point came when a production company needed someone at short notice to record frogs in a Costa Rican cloud forest. The recordist they had booked had fallen ill, and Ferreira was the only person available who could leave within forty-eight hours. ‘I'd never recorded a frog in my life,’ she laughs. ‘I spent the whole flight reading about them.’","Today she is in demand, but she worries about the future of the natural soundscapes she records. On several recent trips she has struggled to find locations free of human noise – the distant hum of a road, the drone of an aircraft. In some forests she first visited fifteen years ago, the variety of sounds has noticeably diminished. ‘You can hear a habitat declining before you can see it,’ she says. ‘Silence isn't peaceful when it's the wrong kind of silence.’","For all that, she remains convinced that her work matters. Recordings made today, she points out, may one day be the only evidence of what certain places once sounded like. ‘In a hundred years, someone might listen to a recording of mine and hear a species that no longer exists. That's a sobering thought – but it's also the reason I keep going.’"],qs:[{q:"In the first paragraph, Ferreira suggests that success as a sound recordist depends mainly on",o:["having access to the latest technology.","being able to cope with long periods of waiting.","travelling to places that few people visit.","recognising the sounds made by different species."],a:1,x:"‘People assume the job is about equipment… Really, it's about patience.’"},{q:"According to the second paragraph, why is sound often recorded separately from the pictures?",o:["Animals are frightened by recording equipment.","Camera operators prefer to work without a recordist nearby.","Viewers expect the sound to be clearer than in real life.","Microphones cannot pick up sounds from distant animals."],a:3,x:"The animal may be ‘half a kilometre away, far beyond the reach of any microphone’."},{q:"What is Ferreira's attitude to the criticism described in the third paragraph?",o:["She believes it comes from a misunderstanding of how films are made.","She accepts it completely and has changed the way she works.","She partly agrees with it, but considers her own approach honest.","She dismisses it as irrelevant to wildlife documentaries."],a:2,x:"‘Some sympathy with this view, but only up to a point’ – and she never invents sounds that don't happen in nature."},{q:"What does Ferreira say about her decision to leave teaching?",o:["She had been planning it for several years.","She now thinks it was a rather risky thing to do.","She regretted it during her first years as a recordist.","She was persuaded to do it by the recordist she met."],a:1,x:"‘Looking back, [it] was probably reckless.’ Reckless = taking risks without thinking."},{q:"How did Ferreira come to be offered the job in Costa Rica?",o:["She was an expert on the animals involved.","She had worked for the production company before.","She was able to travel at very short notice.","The original recordist recommended her."],a:2,x:"She ‘was the only person available who could leave within forty-eight hours’. She knew nothing about frogs (A)."},{q:"What point does Ferreira make in the last two paragraphs?",o:["Human noise has made recording impossible in many forests.","Silence is the most valuable thing a recordist can capture.","Most of her recordings will be of little interest in the future.","Changes in natural sound can reveal damage to the environment."],a:3,x:"‘You can hear a habitat declining before you can see it.’ She struggles to find quiet places, but doesn't say it's impossible (A)."}]},6:{title:"The homework debate",intro:"four extracts in which education experts discuss homework",who:"experts",stem:"Which expert…",sections:[{name:"Helen Marsh",text:["There is little doubt that homework, when it is well designed, can reinforce what pupils learn in class. The trouble is that much of it is not well designed. Too often, tasks are set simply because a timetable says homework must be set, and pupils quickly recognise an exercise that has no real purpose. Research consistently shows that the benefits for younger children are minimal, whereas for older teenagers preparing for exams, regular independent study makes a measurable difference. It is also worth remembering that pupils without support at home are the least likely to benefit. The question, then, is not whether homework should exist but how much, for whom and of what kind."]},{name:"Daniel Okoro",text:["Critics of homework often point to the stress it causes, and in some cases they are right to do so. Yet removing homework altogether would, I believe, widen the gap between children from different backgrounds. Pupils whose parents can pay for private tutors, or who have a quiet room at home, will continue to study outside school regardless; those without such advantages would simply fall further behind. Far from being unfair, a sensible amount of homework gives every child a structured opportunity to practise and so helps to close that gap. Where schools can do more is in offering supervised study spaces after lessons."]},{name:"Sophie Lindqvist",text:["In my experience, the amount of homework children receive has far less impact on their progress than the feedback they get on it. A piece of work that is marked carefully and discussed in class is worth ten that are collected and never mentioned again. Unfortunately, teachers are already stretched, and marking time is the first thing to disappear when workloads rise. I would also question the idea that homework levels the playing field. Children who struggle at school are often the ones who find it most discouraging, particularly if there is no one at home who can help them when they get stuck."]},{name:"Mark Brennan",text:["Much of the debate ignores what children actually do with their free time. An hour spent on a worksheet is an hour not spent reading for pleasure, playing sport or simply being bored – and all of these contribute to a child's development in ways that are hard to measure but no less real. Nor should we forget that it is children from busier or poorer homes who tend to be left behind by it. That said, I am not persuaded that abolishing homework is the answer, least of all for older pupils, for whom the habit of working independently is essential preparation for university and employment."]}],qs:[{q:"has a different view from the others on how homework affects the gap between children from different backgrounds?",a:1,x:"Okoro says homework ‘helps to close that gap’. Marsh, Lindqvist and Brennan all say children without support at home are left behind."},{q:"shares Brennan's view that homework is particularly valuable for older pupils?",a:0,x:"Marsh: ‘for older teenagers preparing for exams, regular independent study makes a measurable difference.’"},{q:"differs from Okoro by identifying detailed feedback, rather than opportunities to practise, as the main benefit of homework?",a:2,x:"Lindqvist values ‘the feedback they get on it’; Okoro values ‘a structured opportunity to practise’. This compares what each sees as the source of benefit."},{q:"shares Marsh's view that independent study is especially worthwhile for older pupils?",a:3,x:"Brennan calls independent work ‘essential preparation for university and employment’ for older pupils; Marsh says it ‘makes a measurable difference’ for older teenagers."}]},7:{title:"Building a wall, stone by stone",intro:"an article about a journalist who learnt the craft of dry-stone walling",paragraphs:!0,paras:["I had driven past the walls a thousand times without giving them a second thought. They run across the hills of the Yorkshire Dales in long grey lines, dividing field from field, climbing slopes so steep it seems impossible that anyone could have carried a stone up them, let alone built with them. It was only when a friend mentioned that she was taking a weekend course in dry-stone walling that I began to wonder how they were made.","{1}","Our instructor, a farmer called Geoff Hartley, had been building and repairing walls for over forty years. He began by walking us along a stretch that had collapsed during the winter, pointing out where the stones had shifted and why. ‘Water gets in, freezes, pushes everything apart,’ he said. ‘A good wall moves a little and settles. A bad one just falls down.’","{2}","By mid-morning my hands were raw and my back ached, and I had placed perhaps a dozen stones. Geoff watched, occasionally stepping in to lift out something I had put in with great care and replace it with a stone I had rejected minutes earlier. It fitted perfectly, of course. ‘You're looking at the face,’ he told me. ‘Look at the whole stone. What's it going to do when the next one goes on top?’","{3}","What struck me most, though, was how little the basic technique had changed. Geoff showed us a section of wall he believed to be more than two hundred years old. Apart from a covering of moss, it was indistinguishable from the one we were building. The same rules applied: two faces leaning slightly inwards, the space between them packed tightly with small stones, and long ‘through stones’ laid across the width at intervals to tie the two sides together.","{4}","That, Geoff explained, is partly why so many walls are now in poor condition. Farming has changed, and the labour needed to maintain them simply isn't there. Some landowners have replaced damaged sections with wire fences, which are cheaper and quicker to put up but offer none of the shelter that sheep and wildlife find in a stone wall.","{5}","On the second afternoon, something shifted. I stopped trying to force stones into the spaces I wanted to fill and began, as Geoff kept telling us, to let the stones decide. My progress was still painfully slow, but the section I was working on began to look like a wall rather than a heap.","{6}","I have not become a waller. My contribution to the landscape amounts to roughly a metre and a half of wall in a field near Malham, and I suspect Geoff has quietly rebuilt some of it since. But I no longer drive past those long grey lines without seeing them for what they are: the work of countless hands, repeated patiently over centuries, and still standing."],options:["There are signs, however, that attitudes are changing. Grants are now available to help farmers restore their walls, and courses like the one I was on are often fully booked months in advance. Many of those who attend, Geoff told me, are people like me: curious outsiders who have never lifted anything heavier than a laptop.","It is a question every waller asks, usually without thinking. Geoff could glance at a pile of apparently identical stones and choose exactly the right one; I would turn the same stone over and over, try it in three different places and still get it wrong. There is no shortcut, he said. You learn by doing it badly for a long time.","Stone walls are not unique to Yorkshire, of course. Similar structures can be found across Ireland, in parts of France and as far away as Peru, where they were built by people who had never heard of the Dales.","I signed up for the same course the following week. Early on a cold Saturday in March, I found myself standing in a muddy field with seven other beginners, a pile of stones and a set of instructions that sounded deceptively simple: pick up a stone, find the right place for it, and never pick it up twice.","When we finished on Sunday evening, Geoff walked along the line of our work, pressing each section firmly with his boot. Mine held. He said nothing, but he nodded, and I have rarely felt so pleased with myself.","Then he handed out gloves and set us to work taking down what remained of the damaged section, sorting the stones into piles by size as we went. It was slow, heavy work, and it soon became clear that rebuilding would be slower still.","Building a wall this way requires no cement or mortar, and very little in the way of tools. But it does require time – far more than most people are prepared to give. An experienced waller might complete a few metres a day; a beginner is lucky to manage one."],key:[3,5,1,6,0,4],x:["The friend's course makes the writer curious; D: ‘I signed up for the same course the following week.’ The next paragraph introduces the instructor.","Geoff shows them the collapsed stretch; F: ‘Then he… set us to work taking down what remained of the damaged section’, which leads to ‘By mid-morning my hands were raw’.","B: ‘It is a question every waller asks’ refers back to Geoff's question ‘What's it going to do when the next one goes on top?’","G ends with how slow walling is; ‘That… is partly why so many walls are now in poor condition’ – there isn't enough labour.","After the problem (walls decaying, wire fences), A introduces the contrast: ‘There are signs, however, that attitudes are changing.’","The writer's section ‘began to look like a wall’; E: on Sunday evening Geoff tests it and it holds. C is about other countries and doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"Second acts",intro:"an article in which four people talk about changing career later in life",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Rosa, accountant → furniture maker",text:["I spent eighteen years in accountancy, and I was good at it, which made leaving harder. There was no dramatic moment – just a slow realisation that I dreaded Monday mornings. I'd always made things with my hands as a hobby, so I took an evening course in furniture making, then a second one. What finally pushed me was a customer at a craft fair who asked if I took commissions – I'd never met her before, and I hadn't even considered it. The first year of running my own workshop was financially terrifying. My family were supportive, although my father still introduces me as ‘our daughter, the accountant’. What I value most now is that I can see what I've achieved at the end of each day. With figures, the work was never really finished."]},{name:"Pavel, pilot → primary teacher",text:["Flying was my childhood dream, and for twenty years I lived it. But the schedule took its toll – I missed birthdays, school plays, my son's first steps. When the airline offered voluntary redundancy, I took it before I could talk myself out of it, so that I could finally be at home with my family. Retraining meant a year at university alongside students half my age, which was humbling. I'd assumed that running a cockpit would prepare me for running a classroom. It didn't: thirty seven-year-olds are far less predictable than a Boeing 737. Financially it was a big step down, and I'd be lying if I said I didn't sometimes miss the view from the flight deck. But I'm home every evening, and that was the point."]},{name:"Amara, journalist → nurse",text:["Journalism was changing when I left – fewer jobs, more pressure to produce content quickly, less time to get things right. I'd reported on health for years and spent a lot of time in hospitals, and I found myself envying the nurses I interviewed. They seemed to know exactly why their work mattered. Nursing school was the hardest thing I've ever done, academically and physically; I was forty-three and exhausted most of the time. What I hadn't expected was how useful my old skills would be. Interviewing people for a living teaches you to listen, and to notice what people aren't saying. That turns out to be a large part of nursing."]},{name:"Jonas, lawyer → baker",text:["I didn't plan to become a baker; I planned to take six months off. I'd burnt out after a long case, and my doctor told me to rest. I started baking bread at home to fill the time, a neighbour asked to buy a loaf, then her friends did, and within a few months I was getting up at four to bake for a local café. By the time the six months were up, I couldn't imagine going back. Some of my former colleagues were openly dismissive, which hurt at first. I earn a fraction of what I used to, but my outgoings have shrunk too, and I've discovered I need far less than I thought. My one regret is not having done it ten years earlier."]}],qs:[{q:"was surprised to find that a skill from their old job was useful in the new one?",a:2,x:"Amara: ‘What I hadn't expected was how useful my old skills would be.’"},{q:"wrongly believed that their previous experience would help in their new role?",a:1,x:"Pavel: ‘I'd assumed that running a cockpit would prepare me for running a classroom. It didn't.’"},{q:"did not originally intend to change career permanently?",a:3,x:"Jonas: ‘I didn't plan to become a baker; I planned to take six months off.’"},{q:"mentions a family member who has not fully accepted the change?",a:0,x:"Rosa's father ‘still introduces me as “our daughter, the accountant”’."},{q:"had a negative reaction from people they used to work with?",a:3,x:"Jonas: ‘Some of my former colleagues were openly dismissive.’"},{q:"was influenced by watching people who already did the job?",a:2,x:"Amara ‘found myself envying the nurses I interviewed’."},{q:"says the new job gives them a sense of having finished something?",a:0,x:"Rosa: ‘I can see what I've achieved at the end of each day. With figures, the work was never really finished.’"},{q:"found retraining physically exhausting?",a:2,x:"Amara: ‘the hardest thing I've ever done, academically and physically… exhausted most of the time.’"},{q:"was finally prompted to act by a stranger's question?",a:0,x:"Rosa: a customer she'd never met asked if she took commissions. Jonas's first customer was a neighbour."},{q:"changed career mainly in order to spend more time with family?",a:1,x:"Pavel: ‘so that I could finally be at home with my family… that was the point.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t2",title:"Test 2",topics:"Slow travel · Why habits stick · City foxes · Learning the cello at 42 · Working from home · Volunteer map-makers · Starting a small business",parts:{1:{title:"The rise of slow travel",text:`For decades, the goal of most holidaymakers was to see as much as possible in as little time as possible. Recently, however, a growing number of travellers have {0} their backs on this approach in favour of ‘slow travel’. Rather than rushing from one famous sight to the next, slow travellers stay in a single place for weeks, getting to know its people, its food and its {1} of life.

Supporters argue that this style of travel {2} for a far richer experience. By staying put, visitors are more likely to {3} up conversations with locals and to discover places that tourists rarely {4} foot in. There are environmental benefits, too: fewer flights mean a smaller carbon footprint.

Critics, on the other hand, point out that slow travel is {5} of reach for most people. Holiday time is limited, and taking a month off work is simply not an {6} for them. Nevertheless, even travellers on a short break can adopt some of its principles – choosing trains over planes, for example, or {7} the urge to fill every hour with activities. As one enthusiast puts it, the aim is not to see more but to see better, and to {8} the most of wherever you happen to be.`,example:{o:["turned","faced","shown","put"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["speed","pace","rate","tempo"],a:1,x:"the pace of life = how fast or slowly people live."},{o:["does","makes","brings","leads"],a:1,x:"make for something = result in it. None of the others works with ‘for’ here."},{o:["hit","take","strike","set"],a:2,x:"strike up a conversation = start one with someone you don't know."},{o:["put","set","place","step"],a:1,x:"set foot in a place = go there."},{o:["beyond","out","away","far"],a:1,x:"out of reach = impossible to get or do."},{o:["opportunity","occasion","option","offer"],a:2,x:"not an option = not possible."},{o:["refusing","denying","rejecting","resisting"],a:3,x:"resist the urge / temptation to do something."},{o:["make","take","get","do"],a:0,x:"make the most of something = use it as fully as possible."}]},2:{title:"Why habits stick",text:`Most of what we do each day is the result not of careful decisions {0} of habit. Psychologists estimate that {1} to half of our daily behaviour is automatic – the same actions, performed in the same places, at roughly the same times.

Habits form {2} a behaviour is repeated in a stable context. Each repetition strengthens the link between the situation and the action, {3} that eventually the situation alone is enough to trigger it. This explains why it is so hard to break a bad habit: the cue is still there, {4} determined we are.

The good news is that the same process can work in our favour. Rather {5} relying on willpower, experts recommend changing the environment. Someone {6} wants to eat less sugar, for instance, would do better not to buy biscuits in the {7} place, instead of trying to resist them every evening. Small changes of this kind, repeated often enough, can make a lasting difference – {8} it usually takes months rather than days.`,example:{a:"but"},gaps:[{a:["up"],x:"up to = as many as."},{a:["when","whenever","if"],x:"A time clause: when a behaviour is repeated."},{a:["so"],x:"so that = with the result that."},{a:["however"],x:"however + adjective = no matter how determined we are."},{a:["than"],x:"Rather than + -ing."},{a:["who","that"],x:"A relative pronoun for a person: someone who wants…"},{a:["first"],x:"in the first place = from the beginning, before anything else."},{a:["although","though"],x:"although / though introduces a contrast."}]},3:{title:"City foxes",text:`Foxes were once an {0} sight in British cities,
but their {1} to urban life has been remarkable.
City foxes are {2} bolder than their country cousins,
and many have lost their natural {3} of humans.
Some residents enjoy seeing them; others regard them as a constant {4},
complaining about noise at night and {5} bins in the street.

Despite their reputation, foxes are rarely {6} towards people or pets.
Researchers who study them have found that their diet is surprisingly {7}:
they will eat anything from insects and fallen fruit
to the {8} that people leave out for birds.`,example:{w:"COMMON",a:"uncommon"},gaps:[{w:"ADAPT",a:["adaptation","adaptability"],x:"A noun after their: adaptation (or adaptability) to something."},{w:"NOTICE",a:["noticeably"],x:"An adverb before a comparative: noticeably bolder."},{w:"SUSPECT",a:["suspicion"],x:"A noun: their natural suspicion of humans."},{w:"ANNOY",a:["annoyance"],x:"A noun after a constant: annoyance."},{w:"TURN",a:["overturned"],x:"Two changes: over- + -ed: overturned bins."},{w:"AGGRESSION",a:["aggressive"],x:"An adjective after are: aggressive towards."},{w:"VARY",a:["varied"],x:"An adjective after surprisingly: varied."},{w:"FEED",a:["food"],x:"feed (verb) → food (noun)."}]},4:{example:{s1:"I'd prefer you not to smoke in here.",key:"RATHER",pre:"I'd",ans:"rather you didn't smoke",post:"in here."},items:[{s1:"It was wrong of you to shout at the waiter.",key:"SHOULD",pre:"You",post:"at the waiter.",a:[["should not have","shouldn't have"],["shouted"]],x:"Criticism of a past action: shouldn't have + past participle."},{s1:"The queue was so long that we gave up and went home.",key:"SUCH",pre:"There was",post:"that we gave up and went home.",a:[["such a long"],["queue"]],x:"such + a + adjective + noun + that: the same result as so + adjective + that."},{s1:"I'm sure she didn't take your phone.",key:"HAVE",pre:"She",post:"your phone.",a:[["can't have","cannot have","couldn't have","could not have"],["taken"]],x:"Certainty about the past (negative): can't have + past participle."},{s1:"People think the company is losing money.",key:"THOUGHT",pre:"The company",post:"money.",a:[["is thought"],["to be losing"]],x:"is thought + to be + -ing for something happening now."},{s1:"We only realised how serious it was when the doctor called.",key:"UNTIL",pre:"It was not",post:"called that we realised how serious it was.",a:[["until the"],["doctor"]],x:"It was not until … that … = only when."},{s1:"Despite working hard, he failed the exam.",key:"EVEN",pre:"He failed the exam",post:"hard.",a:[["even though he"],["worked","was working","had worked"]],x:"even though + clause (despite + -ing)."}]},5:{title:"Starting from scratch",intro:"an article in which a journalist describes learning the cello as an adult",paras:["For most of my adult life, I told myself that I had missed my chance. Music, I believed, was something you learnt as a child, when your brain was still flexible and your time was your own. By the time I turned forty-two, I had a demanding job, two teenage children and a firm conviction that the window had closed. So when a friend who teaches the cello offered me a free lesson, I accepted mainly out of politeness.","That first lesson was humbling. I could not hold the bow without my wrist aching, and the sound I produced resembled a door creaking in a gale. My teacher, Elena, was unbothered. ‘Everyone sounds like that at the start,’ she said. ‘Children just don't mind as much.’ It was, I later realised, the most useful thing anyone told me that year.","Research into adult learning backs her up. While children do have certain advantages – particularly in developing a good ear – adults are often better at understanding structure, planning their practice and making sense of what their teachers tell them. What holds many of us back is not ability but embarrassment. We are used to being competent, and being bad at something in front of others feels almost unbearable.","I dealt with this, at first, by practising only when the house was empty. This turned out to be a mistake. With a busy family, the house was rarely empty, and weeks would pass without my touching the instrument. Eventually Elena suggested I simply practise for fifteen minutes every evening, whoever was listening. My children complained loudly for about a week and then stopped noticing altogether.","Progress, when it came, was uneven. There were weeks when a piece I had been struggling with suddenly fell into place, and others when I seemed to go backwards. Elena encouraged me to keep a practice diary, which helped more than I expected: on bad days, I could look back and see how far I had come since the creaking-door stage.","Eighteen months on, I am under no illusion that I will ever be a professional musician. But last month I played in a small concert for Elena's students – mostly children – and got through a short piece by Bach without a serious mistake. My hands were shaking, but I enjoyed it more than almost anything I have done in years.","What I value most is not the playing itself but what it has done to the rest of my life. Being a beginner again has made me more patient – with myself, and with my own children as they learn to drive and cook and revise for exams. I have stopped believing that there is a right age for starting things. The window, it turns out, was never closed; I had simply stopped looking at it."],qs:[{q:"Why did the writer agree to have the first lesson?",o:["She had always secretly wanted to learn an instrument.","She did not want to seem rude to her friend.","She wanted to prove that she was not too old.","She thought her children would enjoy it."],a:1,x:"‘I accepted mainly out of politeness.’ She believed ‘the window had closed’, so she wasn't trying to prove anything (C)."},{q:"What does the writer suggest about Elena's comment in the second paragraph?",o:["It was meant to make her give up.","It showed that Elena preferred teaching children.","It helped her in a way she only understood later.","It was an unkind joke about her playing."],a:2,x:"‘It was, I later realised, the most useful thing anyone told me that year.’"},{q:"According to the third paragraph, what often stops adults from learning?",o:["the fear of looking incompetent","a lack of natural ability","not having enough time","poor advice from teachers"],a:0,x:"‘What holds many of us back is not ability but embarrassment.’"},{q:"Why did the writer change the way she practised?",o:["Her children asked her not to practise in the evenings.","Waiting for an empty house meant she hardly practised at all.","Her teacher said she was practising too much.","She wanted her family to hear her progress."],a:1,x:"‘The house was rarely empty, and weeks would pass without my touching the instrument.’"},{q:"What does the writer say about her practice diary?",o:["It made her feel worse on bad days.","Elena read it every week.","It helped her learn new pieces faster.","It turned out to be more helpful than she had thought."],a:3,x:"‘Elena encouraged me to keep a practice diary, which helped more than I expected.’"},{q:"What is the main point of the final paragraph?",o:["Learning the cello has changed her outlook more generally.","She hopes to perform in more concerts in future.","Her children have now started learning instruments.","She regrets not starting music as a child."],a:0,x:"‘What I value most is not the playing itself but what it has done to the rest of my life.’"}]},6:{title:"Working from home: four views",intro:"four extracts in which commentators discuss working from home",who:"commentators",stem:"Which commentator…",sections:[{name:"Priya Nair",text:["Working from home has given many employees back the hours they once lost to commuting, and most report being at least as productive as before. The real risk lies elsewhere: younger staff, who learn largely by watching experienced colleagues, miss out badly when everyone is at home. Companies that want to keep remote working should create regular, structured opportunities for junior employees to spend time with senior ones."]},{name:"Tom Hale",text:["The claim that people work just as well at home is, in my view, overstated. Productivity figures are often based on employees' own estimates, which are hardly objective. What is undeniable is that losing the daily commute has improved many people's wellbeing. Yet this benefit does not justify the damage done to teamwork, which depends on the informal conversations that simply don't happen on video calls."]},{name:"Lena Fischer",text:["Productivity has generally held up, but much of the debate assumes that everyone's home is a suitable place to work. For people living in small flats or with young children, it often isn't, and employers that insist on remote working are, in effect, passing office costs on to their staff. That said, flexibility is highly valued: offered a choice, most employees opt for a mix of home and office."]},{name:"Sam Okoro",text:["It is striking how quickly working from home became normal, and I see no reason to reverse it. Output has held up well in most sectors – companies' own systems show this, not just surveys. Where I share the concerns of some critics is over new recruits: people starting their careers need guidance that is much harder to give remotely, and companies must address this seriously."]}],qs:[{q:"shares Okoro's concern about people who are new to their jobs?",a:0,x:"Nair: ‘younger staff, who learn largely by watching experienced colleagues, miss out badly’."},{q:"has a different opinion from the others about whether people work as well at home?",a:1,x:"Hale calls the claim ‘overstated’. Nair, Fischer and Okoro all say productivity has held up."},{q:"mentions a cost of working from home for employees that the others don't?",a:2,x:"Fischer: unsuitable homes, and employers ‘passing office costs on to their staff’."},{q:"agrees with Hale that not having to commute has improved people's lives?",a:0,x:"Nair: remote work ‘has given many employees back the hours they once lost to commuting’."}]},7:{title:"Mapping the world, one street at a time",intro:"an article about volunteers who make maps for aid agencies",paragraphs:!0,paras:["On a wet Tuesday evening, about thirty people are sitting in a university library, staring intently at satellite images of a town most of them will never visit. Every few seconds, someone clicks to outline the roof of a building or trace the line of a road. They are volunteers for a project that produces free online maps of places that commercial map-makers have largely ignored.","{1}","The organiser, a geography lecturer named Claire Bouchard, explains why this matters. When a flood or an earthquake strikes, aid agencies need to know where people live and how to reach them. ‘In a lot of the world, that information simply doesn't exist,’ she says. ‘Our volunteers create it, often in a matter of days.’","{2}","I am given a short tutorial and a small square of countryside to work on. It is surprisingly absorbing. Buildings, I discover, are easy to spot on bright days but much harder in the shadow of trees, and a dirt track can look almost identical to a dry riverbed. After an hour, I have mapped forty-three buildings and one road, which Claire describes kindly as ‘a solid start’.","{3}","That checking is essential. Every piece of work is reviewed by a more experienced volunteer before it is used, and mistakes are sent back with comments. Some of the reviewers have been involved for more than ten years and can map an entire village in the time it takes a newcomer to finish a street.","{4}","Not everyone is convinced by this approach. Some researchers argue that maps drawn by outsiders can miss what really matters to local residents – which paths are unsafe in the rainy season, for example, or which buildings are empty. Claire accepts the criticism. Increasingly, she says, the project trains people in the areas being mapped to add the details that no satellite can see.","{5}","By the end of the evening, the group has mapped more than two thousand buildings. Nobody here will ever meet the people who live in them, and most will never know whether their work was used.","{6}","As I leave, I ask one volunteer, a retired engineer, why he comes every week. He thinks for a moment. ‘It's the only thing I do,’ he says, ‘where I'm absolutely sure it's useful.’"],options:["Such speed comes from years of practice, but also from a genuine enjoyment of the task. One reviewer tells me she finds it relaxing, ‘like doing a jigsaw that actually helps someone’.","The satellite images themselves are provided free of charge by several space agencies, although they are sometimes a few years out of date.","It is the kind of work that sounds tedious, and in some ways it is. Yet the atmosphere in the room is anything but dull: people chat quietly, share snacks and occasionally cheer when a large area is finished.","That does not seem to bother them. What matters, several tell me, is knowing that if something goes wrong in that town, the people sent to help will not be working blind.","My pride is short-lived, however. When Claire checks my work, she finds that three of my ‘buildings’ are in fact large rocks, and that my road stops abruptly in the middle of a field.","Her words were put to the test last year, when a cyclone hit part of the coast the group had been mapping. Within a week, rescue teams were using their maps to plan routes to villages that had been cut off.","In one recent project, local volunteers walked every street of their town with smartphones, recording the names of roads and the locations of wells, clinics and schools."],key:[2,5,4,0,6,3],x:["C describes the room and the work just introduced; ‘why this matters’ in the next paragraph refers back to that work.","F: ‘Her words were put to the test’ follows Claire's claim that volunteers create maps ‘in a matter of days’.","E: ‘My pride is short-lived’ follows ‘a solid start’, and ‘That checking is essential’ picks up ‘When Claire checks my work’.","A: ‘Such speed’ refers to reviewers who map a village while a newcomer finishes a street.","G gives an example of training local people to add details no satellite can see.","D: ‘That does not seem to bother them’ answers ‘most will never know whether their work was used’. B doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"Going it alone",intro:"an article in which four people talk about starting a small business",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Grace – a bakery",text:["I opened the bakery with my savings and a loan from my aunt, which in hindsight was a risk I shouldn't have taken with family money – we're fine now, but there were months when I couldn't sleep. What I got right was the location: a quiet street near two schools, where rents were low and parents passed twice a day. I'd advise anyone starting out to spend a week just watching who walks past a shop before signing anything. The hardest part isn't the baking, it's managing staff; I'd never been anyone's boss before."]},{name:"Ravi – a sports app",text:["Our first version flopped. We'd spent a year building features we thought people wanted, and almost nobody used them. The turning point was a conversation with a football coach who told us, quite bluntly, that all he needed was a way to tell parents when matches were cancelled. We rebuilt the app around that single idea in three weeks. Funding has come from investors, which gives us room to grow, but I sometimes miss the days when the only people we answered to were our users."]},{name:"Marta – a repair workshop",text:["I'd been fixing friends' bikes and furniture for years before I realised people would pay for it. I didn't take out a loan – I started with tools I already owned and rented a corner of a garage – so growth has been slow but steady. Customers often tell me they chose us because we explain what went wrong, rather than just handing back the item. What nobody warned me about was the paperwork: taxes, insurance, safety certificates. I now spend one day a week at a desk, which I never imagined."]},{name:"Owen – an online language school",text:["I set the business up while still working full-time as a teacher, which meant a year of evenings and weekends and very little sleep. It was worth it, because I only resigned once the school was earning enough to replace my salary. Our biggest mistake was pricing: we charged far too little at first, assuming students wouldn't pay more for online lessons. When we raised prices, we actually gained customers, which taught me that people often judge quality by cost."]}],qs:[{q:"regrets the way they paid for the business at the start?",a:0,x:"Grace: the loan from her aunt was ‘a risk I shouldn't have taken with family money’."},{q:"changed their product completely after listening to one person?",a:1,x:"Ravi rebuilt the app around what one football coach needed."},{q:"kept their previous job until the business was successful?",a:3,x:"Owen: ‘I only resigned once the school was earning enough to replace my salary.’"},{q:"was surprised by how much administration the business involves?",a:2,x:"Marta: ‘What nobody warned me about was the paperwork.’"},{q:"found that customers valued something besides the work itself?",a:2,x:"Marta's customers value that ‘we explain what went wrong’."},{q:"discovered that raising prices had an unexpected result?",a:3,x:"Owen: ‘When we raised prices, we actually gained customers.’"},{q:"recommends watching people before choosing where to open?",a:0,x:"Grace: ‘spend a week just watching who walks past a shop before signing anything’."},{q:"feels that outside funding has cost them some independence?",a:1,x:"Ravi misses ‘the days when the only people we answered to were our users’."},{q:"did not borrow money to get started?",a:2,x:"Marta: ‘I didn't take out a loan.’ Grace borrowed from her aunt."},{q:"finds managing other people the most difficult part?",a:0,x:"Grace: ‘The hardest part isn't the baking, it's managing staff.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t3",title:"Test 3",topics:"Urban cycling · Procrastination · Coral reefs · The science of taste · Returning museum objects · Free-diving · How novelists work",parts:{1:{title:"Pedal power",text:`Over the past two decades, cycling has undergone something of a {0} in many European cities. Where bicycles were once seen as the preserve of students and enthusiasts, they are now a common {1} on the morning commute. City authorities have played a {2} part in this change, investing heavily in separate lanes that make riders feel safer.

The benefits are hard to {3}. Cyclists enjoy better health, congestion is eased and air quality improves. Yet the transition has not been entirely smooth. Some drivers resent losing road space, and shopkeepers have sometimes {4} that fewer parking spaces would drive customers away – fears that research has largely failed to {5} out. In fact, studies suggest that cyclists tend to shop locally more often, even if they spend less on each visit.

For the cycling boom to continue, experts say, cities must {6} beyond painting lines on roads. Secure parking, good lighting and routes that {7} seamlessly with public transport are all essential. Without them, many people will simply not feel {8} enough to leave the car at home.`,example:{o:["renewal","return","recovery","revival"],a:3},gaps:[{o:["vision","scene","view","sight"],a:3,x:"a common sight = something you often see."},{o:["chief","prime","key","main"],a:2,x:"play a key part/role in something – a fixed collocation."},{o:["contradict","dispute","object","argue"],a:1,x:"hard to dispute = hard to deny. ‘Argue’ and ‘object’ would need a preposition."},{o:["alerted","notified","warned","threatened"],a:2,x:"warn that + clause. ‘Alert’ and ‘notify’ need a person as object; ‘threaten’ means something different."},{o:["carry","hold","back","bear"],a:3,x:"bear something out = show that it is true."},{o:["reach","go","pass","exceed"],a:1,x:"go beyond something = do more than it. ‘Exceed’ takes a direct object."},{o:["connect","unite","attach","fasten"],a:0,x:"connect with public transport = link up with it."},{o:["assured","confident","certain","definite"],a:1,x:"feel confident enough to do something."}]},2:{title:"Tomorrow, I promise",text:`Almost everyone procrastinates {0} time to time, but for some people putting things off becomes a serious problem. Contrary {1} popular belief, chronic procrastinators are not simply lazy. If {2}, many of them care too much about doing a task well. Faced with the possibility of failure, they delay starting, which, of course, only makes success {3} likely.

Psychologists now see procrastination as a problem of managing emotions rather {4} time. When a task makes us anxious or bored, avoiding it brings immediate relief – and it is this short-term reward that keeps the habit going. The trouble is that the relief does not last, and the task is still there, now accompanied {5} guilt.

The good news is that the habit can be broken. One effective technique is to commit to working on a task for just five minutes. {6} started, people often find it easier to carry on than they expected. Another is to forgive yourself for past delays: one study found that students {7} were kinder to themselves after putting off revision procrastinated less {8} the next exam.`,example:{a:"from"},gaps:[{a:["to"],x:"contrary to (popular belief) = despite what most people think."},{a:["anything"],x:"if anything = if there is any difference, it is the opposite."},{a:["less"],x:"makes success less likely – a comparative."},{a:["than"],x:"rather than = instead of."},{a:["by"],x:"accompanied by something."},{a:["once"],x:"Once started = after they have started."},{a:["who","that"],x:"A relative pronoun for people: students who were kinder to themselves."},{a:["before","for"],x:"before (or for) the next exam."}]},3:{title:"Rainforests of the sea",text:`Coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, yet they are {0} rich in life,
supporting around a quarter of all marine species. Their {1} is often
compared to that of tropical rainforests. Reefs also provide {2} protection
for coastlines, absorbing the energy of waves during storms. Sadly, they are {3}
under threat. When the water becomes too warm, corals expel the tiny algae that give them
colour and food – a process known as bleaching – and this leaves them {4}
against disease. Many scientists predict a {5} decline in the world's reefs
within decades. There are, however, grounds for {6}. Researchers are breeding
corals that are more {7} of heat, and some reefs have shown a remarkable
capacity for {8} when local pressures such as overfishing are removed.`,example:{w:"EXTRAORDINARY",a:"extraordinarily"},gaps:[{w:"DIVERSE",a:["diversity"],x:"Noun after ‘their’: diversity = the range of different kinds."},{w:"VALUE",a:["invaluable"],x:"Adjective before a noun: invaluable = extremely useful (not the opposite of valuable)."},{w:"INCREASE",a:["increasingly"],x:"Adverb before ‘under threat’: more and more."},{w:"DEFENCE",a:["defenceless","defenseless"],x:"Adjective: defenceless = unable to protect itself."},{w:"SIGNIFY",a:["significant"],x:"Adjective before a noun: a significant decline."},{w:"OPTIMIST",a:["optimism"],x:"Noun after ‘grounds for’: optimism."},{w:"TOLERATE",a:["tolerant"],x:"Adjective after ‘more’: tolerant of heat."},{w:"RECOVER",a:["recovery"],x:"Noun after ‘capacity for’: recovery."}]},4:{example:{s1:"I haven't seen my cousin since his wedding.",key:"LAST",pre:"The",ans:"last time I saw my cousin was",post:"at his wedding."},items:[{s1:"It's possible that the storm delayed the train.",key:"MAY",pre:"The train",post:"by the storm.",a:[["may have been"],["delayed","held up"]],x:"Possibility in the past, passive: may have been + past participle."},{s1:"The film was so boring that we left halfway through.",key:"SUCH",pre:"It",post:"that we left halfway through.",a:[["was such"],["a boring film","a dull film"]],x:"such + a + adjective + noun + that."},{s1:"I'd rather you didn't mention this to anyone.",key:"PREFER",pre:"I",post:"this to anyone.",a:[["would prefer you","'d prefer you"],["not to mention"]],x:"would prefer somebody not to do something."},{s1:"They didn't tell us about the change of plan until the last minute.",key:"INFORMED",pre:"We",post:"the change of plan until the last minute.",a:[["were not informed","weren't informed"],["about","of"]],x:"Passive: were not informed about/of something."},{s1:"The committee is expected to announce its decision next week.",key:"DUE",pre:"The committee",post:"its decision next week.",a:[["is due"],["to announce","to make public"]],x:"be due to do something = be expected or planned to do it."},{s1:"I'm sure she didn't mean to offend you.",key:"INTENTION",pre:"I'm sure she",post:"you.",a:[["had no intention"],["of offending","of upsetting","of insulting"]],x:"have no intention of + -ing = not mean to do something."}]},5:{title:"A matter of taste",intro:"an article about a scientist who studies how we perceive flavour",paras:["Ask most people how many tastes the human tongue can detect and they will confidently answer ‘four’: sweet, sour, salty and bitter. Some will add a fifth, umami, the savoury note found in broth and aged cheese. Dr Lena Vogt, who has spent twenty years studying how we perceive food, smiles politely at such answers. ‘The tongue is only the beginning of the story,’ she says. ‘Most of what we call taste is actually smell, and a surprising amount of it happens before the food is anywhere near our mouths.’","Vogt's interest in the subject began, unusually, with a loss. In her mid-twenties a severe viral infection left her unable to smell for almost a year. Food became, as she puts it, ‘a matter of texture and temperature’. Strawberries and tomatoes were indistinguishable; coffee was merely hot and bitter. ‘It was deeply depressing,’ she recalls, ‘but it also made me curious. I wanted to understand exactly what I had lost.’","Her research has since shown just how easily our judgements of flavour can be manipulated. In one experiment, volunteers rated the same wine as noticeably more expensive-tasting when it was served in a heavy glass. In another, crisps were judged fresher and crunchier when the sound of the crunch was amplified through headphones. ‘People are often embarrassed by these results,’ Vogt says. ‘They feel they've been tricked. But the brain is doing exactly what it is supposed to do – combining every available clue to make a quick judgement.’","Food manufacturers have, unsurprisingly, taken a keen interest in such findings. Vogt has advised several companies, and she is candid about the ethical tensions this creates. Some of her work has helped firms to reduce the sugar and salt in their products without customers noticing the difference – an outcome she regards as unambiguously positive. She is less comfortable, however, with requests to make cheap ingredients seem more luxurious. ‘There's a line between improving food and misleading people,’ she says, ‘and it isn't always obvious where it lies.’","Perhaps the most practical implication of her work concerns the elderly. Our sense of smell declines with age, often so gradually that people fail to notice. The consequences can be serious: older people may lose interest in food, eat less and become malnourished, or add excessive salt in an attempt to recover lost flavour. Vogt is working with care homes to design meals that compensate through stronger aromas, contrasting textures and vivid colours. Early results are encouraging, with residents eating more and reporting greater enjoyment of their meals.","Does knowing so much spoil her own enjoyment of food? Vogt laughs. ‘People expect me to analyse every mouthful, but I'm as easily seduced as anyone by a beautifully presented plate. If anything, understanding the mechanisms has made me more grateful. I spent a year without flavour. I don't take a single meal for granted.’"],qs:[{q:"In the first paragraph, what is Vogt's attitude to the common view of taste?",o:["She believes it underestimates the tongue.","She thinks it is surprisingly accurate.","She is amused that people forget about umami.","She regards it as incomplete."],a:3,x:"‘The tongue is only the beginning of the story… Most of what we call taste is actually smell.’"},{q:"What does the writer say about Vogt's loss of smell?",o:["It affected her perception of only certain foods.","It left her permanently unable to enjoy coffee.","It prompted her interest in how flavour works.","It made her decide to train as a doctor."],a:2,x:"‘It also made me curious. I wanted to understand exactly what I had lost.’ It lasted ‘almost a year’, so it wasn't permanent."},{q:"What point does Vogt make about the experiments in the third paragraph?",o:["Such experiments are unfair to the people who take part.","The volunteers were less observant than expected.","Sound matters more than appearance in judging food.","The results reflect a normal function of the brain."],a:3,x:"‘The brain is doing exactly what it is supposed to do – combining every available clue.’"},{q:"How does Vogt feel about her work for food companies?",o:["She believes all of it benefits consumers.","She refuses to work for large manufacturers.","She thinks customers should be told about every change.","She has reservations about some of what she is asked to do."],a:3,x:"Reducing sugar is ‘unambiguously positive’, but she is ‘less comfortable’ with making cheap ingredients seem luxurious."},{q:"According to the fifth paragraph, why is the decline in smell among older people a concern?",o:["It is usually a sign of a more serious illness.","It can lead to unhealthy eating habits.","It happens too suddenly for people to adapt.","It makes care homes more expensive to run."],a:1,x:"They may ‘eat less and become malnourished, or add excessive salt’. It happens gradually, not suddenly."},{q:"In the final paragraph, Vogt suggests that her knowledge",o:["prevents her from enjoying meals fully.","makes her critical of how food is presented.","has made her appreciate food more.","has changed what she chooses to eat."],a:2,x:"‘Understanding the mechanisms has made me more grateful… I don't take a single meal for granted.’"}]},6:{title:"Should museums give objects back?",intro:"four extracts in which experts discuss whether museums should return objects to their countries of origin",who:"experts",stem:"Which expert…",sections:[{name:"Grace Okafor",text:["The argument for returning objects taken during the colonial era is, to my mind, overwhelming. Many were removed by force or under conditions that made genuine consent impossible, and their continued display in European capitals sends a troubling message about who owns history. Critics claim that the countries these objects came from lack the facilities to look after them, but this is increasingly untrue: new museums in Lagos, Dakar and elsewhere meet the highest international standards. Nor does return have to mean the end of collaboration. Loans, joint exhibitions and shared research can all continue."]},{name:"Henrik Lund",text:["I have considerable sympathy with calls for restitution, but I am wary of treating it as a simple matter of right and wrong. The histories of many objects are genuinely complicated: some were sold legitimately, others passed through several owners before reaching a museum. Each case deserves careful research rather than a blanket policy, and in the end it should be the communities the objects came from, not the museums, who have the final say. My greater concern is that the debate has become so polarised that institutions are reluctant to discuss their collections openly, for fear of controversy. That serves no one."]},{name:"Amara Diallo",text:["Museums in Europe often argue that their collections belong to a universal heritage, accessible to visitors from all over the world. It is a noble idea, but it rings rather hollow when the people whose ancestors made these objects would need a visa and a considerable sum of money to see them. I don't believe every object must go back; some communities have told me they are content for certain pieces to stay where they are, provided their origins are properly acknowledged. Nor am I convinced that European audiences would lose much: most visitors spend a few seconds in front of each object, and good replicas and digital records could serve them just as well."]},{name:"Tom Ashworth",text:["The case for return is strongest where there is clear evidence of theft, and in such cases museums should act quickly. But I worry that returning objects on a large scale could empty museums of the very things that inspire an interest in other cultures. A child in Manchester who encounters a Benin bronze may go on to study West African history. The solution, surely, lies in partnership: shared ownership, rotating exhibitions and digital access, so that objects can be seen and studied wherever people live."]}],qs:[{q:"takes a different view from the others on whether each object should be considered individually?",a:0,x:"Okafor finds the argument for return ‘overwhelming’ in general. Lund wants ‘careful research’ on each case, Diallo says not every object must go back, and Ashworth wants return where there is ‘clear evidence of theft’."},{q:"shares Diallo's view on who should decide what happens to objects?",a:1,x:"Lund: ‘it should be the communities the objects came from… who have the final say’. Diallo: communities choose whether pieces stay."},{q:"has a different opinion from Ashworth about how returning objects would affect audiences in Europe?",a:2,x:"Ashworth fears museums would be emptied of inspiring objects; Diallo doubts European audiences ‘would lose much’."},{q:"shares Okafor's view that museums could continue to work together after objects are returned?",a:3,x:"Okafor: ‘Loans, joint exhibitions and shared research can all continue.’ Ashworth: ‘partnership: shared ownership, rotating exhibitions’."}]},7:{title:"One breath",intro:"an article in which a journalist describes learning to free-dive",paragraphs:!0,paras:["The instructor's first question surprised me. I had expected her to ask how long I could hold my breath, or whether I was a strong swimmer. Instead, Marta asked me to describe the last time I had felt completely calm. I thought about it for a long time and couldn't come up with an answer.","{1}","That, it turns out, is the whole point. Free-diving – descending into deep water on a single breath, without tanks – is less a test of lung capacity than of the mind's ability to switch off. The body, Marta explained, has an astonishing ability to conserve oxygen, but it only works properly if you stop fighting it.","{2}","On the second morning we moved from the pool to the sea. The water off the island was so clear that I could see the sandy bottom fifteen metres below, and my first instinct was to panic. Marta attached a rope to a buoy and told me to pull myself down it slowly, one hand after another, and to come back up the moment I felt uncomfortable.","{3}","By the end of the week I could reach twenty metres. This is nothing by the standards of competitive free-divers, some of whom descend beyond a hundred, but for me it felt like flying. What I remember most vividly is not the depth itself but the silence down there, and the extraordinary sense of being entirely present.","{4}","Free-diving has an image problem, and not without reason. Deaths occur every year, usually among people who dive alone or push beyond their limits. Marta was uncompromising about safety: nobody ever went into the water without a partner watching from the surface, and she ended one session early because a student had been out drinking the night before.","{5}","Back at home, I have not become a devoted free-diver. I live two hundred miles from the sea, and my local pool has a strict rule against holding your breath underwater. But something of that week has stayed with me.","{6}"],options:["The first few times, I barely got below five metres before turning back. But gradually, as I learnt to relax my shoulders and ignore the growing pressure in my ears, the rope became less a lifeline than a guide. On the fourth day I touched the sand at the bottom and, for a few seconds, simply stayed there.","When I find myself stuck in traffic or lying awake at three in the morning, I sometimes close my eyes and slow my breathing the way Marta taught me. Within a minute or two, the tension begins to ease. It is, I suppose, the answer to her first question.","I told Marta this, rather sheepishly. She simply nodded, as if it were the answer she had been expecting, and said that by the end of the week I would have one.","Competitive free-diving is now governed by an international federation, which sets strict rules for record attempts and requires medical checks for all competitors.","Such caution can seem excessive, but I came to see it as part of the discipline. The same focus that allows a diver to descend calmly also demands honesty about one's own limits – and the humility to stop.","To demonstrate, she had us lie face down in the shallow end of the pool, breathing through a snorkel and concentrating on nothing but the rhythm of our breath. Then, on her signal, we held it. My first attempt lasted forty seconds before my body demanded air. By the end of the session, lying perfectly still, I had managed two and a half minutes.","It is a feeling that experienced divers describe in almost spiritual terms. Several of Marta's former students, she told me, had taken up the sport after years of anxiety, and found in it a kind of calm that nothing else had given them."],key:[2,5,0,6,4,1],x:["The writer couldn't answer; C: ‘I told Marta this, rather sheepishly’ – and ‘That, it turns out, is the whole point’ follows.","‘Stop fighting it’ – F: ‘To demonstrate, she had us lie face down in the shallow end of the pool’, before they move ‘from the pool to the sea’.","After the rope instructions, A: ‘The first few times, I barely got below five metres’, leading to ‘By the end of the week I could reach twenty metres’.","‘The extraordinary sense of being entirely present’ – G: ‘It is a feeling that experienced divers describe in almost spiritual terms.’","After the strict safety rules, E: ‘Such caution can seem excessive’.","‘Something of that week has stayed with me’ – B explains what, and ends with ‘the answer to her first question’. D (the federation) doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"How I write",intro:"an article in which four novelists describe how they work",who:"novelists",stem:"Which novelist…",sections:[{name:"Isabel Kerr",text:["I write every morning from six until ten, before the rest of the house is awake. I learnt early on that inspiration is unreliable; if I waited for it, I'd produce one book a decade. The discipline matters more than the mood. I plan very little in advance – I usually know the opening scene and roughly how the story ends, but everything in between surprises me. My first drafts are chaotic, and I rewrite each novel at least four times. The hardest part isn't writing but cutting: I once deleted a hundred pages I'd spent a year on, and the book was far better for it. I don't show anyone my work until it's finished, not even my husband."]},{name:"Daniel Mensah",text:["Before I write a word, I spend months planning. I have notebooks full of character histories, maps of imaginary towns and timelines of events that never appear in the finished book. Some writers find that approach stifling, but for me it's liberating – once I know the structure, I can concentrate on the sentences. I write in cafés, surrounded by chatter; silence makes me anxious. My editor is my most important reader. She's brutally honest, and I've learnt to trust her judgement more than my own, even when it stings. My biggest challenge is endings: I rewrote the last chapter of my latest novel eleven times."]},{name:"Priya Nair",text:["I came to fiction late, after twenty years as a journalist, and I think that shows in how I work. I'm used to deadlines, so I set myself a target of a thousand words a day, and I rarely miss it. Journalism also taught me to listen: most of my characters begin with something I overheard on a bus or in a queue. I research obsessively – for my last book, which is set in a hospital, I spent three months following nurses at work. What I miss about journalism is the immediate response from readers. With a novel, you work alone for two years and then wait anxiously to find out whether anyone cares."]},{name:"Owen Hartley",text:["I have no routine at all. Some weeks I write every day; some months I write nothing. I used to feel guilty about this, but I've accepted that I work in bursts, and the gaps are when the ideas grow. I always write the first draft by hand, which slows me down in a useful way. When I'm stuck, I go for long walks – I've solved more plot problems on the cliffs near my house than at my desk. I read my work aloud to my teenage daughter, who is my toughest critic; if she gets bored, I know the scene has to go. My advice to new writers? Finish things. A flawed finished book teaches you more than a perfect unfinished one."]}],qs:[{q:"has rewritten the ending of a book many times?",a:1,x:"Mensah: ‘I rewrote the last chapter of my latest novel eleven times.’"},{q:"gets ideas for characters from conversations they happen to hear?",a:2,x:"Nair: ‘most of my characters begin with something I overheard on a bus or in a queue.’"},{q:"does not have a regular writing timetable?",a:3,x:"Hartley: ‘I have no routine at all.’"},{q:"does not let anyone see a book until it is complete?",a:0,x:"Kerr: ‘I don't show anyone my work until it's finished.’"},{q:"finds that physical exercise helps them solve problems in a story?",a:3,x:"Hartley: ‘I've solved more plot problems on the cliffs… than at my desk.’"},{q:"prefers to work somewhere noisy?",a:1,x:"Mensah: ‘I write in cafés, surrounded by chatter; silence makes me anxious.’"},{q:"misses getting a quick reaction to their work?",a:2,x:"Nair: ‘What I miss about journalism is the immediate response from readers.’"},{q:"improved a book by removing a large part of it?",a:0,x:"Kerr: ‘I once deleted a hundred pages… and the book was far better for it.’"},{q:"values the honest opinion of a professional colleague?",a:1,x:"Mensah's editor is ‘brutally honest’, and he trusts her judgement."},{q:"sets a daily target for how much they write?",a:2,x:"Nair: ‘a target of a thousand words a day’. Kerr writes at set hours, not a set amount."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t4",title:"Test 4",topics:"Wolves return · The four-day week · The placebo effect · Building with earth · Teenagers and social media · A night at the bakery · Learning a language later in life",parts:{1:{title:"The wolf returns",text:`After an absence of more than a century, wolves are making a remarkable {0} across much of Europe. Legal protection, combined with the abandonment of farmland, has allowed populations to recover at a {1} that has surprised even conservationists. Wolves have now been {2} in almost every country on the continent, including densely populated ones such as the Netherlands and Belgium.

Not everyone is pleased to see them. Sheep farmers, in particular, have borne the {3} of the losses, and in some regions attacks on livestock have risen sharply. Farmers argue that the compensation they receive rarely reflects the true cost, and that living under the {4} of attack takes a heavy toll on their mental health. Conservationists, for their part, point out that simple measures – electric fences, guard dogs and shepherds – can {5} losses dramatically.

The challenge, then, is to find ways for people and wolves to {6} side by side. Where farmers have been fully {7} in decisions and given practical support, tensions have eased considerably. Where they feel that policies have been imposed from above, the wolf has become a {8} for a much wider sense of rural grievance.`,example:{o:["rebirth","comeback","renewal","restoration"],a:1},gaps:[{o:["velocity","tempo","rate","degree"],a:2,x:"at a rate that… = at a speed that. ‘Velocity’ is technical and ‘tempo’ is for music."},{o:["watched","sighted","glanced","viewed"],a:1,x:"be sighted = be seen, especially something rare or wild."},{o:["strain","pressure","force","brunt"],a:3,x:"bear the brunt of something = suffer the worst of it."},{o:["threat","hazard","risk","danger"],a:0,x:"live under the threat of something – a fixed phrase."},{o:["shrink","fall","cut","drop"],a:2,x:"cut losses = reduce them. ‘Fall’ has no object, and ‘drop’ and ‘shrink’ don't collocate with losses here."},{o:["cooperate","coexist","collaborate","cohabit"],a:1,x:"coexist = live together in the same place. ‘Cohabit’ is used for couples."},{o:["occupied","concerned","absorbed","involved"],a:3,x:"be involved in decisions = take part in making them."},{o:["middle","focus","centre","point"],a:1,x:"a focus for something = the thing that feelings or attention gather around."}]},2:{title:"A shorter week",text:`The idea of a four-day working week has moved from the fringes of political debate {0} the mainstream in a remarkably short time. In a series of recent trials, companies reduced their employees' hours {1} cutting their pay, on the condition that productivity remained the same. The results were striking. Not {2} did most firms report no drop in output, but staff were less stressed, took fewer sick days and were less likely to leave.

Sceptics argue that such trials tell us little, {3} that the companies involved volunteered to take part and were therefore likely to be enthusiastic. There is also the question of {4} the model could work in sectors such as healthcare or retail, where someone has to be present regardless of the day. {5} these objections, a surprising number of the companies involved have chosen to keep the shorter week. As one manager put it, ‘Once people have had a taste of it, there's no going back.’

{6} this will ultimately lead to a change in the law remains to be seen, but the debate is unlikely to go {7} anytime soon. For many workers, the question is no longer whether it is possible, but {8} long they will have to wait.`,example:{a:"to"},gaps:[{a:["without"],x:"reduce hours without cutting pay."},{a:["only"],x:"Not only did… but (also)… – inversion after ‘not only’."},{a:["given","considering"],x:"given that = considering the fact that."},{a:["whether","how"],x:"the question of whether/how something could work."},{a:["despite"],x:"Despite + noun = although there are…"},{a:["whether"],x:"Whether… remains to be seen = nobody knows yet if…"},{a:["away"],x:"go away = disappear."},{a:["how"],x:"how long they will have to wait."}]},3:{title:"The power of belief",text:`The placebo effect – the improvement in symptoms that some patients report after taking
a treatment with no active ingredients – is one of the most {0} phenomena in medicine.
{1} dismissed as imaginary, it is now known to produce measurable changes
in the brain. Remarkably, placebos can remain {2} even when patients
are told that the pills contain nothing. The {3} of the effect seems to depend
partly on the ritual of treatment: injections tend to work better than pills, and
{4} coloured tablets better than plain ones. There is a darker side, too.
In the ‘nocebo’ effect, patients suffer {5} side effects simply because
they have been warned about them, which may explain why some people
{6} stop taking useful medication. Researchers believe that a better
{7} of these effects could help doctors to communicate more {8}.`,example:{w:"INTRIGUE",a:"intriguing"},gaps:[{w:"ORIGIN",a:["originally"],x:"Adverb: originally dismissed = dismissed at first."},{w:"EFFECT",a:["effective"],x:"Adjective after ‘remain’: remain effective."},{w:"STRONG",a:["strength"],x:"Noun after ‘the’: the strength of the effect."},{w:"BRIGHT",a:["brightly"],x:"Adverb before a participle adjective: brightly coloured."},{w:"PLEASANT",a:["unpleasant"],x:"Negative adjective: unpleasant side effects."},{w:"NECESSARY",a:["unnecessarily"],x:"Negative adverb: stop taking medication without a good reason."},{w:"UNDERSTAND",a:["understanding"],x:"Noun after ‘a better’: a better understanding of."},{w:"THOUGHT",a:["thoughtfully"],x:"Adverb after ‘communicate more’: thoughtfully = carefully, with consideration."}]},4:{example:{s1:"It was the first time I had ever tried sushi.",key:"NEVER",pre:"I",ans:"had never tried sushi",post:"before."},items:[{s1:"People believe the painting was stolen in 1985.",key:"THOUGHT",pre:"The painting",post:"in 1985.",a:[["is thought"],["to have been stolen","to have been taken"]],x:"Impersonal passive about the past: is thought + to have been + past participle."},{s1:"I only realised I had left my passport at home when I reached the airport.",key:"UNTIL",pre:"It was not",post:"that I realised I had left my passport at home.",a:[["until i reached","until i got to","until i arrived at"],["the airport"]],x:"Cleft sentence: It was not until… that…"},{s1:"The manager said it was my fault that the order was late.",key:"BLAMED",pre:"The manager",post:"the late order.",a:[["blamed me"],["for"]],x:"blame somebody for something."},{s1:"You won't get a refund unless you keep the receipt.",key:"PROVIDED",pre:"You will get a refund",post:"the receipt.",a:[["provided that you","provided you"],["keep","hold on to","hang on to"]],x:"provided (that) = only if."},{s1:"Julia finds it hard to concentrate when there is loud music.",key:"DIFFICULTY",pre:"Julia",post:"when there is loud music.",a:[["has difficulty","has great difficulty"],["concentrating","in concentrating"]],x:"have difficulty (in) + -ing."},{s1:"It was a mistake for them not to book a table in advance.",key:"SHOULD",pre:"They",post:"a table in advance.",a:[["should have"],["booked"]],x:"Criticism of a past action: should have + past participle (they didn't book, and that was a mistake)."}]},5:{title:"Building with earth",intro:"an article about an architect who builds houses from earth",paras:["When Anna Ferreira announced, fifteen years ago, that she intended to build houses out of mud, several of her former colleagues assumed she was joking. Ferreira had spent a decade at a prestigious firm designing glass-and-steel office towers, and her sudden interest in one of the world's oldest building materials struck many as a kind of professional crisis. ‘Perhaps it was,’ she concedes. ‘But it was a productive one.’","Earth, she points out, is hardly an exotic choice. Roughly a third of the world's population still lives in buildings made from it, and some earthen structures have stood for more than a thousand years. What changed her mind was a visit to a village in northern Portugal, where she stayed in a farmhouse built by her great-grandfather. ‘It was forty degrees outside and pleasantly cool inside, without any air conditioning,’ she recalls. ‘I remember thinking: why have we forgotten how to do this?’","The technique she uses, known as rammed earth, involves compressing a damp mixture of soil, sand and gravel between wooden frames, layer by layer, until it forms walls as hard as stone. The process is slow and labour-intensive, which makes it more expensive than conventional construction in countries where labour is costly. Ferreira is frank about this. ‘People imagine that because the material is cheap, the building must be,’ she says. ‘In Europe, that simply isn't the case – at least not yet.’","Where earth wins, she argues, is over the lifetime of a building. Concrete production accounts for a significant share of global carbon emissions, whereas earth can often be dug from the building site itself. Earthen walls regulate humidity naturally and store heat during the day, releasing it slowly at night, which can dramatically reduce energy bills. And when the building eventually comes to the end of its life, the walls can simply return to the ground.","Not all of her projects have gone smoothly. An early commission, a school in a rainy coastal town, developed damp patches within two winters, and Ferreira had to redesign the roof at her own expense. ‘It was humiliating,’ she admits, ‘but it taught me that earth has to be understood on its own terms. You can't simply swap it into a design meant for concrete and hope for the best.’","Interest in her work has grown enormously, particularly among younger architects. Ferreira is cautiously encouraged, though she worries that earth could become a fashionable label rather than a genuine shift in practice. ‘I'd rather see a hundred ordinary houses built well,’ she says, ‘than one spectacular building that ends up on magazine covers.’"],qs:[{q:"How does Ferreira now view her decision to leave her previous job?",o:["She thinks her colleagues misunderstood her motives.","She believes it was an entirely rational choice.","She regrets the way she handled it.","She accepts it may have been a crisis, but a useful one."],a:3,x:"‘Perhaps it was,’ she concedes. ‘But it was a productive one.’"},{q:"What prompted Ferreira's interest in building with earth?",o:["research into traditional buildings around the world","the discovery that earth is cheap to buy","a request from a member of her family","experiencing how comfortable an earthen house was in hot weather"],a:3,x:"In her great-grandfather's farmhouse it was ‘forty degrees outside and pleasantly cool inside’."},{q:"What does Ferreira say about the cost of rammed earth in Europe?",o:["It depends mainly on the type of soil used.","It is lower than most people expect.","It is higher than that of conventional building.","It is certain to fall dramatically soon."],a:2,x:"Labour makes it ‘more expensive than conventional construction’; people wrongly assume it must be cheap."},{q:"In the fourth paragraph, the writer's main point is that earthen buildings",o:["are easier to demolish than other buildings.","need less maintenance than people assume.","are more attractive than concrete ones.","have environmental and practical advantages over time."],a:3,x:"‘Where earth wins… is over the lifetime of a building’: lower emissions, natural humidity control, lower bills."},{q:"What did Ferreira learn from the problems with the school?",o:["that clients should share the cost of mistakes","that designs must be adapted to the material","that she should never work in wet climates","that earth is unsuitable for public buildings"],a:1,x:"‘You can't simply swap it into a design meant for concrete and hope for the best.’"},{q:"In the final paragraph, what concern does Ferreira express?",o:["that younger architects lack the necessary skills","that magazines misrepresent her work","that building with earth may become a trend rather than a real change","that ordinary houses are rarely built well"],a:2,x:"She worries earth ‘could become a fashionable label rather than a genuine shift in practice’."}]},6:{title:"Teenagers and social media",intro:"four extracts in which psychologists discuss the effects of social media on teenagers",who:"psychologists",stem:"Which psychologist…",sections:[{name:"Dr Ruth Palmer",text:["The alarm about teenagers and social media is, in my view, somewhat overblown. The large studies that grab headlines typically find only a small association between screen time and wellbeing – smaller, in one analysis, than the link between wellbeing and eating potatoes. That is not to say there are no risks: for a minority of vulnerable young people, particular kinds of content can do real harm. But blanket restrictions punish the many for the difficulties of the few, and they cut teenagers off from friendships that, for many, are a genuine source of support."]},{name:"Prof. Samuel Adeyemi",text:["It is true that the overall statistical effects are modest, but averages can hide a great deal. When we look specifically at teenage girls who spend several hours a day on image-based platforms, the picture becomes much more worrying, with clear links to anxiety and poor body image. I don't favour outright bans, which rarely work, but the companies responsible have been allowed to design products that exploit adolescent psychology with remarkably little oversight. Regulation is long overdue."]},{name:"Dr Ines Moreau",text:["Much of the debate focuses on how much time young people spend online, but that is the wrong question. An hour spent chatting with friends is very different from an hour spent comparing yourself to strangers. In my clinical work, what matters most is what teenagers are doing and how it makes them feel. Parents who simply set time limits often miss this entirely. Far more useful are regular, non-judgemental conversations about online experiences – and I would certainly welcome tighter rules on how platforms promote content to children."]},{name:"Dr Paul Whitfield",text:["My concern is less with the content than with what social media displaces. Sleep, above all: teenagers who keep their phones in their bedrooms routinely lose an hour or more of sleep a night, and the consequences for mood and concentration are well documented. Exercise and face-to-face contact suffer too. The platforms themselves are not the main problem, and I would not support stricter regulation of them. What we need are clear household habits – phones out of bedrooms at night, screen-free meals – which parents can introduce tomorrow."]}],qs:[{q:"takes a different view from the others on whether time spent online is the key issue?",a:2,x:"Moreau: ‘that is the wrong question’ – what teenagers do matters more. The others all treat time online as central (screen time, several hours a day, lost sleep)."},{q:"shares Adeyemi's view that social media companies should face stricter rules?",a:2,x:"Moreau: ‘I would certainly welcome tighter rules on how platforms promote content.’ Whitfield rejects stricter regulation."},{q:"shares Palmer's view that only certain young people are seriously affected?",a:1,x:"Adeyemi: averages hide effects on a specific group – ‘teenage girls who spend several hours a day on image-based platforms’."},{q:"has a different opinion from Adeyemi about the main source of harm?",a:3,x:"Whitfield: ‘My concern is less with the content than with what social media displaces’ – sleep, exercise, contact. Adeyemi blames the design and content of platforms."}]},7:{title:"The night shift",intro:"an article in which a journalist spends a night working in a bakery",paragraphs:!0,paras:["At ten o'clock on a Tuesday night, when most of the town is thinking about bed, the lights come on at Harlow's Bakery. By the time the first customers arrive at seven, the shelves will hold four hundred loaves, two hundred croissants and a mountain of cinnamon buns – every one of them shaped by hand. I had come to find out what it takes.","{1}","Tom Harlow is the third generation of his family to run the bakery. He is a quiet man in his fifties with forearms like a rower's, and he greeted me with a handshake and an apron. ‘You'll be tired by two,’ he said. ‘Everyone is, the first time.’","{2}","The first job was to divide and weigh the dough that had been rising since the afternoon. Tom did it by eye, cutting pieces within a gram or two of the target weight; I did it with the scales and was still slower. Then came the shaping, which is where the real skill lies.","{3}","By half past two, as Tom had predicted, I was struggling. The heat from the ovens was relentless, my feet ached and the radio – tuned permanently to a station that played nothing but eighties pop – had begun to feel like a form of punishment. Tom, meanwhile, moved at exactly the same pace he had at ten.","{4}","The economics of a small bakery are unforgiving. Tom's flour costs have nearly doubled in three years, and he competes with supermarkets that sell loaves for a fraction of his price. Several independent bakeries in the area have closed. Yet Harlow's survives, and on Saturdays there is a queue down the street.","{5}","At six, the first light appeared in the sky and the shop filled with the smell of fresh bread. Tom's daughter arrived to open up, and I was sent home with a loaf still warm from the oven.","{6}"],options:["Tom puts this down to something no supermarket can offer. ‘People can taste the difference,’ he says, ‘and they like knowing who made their bread.’ He also supplies three local cafés, which guarantees a steady income through the quiet months.","The answer, it turned out, was mostly stamina – and a willingness to work while everyone else is asleep. Harlow's has baked through the night, six nights a week, for almost eighty years.","Bread-making machines have become increasingly popular in home kitchens, and many people now bake their own bread at weekends.","Tom's hands moved with a speed that made it look effortless: a twist, a fold, a quick roll across the bench, and a perfect loaf appeared. Mine emerged lopsided, and Maria quietly reshaped several of them when she thought I wasn't looking.","I slept until two in the afternoon. When I woke, I ate half the loaf with butter, standing at the kitchen counter – and understood, for the first time, why people queue.","His team that night consisted of two others: Maria, who has worked alongside him for twenty years, and a nineteen-year-old apprentice named Kieran, who looked almost as nervous as I felt.","When I asked how he kept going, he shrugged. ‘You stop noticing,’ he said. ‘After thirty years, your body just knows what time it is.’ Kieran, I noticed, was yawning even more than I was."],key:[1,5,3,6,0,4],x:["‘I had come to find out what it takes’ – B: ‘The answer, it turned out, was mostly stamina’.","After Tom's welcome, F introduces the rest of the team, including Maria and Kieran, who appear later.","‘The shaping, which is where the real skill lies’ – D shows Tom's skill, and Maria reshaping the writer's loaves.","Tom moved at the same pace – G: ‘When I asked how he kept going, he shrugged.’","‘Yet Harlow's survives… a queue’ – A: ‘Tom puts this down to something no supermarket can offer.’","Sent home with a warm loaf – E: ‘When I woke, I ate half the loaf.’ C (home bread machines) doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"Never too late",intro:"an article in which four people describe learning a new language as adults",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Marta, Japanese",text:["I started learning Japanese at fifty-two, after my son moved to Osaka and married a Japanese woman. I wanted to be able to talk to my grandchildren. The writing system nearly defeated me – three scripts, one of them with thousands of characters – and for the first year I seriously considered giving up. What kept me going was a weekly video call with my daughter-in-law's mother, who knows no English. We muddle through with gestures and a dictionary, and we've become genuine friends. My grammar is still poor, but I've stopped worrying about mistakes."]},{name:"Colin, Spanish",text:["I'd always told myself I had no talent for languages – I failed French at school. When I retired to Spain, I realised I couldn't spend the rest of my life pointing at things. I took an intensive course, four hours a day for three months, and it was exhausting but effective. The breakthrough came when I joined the village choir: singing forced me to get the pronunciation right, and afterwards everyone went to the bar, where I had no choice but to talk. I now see that my old belief about having no talent was simply an excuse."]},{name:"Leila, German",text:["I learnt German for my job, when my company moved me to Munich at thirty-eight. I was surprised how much my age helped rather than hindered. As a teenager I'd learnt vocabulary by heart and forgotten it just as quickly; as an adult I understood how the grammar worked and could see the patterns. Apps were useless for me – I need a teacher to explain things. The hardest part was the emotional side: in meetings I felt like a less intelligent version of myself, unable to make jokes or express subtle opinions. That feeling has faded, but it took two years."]},{name:"Raj, Italian",text:["I took up Italian purely for pleasure, after falling in love with Italian opera. I've never lived in Italy and probably never will. I study for twenty minutes every morning with an app and read a page of an Italian novel before bed, looking up every word I don't know. Progress has been slow, and I'm fine with that – there's no deadline. My biggest surprise has been how much the language has changed the way I listen to music. I now understand the jokes and wordplay in the operas that I'd been missing for years."]}],qs:[{q:"used to believe they were not capable of learning a language?",a:1,x:"Colin: ‘I'd always told myself I had no talent for languages.’"},{q:"found that being older made some aspects of learning easier?",a:2,x:"Leila: ‘I was surprised how much my age helped rather than hindered.’"},{q:"has made a close friend through the language?",a:0,x:"Marta and her daughter-in-law's mother have ‘become genuine friends’."},{q:"is under no pressure to make quick progress?",a:3,x:"Raj: ‘Progress has been slow, and I'm fine with that – there's no deadline.’"},{q:"found that a group activity improved their speaking?",a:1,x:"Colin: the village choir – and the bar afterwards."},{q:"felt unable to show their true personality in the language?",a:2,x:"Leila: ‘I felt like a less intelligent version of myself, unable to make jokes.’"},{q:"nearly gave up because of the written form of the language?",a:0,x:"Marta: ‘The writing system nearly defeated me… I seriously considered giving up.’"},{q:"uses technology as their main way of studying?",a:3,x:"Raj studies ‘every morning with an app’. Leila found apps useless."},{q:"is no longer worried about making mistakes?",a:0,x:"Marta: ‘I've stopped worrying about mistakes.’"},{q:"prefers learning with a teacher to using apps?",a:2,x:"Leila: ‘Apps were useless for me – I need a teacher to explain things.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t5",title:"Test 5",topics:"The slow food movement · Handwriting · Glaciers · A voice actor · Tourism in historic cities · Restoring a piano · Volunteers abroad",parts:{1:{title:"Slow food",text:`The slow food movement began in Italy in 1986 as a {0} against the opening of a fast-food restaurant near Rome's Spanish Steps. What started as a local complaint has since grown into an international organisation with members in more than 150 countries. Its aim is to {1} traditional ways of growing, cooking and eating food that are in danger of disappearing.

At the {2} of the movement lies the belief that food should be good, clean and fair: good to eat, produced without harming the environment, and {3} at a price that gives farmers a decent living. Critics have sometimes {4} it of being a hobby for the wealthy, since locally produced food often costs more than supermarket alternatives. Supporters reply that the true cost of cheap food is simply hidden – in damaged soil, polluted rivers and struggling rural communities.

Whatever one's view, the movement has undoubtedly {5} a nerve. Farmers' markets have multiplied, and many consumers now take a keen {6} in where their food comes from. Whether this will prove to be a lasting change or a passing {7} remains to be seen, but for now, at least, the idea shows no {8} of losing its appeal.`,example:{o:["complaint","protest","dispute","strike"],a:1},gaps:[{o:["reserve","observe","preserve","deserve"],a:2,x:"preserve traditions = keep them from disappearing."},{o:["inside","middle","depth","heart"],a:3,x:"At the heart of something lies… – a fixed expression."},{o:["dealt","sold","traded","bargained"],a:1,x:"sold at a price."},{o:["blamed","criticised","accused","charged"],a:2,x:"accuse somebody of something. ‘Blame’ takes ‘for’, ‘criticise’ takes ‘for’."},{o:["beaten","made","met","struck"],a:3,x:"strike a nerve = touch on something people care about strongly."},{o:["attention","interest","concern","curiosity"],a:1,x:"take an interest in something."},{o:["wave","manner","style","fad"],a:3,x:"a passing fad = something popular for only a short time."},{o:["hint","mark","trace","sign"],a:3,x:"show no sign of doing something – a fixed phrase."}]},2:{title:"The lost art of handwriting",text:`For most of the twentieth century, learning to write neatly was {0} of the central goals of primary education. Today, many children spend more time typing than writing by hand, and some schools have stopped teaching joined-up writing altogether. Does it matter?

A growing body of research suggests that it {1}. Studies have found that students who take notes by hand tend to remember more {2} those who type, apparently because writing is slower and forces them to summarise ideas in their own words {3} than copying them word for word. Brain scans of young children show that forming letters by hand activates areas linked to reading in a way that pressing keys does {4}.

{5} of this means that keyboards should be banned from classrooms. Typing is an essential skill, and for pupils with conditions such as dyslexia, it can be a lifeline. But researchers argue that handwriting should not be abandoned {6} quickly. As one put it, ‘We're only just beginning to understand what we might lose.’ In the meantime, there are signs of a small revival: sales of notebooks and fountain pens have risen sharply, {7} among young adults, many of {8} say they enjoy the slower, more personal feel of writing by hand.`,example:{a:"one"},gaps:[{a:["does"],x:"The auxiliary replaces the verb: it does (matter)."},{a:["than"],x:"more than – a comparison."},{a:["rather"],x:"rather than = instead of."},{a:["not"],x:"in a way that pressing keys does not."},{a:["none"],x:"None of this means… = this doesn't mean…"},{a:["too"],x:"too quickly = more quickly than is sensible."},{a:["especially","particularly","notably"],x:"especially = more than others."},{a:["whom"],x:"many of whom – a relative pronoun after a preposition."}]},3:{title:"Rivers of ice",text:`Glaciers have shaped some of the most {0} landscapes on Earth, carving
valleys and leaving behind lakes and scattered boulders. Today, however, they are
retreating at a rate that has {1} scientists. In the Alps, some
glaciers have lost more than half their volume in a single {2}, and
many smaller ones are expected to {3} entirely within decades.
The consequences extend well beyond the loss of {4} scenery.
Millions of people depend on meltwater for drinking, farming and electricity, and
the {5} of these supplies could become a source of conflict. There
is also a {6} risk of sudden floods when lakes formed by melting ice
burst their natural banks. Glaciers are, in this sense, a highly {7}
indicator of a changing climate – one that is {8} to ignore.`,example:{w:"SPECTACLE",a:"spectacular"},gaps:[{w:"ALARM",a:["alarmed"],x:"Past participle after ‘has’: has alarmed scientists."},{w:"GENERATE",a:["generation"],x:"Noun after ‘a single’: in a single generation."},{w:"APPEAR",a:["disappear"],x:"Negative verb: disappear entirely."},{w:"BREATH",a:["breathtaking"],x:"Compound adjective: breathtaking scenery."},{w:"RELY",a:["reliability","unreliability"],x:"Noun after ‘the’: the (un)reliability of these supplies."},{w:"GROW",a:["growing"],x:"Adjective before a noun: a growing risk."},{w:"SENSE",a:["sensitive"],x:"Adjective after ‘highly’: a highly sensitive indicator = one that reacts to small changes."},{w:"POSSIBLE",a:["impossible"],x:"Negative adjective: impossible to ignore."}]},4:{example:{s1:"The concert was cancelled because of the storm.",key:"CALLED",pre:"The storm meant that the concert",ans:"had to be called off",post:"."},items:[{s1:"‘You really must visit us in Scotland,’ Helen said to me.",key:"INSISTED",pre:"Helen",post:"them in Scotland.",a:[["insisted on my","insisted on me"],["visiting"]],x:"insist on + object + -ing."},{s1:"There were fewer people at the meeting than we had expected.",key:"TURNED",pre:"Fewer people",post:"the meeting than we had expected.",a:[["turned up","turned out"],["for","at","to"]],x:"turn up for / turn out for = come to an event."},{s1:"I didn't know that the museum was closed on Mondays.",key:"UNAWARE",pre:"I",post:"the museum was closed on Mondays.",a:[["was unaware"],["that"]],x:"be unaware that + clause."},{s1:"We couldn't have finished the project without your help.",key:"HAD",pre:"If you",post:", we couldn't have finished the project.",a:[["had not helped","hadn't helped"],["us"]],x:"Third conditional: If you hadn't helped us…"},{s1:"She was determined to finish the marathon, even though her ankle was painful.",key:"SPITE",pre:"She was determined to finish the marathon",post:"ankle.",a:[["in spite of"],["her painful"]],x:"in spite of + noun phrase: in spite of her painful ankle."},{s1:"It's pointless to complain now – the decision has been made.",key:"USE",pre:"It's",post:"now – the decision has been made.",a:[["no use","not any use"],["complaining"]],x:"It's no use + -ing = it's pointless to…"}]},5:{title:"A voice you know",intro:"an article about a voice actor",paras:["You have almost certainly heard Maya Lindgren's voice, even if you have never heard her name. She has been a talking fox, a nervous robot, a supermarket's announcements and, for the last six years, the voice that tells millions of drivers to turn left in two hundred metres. ‘People sometimes recognise me in shops when I ask for something,’ she says. ‘They look at me strangely, as though they're trying to remember where they've met me.’","Lindgren fell into the work by accident. She trained as a stage actor, and for the first few years after drama school she scraped a living in small theatre productions. A friend who worked in radio asked her to fill in for a colleague who had lost her voice, and the producer liked her enough to book her again. ‘I thought it would be a stopgap,’ she says. ‘Twenty years later, I'm still here.’","The work is more demanding than it sounds. For animated films, Lindgren often records alone in a booth, without the other actors, reacting to lines that will be added later. ‘You have to imagine the whole scene,’ she explains. ‘There's no one to bounce off, no audience, nothing to see. Everything has to come from your imagination and your voice.’ A single sentence might be recorded thirty times with tiny variations until the director is satisfied.","Her navigation work, by contrast, is almost mechanical. She recorded several thousand phrases – street types, numbers, directions – which software then assembles into complete instructions. ‘It's strange to hear sentences I never actually said,’ she admits. ‘It's my voice, but not quite me.’","That unease has grown with the arrival of artificial voices that can imitate a real person after hearing just a few minutes of speech. Several of Lindgren's colleagues have discovered their voices being used in advertisements they never agreed to. She is now active in a campaign for laws that would give performers control over copies of their voices. ‘Our voice is our livelihood,’ she says. ‘It shouldn't be possible to take it without asking.’","Despite her concerns, she remains optimistic about the profession. ‘A machine can copy the sound of a voice,’ she says, ‘but not the choices an actor makes – where to pause, what to hold back. That's where the art is.’ Then, with a perfectly timed pause, she adds: ‘At least, I hope so.’"],qs:[{q:"What does Lindgren say about being recognised in public?",o:["It happens because people know her name.","People find her voice familiar but can't place it.","It only happens when she speaks to children.","She finds it embarrassing when it happens."],a:1,x:"People look at her ‘as though they're trying to remember where they've met me’."},{q:"How did Lindgren begin working as a voice actor?",o:["A theatre director recommended her.","She was trained for it at drama school.","She replaced someone who was unwell.","She applied for a job at a radio station."],a:2,x:"A friend asked her ‘to fill in for a colleague who had lost her voice’."},{q:"What makes recording for animated films challenging, according to Lindgren?",o:["The directors are difficult to please.","She has to create a scene entirely in her mind.","She has to learn long scripts by heart.","The other actors often arrive late."],a:1,x:"‘You have to imagine the whole scene… Everything has to come from your imagination.’"},{q:"How does Lindgren feel about her navigation work?",o:["It has made her famous.","It is the most creative part of her career.","It feels slightly unsettling to her.","It is too repetitive to enjoy."],a:2,x:"‘It's strange to hear sentences I never actually said… It's my voice, but not quite me.’"},{q:"Why is Lindgren campaigning for new laws?",o:["to increase actors' pay","to protect navigation companies","to stop companies using voices without permission","to ban artificial voices completely"],a:2,x:"Laws ‘that would give performers control over copies of their voices’ – ‘it shouldn't be possible to take it without asking’."},{q:"What does the final paragraph suggest about Lindgren's view of the future?",o:["She plans to stop working as a voice actor.","She is confident that machines will never replace actors.","She believes the profession will soon disappear.","She is hopeful, although not entirely certain."],a:3,x:"She is ‘optimistic’, but ends with ‘At least, I hope so.’"}]},6:{title:"Too many visitors?",intro:"four extracts in which writers discuss mass tourism in historic cities",who:"writers",stem:"Which writer…",sections:[{name:"Sofia Bianchi",text:["Anyone who has tried to cross the Rialto Bridge in August knows that Venice has a problem. The city receives more than twenty million visitors a year, most of them day-trippers who spend little and leave behind crowds and litter. Meanwhile, residents are being priced out as flats are turned into holiday rentals. Entry fees for day visitors are a start, but they are set far too low to change behaviour. What is needed is a strict limit on the number of short-term rentals – otherwise the city will become a museum with nobody living in it."]},{name:"James Holloway",text:["It has become fashionable to complain about ‘overtourism’, but we should be careful. Tourism supports millions of jobs, and many of the cities now calling for fewer visitors were desperate for them a generation ago. The real problem is not numbers but concentration: everyone wants to see the same five sights at the same time of day. Better information, timed tickets and promotion of less famous neighbourhoods could spread visitors more evenly without turning anyone away."]},{name:"Elena Novak",text:["I live in Prague's old town, and I have watched my street change beyond recognition. The bakery has become a souvenir shop, and my neighbours have been replaced by a rotating cast of weekend guests. Tourism has brought money, certainly, but much of it goes to international companies rather than local people. I am not against visitors; I simply want our city to remain a place where ordinary people can afford to live. Controlling holiday rentals would do more than anything else to achieve that."]},{name:"Marcus Lee",text:["Much of the anger about tourism is really anger about housing, and it is being directed at the wrong target. Short-term rentals are a factor, but in most cities they account for a small share of homes. The deeper causes are decades of too little house building and rising prices everywhere. Blaming tourists is politically convenient, but it will not make homes affordable. Cities should build more housing – and continue to welcome the visitors whose spending keeps local economies alive."]}],qs:[{q:"shares Novak's view on the most effective way to protect residents?",a:0,x:"Bianchi: ‘a strict limit on the number of short-term rentals’; Novak: ‘Controlling holiday rentals would do more than anything else.’"},{q:"has a different view from the others about the role of holiday rentals in the housing problem?",a:3,x:"Lee: rentals ‘account for a small share of homes’; the real causes are too little building. Bianchi and Novak see rentals as central."},{q:"expresses a similar view to Lee about the economic benefits of tourism?",a:1,x:"Holloway: ‘Tourism supports millions of jobs’; Lee: visitors' spending ‘keeps local economies alive’."},{q:"takes a different view from Holloway on whether the number of visitors should be reduced?",a:0,x:"Holloway wants to spread visitors ‘without turning anyone away’; Bianchi wants fees high enough ‘to change behaviour’ and calls the numbers a problem."}]},7:{title:"The piano in the barn",intro:"an article in which a writer describes restoring an old piano",paragraphs:!0,paras:["The piano had been in my grandparents' barn for as long as I could remember, hidden under a dusty tarpaulin between a broken lawnmower and several decades of newspapers. Nobody could say how it had got there. When the farm was sold last spring, my family was ready to take it to the tip.","{1}","The piano tuner who came to look at it, a softly spoken man named Arthur, was not encouraging. He lifted the lid, pressed a few keys that produced nothing but dull thuds, and shook his head. Mice had nested in the felt, the wood had swollen with damp and several strings had snapped. ‘It'll cost you more than a new one,’ he said.","{2}","So began the most absorbing six months of my life. Arthur agreed to guide me, on the condition that I did the work myself. Every Saturday I drove to his workshop with some part of the piano in the back of my car, and he showed me what to do with it.","{3}","The hardest part was the hammers – the small felt-covered pieces that strike the strings. Eighty-eight of them, each worn and moth-eaten, each needing to be removed, re-covered and replaced with exactly the right tension. After the first twenty, I was ready to admit defeat.","{4}","Along the way, I discovered something of the piano's history. Tucked inside the frame was a yellowed receipt from a music shop in Leeds, dated 1924, made out to a Miss E. Walker. My grandmother's maiden name was Walker, and her aunt, I later learnt, had been a music teacher.","{5}","The piano now stands in my living room. It will never be a concert instrument; the middle C still sticks slightly in wet weather. But when my daughter sits down to practise her scales, I sometimes think of Miss Walker, a century ago, doing exactly the same thing.","{6}"],options:["Arthur, sensing my frustration, sat down beside me and re-covered the next one himself, talking me through every movement. By the end of that afternoon I had found a rhythm, and the remaining sixty-eight took me just three weekends.","I asked him what it would cost if I did most of the work myself. He looked at me for a long moment, then laughed. ‘Less money,’ he said. ‘A lot more time.’","Pianos were once found in almost every middle-class home in Britain, and many have since been abandoned or destroyed.","I persuaded them to let me take it instead. I had no particular plan and no knowledge of pianos; I simply couldn't bear the thought of it being broken up for scrap.","I have since heard from two other families who found old pianos in outbuildings and wondered whether theirs could be saved. My answer is always the same: probably – if you have a patient teacher.","I learnt to strip and clean the keys, to glue the cracked soundboard and to replace the snapped strings. Arthur rarely praised anything, but when he did, it meant more than any compliment I had received in years.","It seems likely that the piano was hers, and that it passed to my grandmother when she died. Why it ended up in the barn, forgotten, remains a mystery."],key:[3,1,5,0,6,4],x:["The family was ready to take it to the tip – D: ‘I persuaded them to let me take it instead.’","‘It'll cost you more than a new one’ – B: ‘I asked him what it would cost if I did most of the work myself.’ Then ‘So began…’","Every Saturday with a part of the piano – F lists what the writer learnt to do.","After twenty hammers the writer was ready to give up – A: Arthur, ‘sensing my frustration’, helps; ‘the remaining sixty-eight’ (88 − 20).","The receipt made out to Miss E. Walker – G: ‘It seems likely that the piano was hers.’","E, about other families' pianos, ends the story. C (pianos in middle-class homes) doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"A year of giving",intro:"an article in which four people describe volunteering abroad",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Hannah, teaching in Nepal",text:["I spent a year teaching English in a village school in Nepal. I'd imagined I would be transforming children's lives, but the reality was humbler: the local teachers knew far more about what their pupils needed than I did, and the most useful thing I could do was support them. The first few months were lonely, as I spoke almost no Nepali. What changed everything was learning to cook with the family I lived with. By the end, I felt I'd received far more than I'd given."]},{name:"Tom, building in Peru",text:["I joined a project building a community centre in the Andes. I'm a trained carpenter, so I thought my skills would be in demand – and they were, but not in the way I'd expected. The local builders had techniques for working with mud brick that I'd never seen, and I spent the first weeks as their apprentice rather than their teacher. The altitude was brutal; I was breathless for a month. I'd go again tomorrow, but I'd do more research first into whether a project is genuinely wanted by the community."]},{name:"Aisha, conservation in Madagascar",text:["I volunteered on a turtle conservation project, counting nests and patrolling beaches at night. It was physically exhausting and occasionally dull – many nights we saw nothing at all. But the data we collected is used by scientists, so I knew it mattered. The organisation charged volunteers a considerable fee, which I only later discovered went largely to a company in Europe rather than to the project. I'd advise anyone thinking of volunteering to find out exactly where their money goes."]},{name:"Luis, healthcare in Ghana",text:["As a newly qualified nurse, I spent six months in a rural clinic in Ghana. The lack of equipment was shocking at first; I had to relearn how to do things I'd taken for granted. The Ghanaian nurses were extraordinarily resourceful, and I learnt more from them than in three years of training. Coming home was the hardest part. Friends wanted exciting stories, but I struggled to explain what I'd seen, and for a while I felt disconnected from my old life."]}],qs:[{q:"found that local people had skills they did not have themselves?",a:1,x:"Tom: the local builders' techniques ‘I'd never seen’; he became ‘their apprentice’."},{q:"found it difficult to readjust after returning home?",a:3,x:"Luis: ‘Coming home was the hardest part… I felt disconnected from my old life.’"},{q:"felt isolated because of a language barrier?",a:0,x:"Hannah: ‘The first few months were lonely, as I spoke almost no Nepali.’"},{q:"suffered physically from the environment?",a:1,x:"Tom: ‘The altitude was brutal; I was breathless for a month.’"},{q:"recommends checking how volunteers' payments are used?",a:2,x:"Aisha: ‘find out exactly where their money goes’."},{q:"had to adapt to working without basic resources?",a:3,x:"Luis: ‘The lack of equipment was shocking… I had to relearn how to do things.’"},{q:"realised that their role was less significant than they had expected?",a:0,x:"Hannah: ‘I'd imagined I would be transforming children's lives, but the reality was humbler.’"},{q:"admits that parts of the work were boring?",a:2,x:"Aisha: ‘occasionally dull – many nights we saw nothing at all’."},{q:"would check whether a community really wants a project?",a:1,x:"Tom: ‘I'd do more research first into whether a project is genuinely wanted by the community.’"},{q:"felt closer to local people through a shared everyday activity?",a:0,x:"Hannah: ‘What changed everything was learning to cook with the family I lived with.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t6",title:"Test 6",topics:"Light pollution · Why we tell stories · Ancient DNA · Mapping the ocean floor · Is a degree worth it? · A summer on an island · Chefs' first restaurants",parts:{1:{title:"Losing the dark",text:`For most of human history, a clear night sky offered a {0} of thousands of stars. Today, around a third of the world's population can no longer see the Milky Way from where they live. The {1} is light pollution – the excessive artificial light that spills from streetlights, buildings and advertising into the night sky.

The effects go well beyond spoiling the view. Many animals rely on darkness to find food, navigate or reproduce, and artificial light can {2} their behaviour in damaging ways. Newly hatched turtles, for instance, are drawn towards bright coastal hotels {3} than the sea. In humans, exposure to light at night has been {4} to disrupted sleep and a range of health problems.

Unlike many environmental problems, however, light pollution is relatively easy to {5}. Shielding lamps so that they point downwards, using warmer colours and switching lights off when they are not needed can make an immediate difference. Several towns have now been officially {6} as ‘dark sky places’, attracting visitors who are {7} to travel long distances for the rare experience of a truly black night. It is a reminder that some of the most precious things are those we only notice when they are {8} to us.`,example:{o:["view","glance","scene","look"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["offender","culprit","criminal","suspect"],a:1,x:"the culprit = the thing responsible for a problem."},{o:["differ","switch","vary","alter"],a:3,x:"alter behaviour = change it. ‘Vary’ and ‘differ’ don't take an object like this."},{o:["sooner","instead","rather","other"],a:2,x:"rather than = instead of."},{o:["bound","linked","attached","joined"],a:1,x:"be linked to something = be connected with it."},{o:["clutch","grip","seize","tackle"],a:3,x:"tackle a problem = deal with it."},{o:["admitted","permitted","recognised","confessed"],a:2,x:"be officially recognised as something."},{o:["prepared","arranged","organised","planned"],a:0,x:"be prepared to do something = be willing to."},{o:["denied","rejected","refused","declined"],a:0,x:"be denied something = not be allowed to have it."}]},2:{title:"Once upon a time",text:`Every known human culture tells stories. {0} is it about narrative that makes it so universal? One theory is that stories evolved as a kind of mental rehearsal, allowing us to explore dangerous or complicated situations {1} actually having to face them. By following a character through a crisis, we learn how we {2} respond ourselves.

Stories also appear to bring people together. Researchers studying groups of hunter-gatherers in the Philippines found that the most skilled storytellers were not {3} admired but were also more likely to be chosen as companions. Tellingly, many of their stories concerned cooperation and fairness – values {4} help large groups to function.

There is a darker side, {5}. The same power that makes stories effective teachers also makes them dangerous in the wrong hands. A compelling narrative can persuade people of things that are simply untrue, and misinformation spreads {6} faster when it takes the form of a story {7} than a list of facts. Understanding {8} stories work on us may be the best defence against being misled by them.`,example:{a:"What"},gaps:[{a:["without"],x:"without + -ing = not doing something."},{a:["might","would","could","should"],x:"A modal for a possible reaction: how we might respond."},{a:["only"],x:"not only… but also…"},{a:["which","that"],x:"A relative pronoun for things: values which help…"},{a:["though","however"],x:"though at the end of a clause = however."},{a:["far","much","even"],x:"far/much + comparative: far faster."},{a:["rather"],x:"rather than = instead of."},{a:["how"],x:"understanding how something works."}]},3:{title:"Reading ancient DNA",text:`Until recently, the idea of reading the genetic code of people who died thousands of years ago
would have seemed {0}. Today, thanks to a series of technical {1},
scientists can extract DNA from bones and teeth that are tens of thousands of years old.
The results have been {2}. We now know, for example, that most modern
humans carry a small but {3} amount of Neanderthal DNA, and that
ancient populations were far more {4} than once assumed, moving and mixing
across continents. The work is not without {5}, however. Some researchers
have been criticised for treating human remains {6}, with little regard
for the wishes of descendant communities. Most now agree that consulting these communities
is an {7} part of the research, and that their {8} should be respected.`,example:{w:"BELIEVE",a:"unbelievable"},gaps:[{w:"ADVANCE",a:["advances"],x:"Plural noun after ‘a series of technical’: advances."},{w:"REVOLUTION",a:["revolutionary"],x:"Adjective after ‘have been’: revolutionary."},{w:"MEASURE",a:["measurable"],x:"Adjective before a noun: a measurable amount."},{w:"MIGRATE",a:["migratory"],x:"Adjective after ‘far more’: migratory = moving from place to place."},{w:"CONTROVERSIAL",a:["controversy"],x:"Noun after ‘not without’: controversy."},{w:"CARE",a:["carelessly"],x:"Adverb describing how they treated remains: carelessly."},{w:"ESSENCE",a:["essential"],x:"Adjective after ‘an’: an essential part."},{w:"DECIDE",a:["decisions"],x:"Plural noun after ‘their’: their decisions."}]},4:{example:{s1:"I'm sure he forgot about the meeting.",key:"MUST",pre:"He",ans:"must have forgotten about",post:"the meeting."},items:[{s1:"Nobody told me that the flight had been cancelled.",key:"WAS",pre:"I",post:"that the flight had been cancelled.",a:[["was not","wasn't"],["told","informed"]],x:"Passive: I was not told that…"},{s1:"I'd love to go to the party, but I have to work.",key:"WISH",pre:"I",post:"to the party, but I have to work.",a:[["wish i could","wish that i could"],["go","come"]],x:"wish + could for a present situation you can't change."},{s1:"The new manager is completely different from the previous one.",key:"COMMON",pre:"The new manager",post:"the previous one.",a:[["has nothing"],["in common with"]],x:"have nothing in common with somebody."},{s1:"It's likely that prices will rise again next year.",key:"CHANCES",pre:"The",post:"rise again next year.",a:[["chances are that","chances are"],["prices will"]],x:"The chances are (that) = it is likely that."},{s1:"The weather was so bad that the match had to be postponed.",key:"OWING",pre:"The match had to be postponed",post:"weather.",a:[["owing to"],["the bad","the terrible","the awful","the poor"]],x:"owing to + noun = because of."},{s1:"I didn't expect the exam to be so easy.",key:"EASIER",pre:"The exam",post:"I had expected.",a:[["was much easier","was far easier","was a lot easier","was easier"],["than"]],x:"Comparative + than: much easier than I had expected."}]},5:{title:"Mapping the deep",intro:"an article about a scientist who maps the ocean floor",paras:["It is often said that we know more about the surface of Mars than about the floor of our own oceans. For Dr Helen Achebe, the claim is not an exaggeration but a daily frustration. ‘Until a few years ago, less than a fifth of the seabed had been mapped in any detail,’ she says. ‘We were navigating the planet with a blank space covering most of the map.’","Achebe leads one of several teams working on an international project to map the entire ocean floor by the end of the decade. The task is immense. Light does not penetrate far below the surface, so the seabed cannot simply be photographed; instead, ships send pulses of sound downwards and measure how long the echoes take to return. A single vessel, working day and night, can map an area the size of a small country in a year. The oceans cover more than seventy per cent of the planet.","Progress has come largely from an unexpected source: ordinary ships. Achebe's team has persuaded fishing boats, cargo vessels and even private yachts to switch on their echo sounders and share the data as they travel. ‘Each ship only covers a thin line,’ she explains, ‘but thousands of thin lines add up.’ The approach has its critics, who argue that data from amateur crews is too inconsistent to be trusted. Achebe acknowledges the problem but believes it can be managed. ‘We check everything against other sources,’ she says. ‘An imperfect map is better than no map at all.’","The discoveries have already been remarkable. Undersea mountains taller than any in Europe have appeared where charts showed only flat plain, and previously unknown canyons have been found close to busy shipping routes. Such knowledge has very practical uses: it helps to predict how tsunamis will travel, where fish will gather and where undersea cables can safely be laid.","Yet Achebe is uneasy about one potential use of her maps. Several companies are eager to mine the seabed for metals used in batteries, and detailed maps make that far easier. ‘I didn't get into this to help anyone dig up the ocean floor,’ she says. ‘But you can't make knowledge available only to the people you approve of.’ She believes the answer lies in strong international rules rather than in keeping the maps secret.","When she is asked what first drew her to the work, Achebe describes a childhood holiday on the coast of Ghana, where she spent hours staring at the horizon and wondering what lay beneath. ‘I still wonder,’ she says. ‘The difference is that now, for the first time, we might actually find out.’"],qs:[{q:"What point does Achebe make in the first paragraph?",o:["Space exploration has received too much funding.","Maps of Mars are less accurate than people believe.","Our knowledge of the seabed has been surprisingly limited.","Navigation at sea is still dangerous."],a:2,x:"‘Less than a fifth of the seabed had been mapped in any detail.’"},{q:"What does the writer emphasise in the second paragraph?",o:["the cost of the ships involved","the risks faced by researchers","the poor quality of existing maps","the scale of the mapping task"],a:3,x:"‘The task is immense’ – one ship maps a small country a year, and the oceans cover seventy per cent of the planet."},{q:"How does Achebe respond to criticism of using data from ordinary ships?",o:["She agrees that only professional crews should be used.","She admits it has some basis but believes it can be dealt with.","She says the data is only used for rough estimates.","She rejects it completely."],a:1,x:"‘Achebe acknowledges the problem but believes it can be managed.’"},{q:"According to the fourth paragraph, the new maps are useful because they",o:["reveal where valuable metals can be found.","have practical applications for safety and industry.","have made shipping routes shorter.","show that Europe's mountains are smaller than was thought."],a:1,x:"They help predict tsunamis, find fish and lay cables safely."},{q:"What is Achebe's view on mining the seabed?",o:["She thinks the maps should be kept secret.","She supports it if it is profitable.","She is uneasy about it, but believes regulation is the answer.","She thinks it is unlikely ever to happen."],a:2,x:"She is ‘uneasy’, but ‘the answer lies in strong international rules rather than in keeping the maps secret’."},{q:"In the final paragraph, Achebe suggests that",o:["she prefers looking at the ocean to studying it.","her interest in the sea has faded.","she regrets leaving Ghana.","her childhood curiosity may soon be satisfied."],a:3,x:"‘I still wonder… now, for the first time, we might actually find out.’"}]},6:{title:"Is a degree still worth it?",intro:"four extracts in which experts discuss the value of a university degree",who:"experts",stem:"Which expert…",sections:[{name:"Dr Karen Mills",text:["The financial case for a degree remains strong on average: graduates still earn considerably more over their lifetimes than non-graduates. But averages conceal enormous variation. A degree in medicine or engineering from a leading university pays off handsomely; a degree in some other subjects from less prestigious institutions may barely cover its own cost. Students deserve honest information about these differences before they commit to years of debt. Universities have been reluctant to provide it."]},{name:"Prof. Ahmed Rahman",text:["To judge a degree purely by its effect on salaries is to misunderstand what education is for. University teaches people to think critically, to weigh evidence and to engage with ideas they find uncomfortable – qualities a democracy badly needs. That said, I accept that the cost to students has become unreasonable. The solution is not to discourage young people from going, but for governments to fund higher education properly."]},{name:"Lucy Brennan",text:["As someone who runs a recruitment agency, I see how employers' attitudes are shifting. Many large firms have dropped the requirement for a degree altogether, focusing instead on skills and aptitude. Apprenticeships, in particular, offer young people the chance to earn while they learn and to finish without debt. We have spent decades telling teenagers that university is the only route to success, and many of them go simply because they've been told to. That simply isn't good enough."]},{name:"Dr Tomasz Nowak",text:["Whether a degree is ‘worth it’ depends on the individual. For some students, it opens the door to a rewarding career; for others, it is three expensive years spent drifting. The critical factor, in my experience, is motivation: students who have chosen their course with a clear purpose tend to thrive. What worries me is that too many young people apply simply because it is expected of them. Schools should do much more to help them think through their options carefully."]}],qs:[{q:"shares Mills's view that young people need better guidance before choosing to go to university?",a:3,x:"Nowak: ‘Schools should do much more to help them think through their options.’ Mills: ‘Students deserve honest information.’"},{q:"has a different view from Rahman about how the value of a degree should be judged?",a:0,x:"Rahman rejects judging a degree ‘purely by its effect on salaries’; Mills measures it in earnings and cost."},{q:"expresses a similar view to Nowak about why some young people go to university?",a:2,x:"Brennan: ‘many of them go simply because they've been told to’; Nowak: ‘because it is expected of them’."},{q:"has a different opinion from Brennan about whether young people should be encouraged to go to university?",a:1,x:"Rahman: ‘The solution is not to discourage young people from going’; Brennan promotes apprenticeships instead."}]},7:{title:"Keeper of the light",intro:"an article in which a writer describes a summer as a volunteer on a remote island",paragraphs:!0,paras:["The advert asked for someone ‘comfortable with solitude, practical, and not easily bored’. I was two of those things. The job was to spend the summer as a volunteer warden on a small island off the west coast of Ireland, looking after the old lighthouse cottages and counting the seabirds that nest on the cliffs.","{1}","The island is less than a kilometre long. There are no trees, no shops and, for most of the day, no other people. A boat brings supplies once a week, weather permitting, and in my second week the weather did not permit. For nine days I lived on tinned beans and the last of the bread.","{2}","My daily routine quickly took shape. In the mornings I walked the cliffs with a clipboard, counting puffins, guillemots and razorbills. In the afternoons I repaired whatever had broken – a gutter, a window frame, the generator, which broke more often than anything else.","{3}","Day-trippers arrived most weekends in July, forty or fifty at a time, and I became an unofficial guide. At first I resented the intrusion. By August, I found myself looking forward to Saturdays, and rehearsing facts about puffins in the shower.","{4}","The lighthouse itself has been automatic since the 1980s; a technician visits twice a year to check the equipment. But the old keepers' logbooks are still in the cottage, recording a century of storms, shipwrecks and the small events of daily life. I read them in the evenings by the light of a paraffin lamp.","{5}","When the boat came to take me back to the mainland at the end of September, I stood on the jetty for a long time before I got on board.","{6}"],options:["Their questions made me realise how much I had learnt without noticing. I could tell a razorbill from a guillemot at two hundred metres, and I knew which ledges the puffins returned to each evening.","The island was inhabited until the 1950s, when the last families moved to the mainland in search of work and schools.","The third – not easily bored – was the one I doubted. I have always been someone who needs to be busy, and I had never spent more than a day or two alone. I applied partly, I think, to find out whether I could.","I had gone to the island to discover whether I could bear my own company. I came back knowing that I could – and, more surprisingly, that I would miss it.","It was then that I learnt the first lesson of island life: plan for the worst. From then on, I kept enough food to last three weeks, and I never ran out again.","One entry, from 1937, simply reads: ‘Fog. Nothing seen or heard all day. Tea at four.’ I have thought about it often since. There is something strangely comforting in a life measured in such small certainties.","What surprised me was how quickly the hours filled. I had brought a pile of novels, expecting long empty evenings; by the end of the summer I had finished only two."],key:[2,4,6,0,5,3],x:["‘I was two of those things’ – C: ‘The third – not easily bored – was the one I doubted.’","Nine days of beans when the boat couldn't come – E: ‘It was then that I learnt the first lesson of island life: plan for the worst.’","A full routine of counting and repairs – G: ‘What surprised me was how quickly the hours filled.’","The writer became a guide to day-trippers – A: ‘Their questions made me realise how much I had learnt.’","The keepers' logbooks – F quotes ‘one entry, from 1937’.","Standing on the jetty before leaving – D: ‘I came back knowing… that I would miss it.’ B (when people left the island) doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"The first restaurant",intro:"an article in which four chefs describe opening their first restaurant",who:"chefs",stem:"Which chef…",sections:[{name:"Nina Kowalski",text:["I opened my first restaurant at twenty-six with money borrowed from my parents and a menu that was far too ambitious. I wanted to cook everything I'd learnt in five years of training, all at once. The critics were kind, but customers were confused, and for the first six months we lost money every week. What saved us was simplifying: I cut the menu in half and focused on dishes from my grandmother's village in Poland. Suddenly people understood what we were about."]},{name:"Kwame Asante",text:["My first place was a food truck, not a restaurant. I couldn't afford rent, and a truck let me test ideas cheaply and go wherever the customers were. The hours were brutal – I was cooking, serving, driving and doing the accounts – and I barely saw my family for two years. But I learnt more about what people actually want to eat than any cookery school could have taught me. When I finally opened a proper restaurant, I already had a loyal following."]},{name:"Sophie Laurent",text:["I had worked for fifteen years in other people's kitchens before I opened my own, and I thought I knew everything. I didn't. Cooking turned out to be the easy part; managing staff was much harder. In the first year, three of my chefs left, and I realised I was running the kitchen the way I'd been treated as a young cook – with shouting and endless hours. I had to change completely. Now I'm proud that people stay with us for years."]},{name:"Raj Mehta",text:["Our first restaurant opened in March 2020, two weeks before the pandemic closed everything. It was the worst possible timing. We survived by turning the dining room into a kitchen for home deliveries, and by cooking meals for hospital staff, which local businesses paid for. Those months taught me that a restaurant is really about its community. When we reopened properly, the neighbours we'd fed during the worst months were our first customers."]}],qs:[{q:"tried to do too much at the beginning?",a:0,x:"Kowalski: ‘a menu that was far too ambitious… everything… all at once’."},{q:"had to change the way they treated their staff?",a:2,x:"Laurent ran the kitchen ‘with shouting and endless hours’ – ‘I had to change completely.’"},{q:"had very little time for their family?",a:1,x:"Asante: ‘I barely saw my family for two years.’"},{q:"was supported by people from the local area?",a:3,x:"Mehta: local businesses paid for meals, and the neighbours were the first customers."},{q:"started with a business that could move from place to place?",a:1,x:"Asante's food truck could ‘go wherever the customers were’."},{q:"found an unexpected part of running a restaurant the hardest?",a:2,x:"Laurent: ‘Cooking turned out to be the easy part; managing staff was much harder.’"},{q:"received good reviews while the business was losing money?",a:0,x:"Kowalski: ‘The critics were kind… we lost money every week.’"},{q:"changed the business completely in response to a crisis?",a:3,x:"Mehta turned the dining room into a kitchen for deliveries during the pandemic."},{q:"became successful by drawing on their family background?",a:0,x:"Kowalski focused on ‘dishes from my grandmother's village in Poland’."},{q:"had regular customers before opening a restaurant?",a:1,x:"Asante: ‘I already had a loyal following.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t7",title:"Test 7",topics:"The science of habits · Multilingual children · Volcanoes · A translator's life · Remote working · A first marathon · Collectors",parts:{1:{title:"Creatures of habit",text:`Psychologists estimate that around 40 per cent of what we do each day is not the {0} of conscious decisions but of habit. We brush our teeth, check our phones and take the same route to work largely on automatic pilot. This is not necessarily a bad thing: habits free up mental {1} for problems that genuinely require our attention.

The difficulty {2} when we try to change them. Research suggests that willpower alone is a surprisingly weak {3} against an established habit, particularly when we are tired or stressed. What works better is to {4} the environment that triggers the behaviour. People who want to eat less sugar, for example, are far more likely to succeed if they simply stop keeping sweets in the house than if they rely on resisting them.

New habits, meanwhile, take longer to form than popular advice would have us believe. The often-quoted figure of 21 days has little scientific {5}; one study found that it took participants an average of 66 days for a new behaviour to become automatic, with enormous {6} between individuals. Missing a single day, reassuringly, made little difference. The key, it seems, is not perfection but {7}: small actions, repeated often enough, eventually {8} root.`,example:{o:["reason","result","cause","motive"],a:1},gaps:[{o:["size","extent","volume","capacity"],a:3,x:"mental capacity = the ability of the mind to deal with things."},{o:["arises","raises","rouses","lifts"],a:0,x:"a difficulty arises = appears. ‘Raise’ needs an object."},{o:["instrument","gadget","weapon","device"],a:2,x:"a weapon against something – used figuratively for fighting a problem."},{o:["exchange","transfer","convert","change"],a:3,x:"change the environment."},{o:["foot","basis","base","ground"],a:1,x:"have little scientific basis = not be supported by science."},{o:["variety","variation","alteration","modification"],a:1,x:"variation between individuals = differences."},{o:["coherence","constancy","consistency","conformity"],a:2,x:"consistency = doing something in the same way over time – the opposite of perfection here."},{o:["put","get","take","make"],a:2,x:"take root = become established."}]},2:{title:"Growing up with two languages",text:`Parents raising children with two languages are often warned that it will confuse them or delay their speech. There is little evidence {0} support this fear. Bilingual children may occasionally mix words from both languages in a single sentence, but this is a normal stage of development {1} than a sign of confusion. By the age of four or five, most can switch between their languages with ease, depending {2} who they are talking to.

In fact, growing up with two languages may bring advantages that go well beyond communication. Some studies suggest that bilingual children are better at focusing on a task {3} ignoring distractions, perhaps because they constantly have to choose which language to use. These findings are debated, {4} there is broad agreement that bilingualism does no harm and offers access to two cultures.

The biggest challenge, researchers say, is keeping the second language alive. Children quickly notice which language has higher status, and many stop speaking their parents' language once they start school. {5} this happens, parents should avoid forcing the issue, {6} can make children resent the language. Books, films and trips to visit relatives are far more effective. {7} important is that the language is associated with enjoyment rather {8} duty.`,example:{a:"to"},gaps:[{a:["rather"],x:"rather than = instead of being."},{a:["on"],x:"depend on somebody/something."},{a:["while","and","whilst"],x:"focusing on a task while ignoring distractions."},{a:["but","yet","though","although"],x:"Contrast: the findings are debated, but…"},{a:["if","when","once"],x:"If/When this happens…"},{a:["which"],x:"which refers back to the whole idea of forcing the issue."},{a:["what"],x:"What is important is… = the important thing is…"},{a:["than"],x:"rather than."}]},3:{title:"Living with volcanoes",text:`Around 800 million people live within a hundred kilometres of an active volcano, and
many of them would find the idea of moving {0}. Volcanic soil is exceptionally
{1}, producing rich harvests, and tourism can be a vital source of income.
Predicting eruptions remains {2} difficult. Volcanoes often show warning signs,
such as small earthquakes or changes in the gases they release, but these are not
always {3}. Some volcanoes rumble for years without erupting, while others
explode with very little warning. This {4} makes decisions about
evacuation extremely hard. Order people to leave too early, and officials risk being
accused of an {5}; leave it too late, and the consequences may be
{6}. Scientists increasingly argue that clear communication with
local communities is just as important as {7} equipment. Where people
understand the risks and trust the experts, evacuations tend to be far more {8}.`,example:{w:"THINK",a:"unthinkable"},gaps:[{w:"FERTILISE",a:["fertile"],x:"Adjective after ‘exceptionally’: fertile soil."},{w:"NOTORIOUS",a:["notoriously"],x:"Adverb before an adjective: notoriously difficult."},{w:"RELY",a:["reliable"],x:"Adjective after ‘not always’: reliable."},{w:"CERTAIN",a:["uncertainty"],x:"Noun after ‘This’: uncertainty."},{w:"REACT",a:["overreaction"],x:"Noun with a prefix: an overreaction = too strong a response."},{w:"CATASTROPHE",a:["catastrophic"],x:"Adjective after ‘may be’: catastrophic."},{w:"SOPHISTICATE",a:["sophisticated"],x:"Adjective before a noun: sophisticated equipment."},{w:"SUCCEED",a:["successful"],x:"Adjective after ‘far more’: successful."}]},4:{example:{s1:"I've never eaten such a delicious meal.",key:"EVER",pre:"This is the most delicious meal",ans:"I have ever eaten",post:"."},items:[{s1:"The council intends to close the library next year.",key:"INTENTION",pre:"The council",post:"the library next year.",a:[["has every intention of","has the intention of","has an intention of"],["closing"]],x:"have every intention of + -ing."},{s1:"I didn't recognise Anna until she spoke.",key:"WHEN",pre:"It was only",post:"that I recognised her.",a:[["when anna"],["spoke","started speaking","started to speak","began to speak"]],x:"Cleft sentence: It was only when… that…"},{s1:"‘I didn't take the money,’ said Tom.",key:"DENIED",pre:"Tom",post:"the money.",a:[["denied having","denied that he had","denied he had"],["taken","stolen"]],x:"deny having + past participle (at least three words: ‘denied having taken’)."},{s1:"The hotel was full, so we had to stay somewhere else.",key:"NO",pre:"There",post:"at the hotel, so we had to stay somewhere else.",a:[["was no room","were no rooms","was no space","were no vacancies"],["available","left","free"]],x:"There was no room available / left."},{s1:"Although she earns a lot, she never seems to have any money.",key:"MUCH",pre:"However",post:", she never seems to have any money.",a:[["much she"],["earns"]],x:"However much + clause = although… a lot. (Careful: at least three words – ‘much she earns’.)"},{s1:"They say the company is planning to move abroad.",key:"RUMOURED",pre:"The company",post:"planning to move abroad.",a:[["is rumoured"],["to be"]],x:"Impersonal passive: is rumoured to be + -ing."}]},5:{title:"Between the lines",intro:"an article about a literary translator",paras:["When Clara Weiss finished translating her first novel from Polish into English, she sent a nervous email to its author. She had changed the title, rearranged a dozen paragraphs and replaced a running joke about a famous Polish singer with one about an English footballer. ‘I expected him to be furious,’ she recalls. ‘Instead he wrote back: “Finally, someone who understands that a translation is not a photocopy.”’","Weiss has now translated more than thirty books, and she is used to the idea, still common among readers, that her job is simply to swap one language for another. ‘If it were that simple, machines would have replaced us years ago,’ she says. ‘Every sentence involves dozens of decisions. Do I keep the rhythm or the meaning? The formality or the warmth? You can rarely have both.’","The hardest texts, she says, are not the ones full of difficult vocabulary but those that depend on things English speakers simply do not share. Wordplay is an obvious example, but so are regional dialects, religious references and even the way people address each other. Polish, like many languages, has several forms of ‘you’, and a character's choice between them can reveal a whole relationship. ‘In English, all of that disappears,’ she says. ‘I have to find somewhere else to put it.’","Translators have traditionally been invisible, their names absent from covers and rarely mentioned in reviews. That is slowly changing. Several major prizes now reward the translator as well as the author, and some publishers print both names on the front. Weiss welcomes the change but is wary of translators becoming celebrities. ‘The worst translations are the ones where you can hear the translator showing off,’ she says. ‘Ideally, the reader should forget I exist.’","Machine translation has improved dramatically, and Weiss admits that she occasionally uses it for a rough first version of technical passages. But she is sceptical about claims that it will soon replace human translators of literature. ‘A machine can tell you what a sentence means,’ she says. ‘It can't yet tell you what it's for – why the writer chose this word, at this moment, for this reader.’","Asked what keeps her going, Weiss mentions the letters she receives from readers who have discovered a writer they would otherwise never have read. ‘Most of the world's great books were written in languages most of us don't speak,’ she says. ‘Without translation, we'd each be living in a very small room.’"],qs:[{q:"Why does Weiss describe the reaction of the author of her first translation?",o:["to explain why she changed the title","to suggest that she should have asked permission first","to show how difficult authors can be","to illustrate that a good translation is not a literal copy"],a:3,x:"The author praised her: ‘a translation is not a photocopy’."},{q:"What point does Weiss make in the second paragraph?",o:["Machines will soon be able to translate novels.","Translation involves constant choices between competing qualities.","Readers generally understand what translators do.","Keeping the rhythm is more important than keeping the meaning."],a:1,x:"‘Do I keep the rhythm or the meaning? The formality or the warmth? You can rarely have both.’"},{q:"According to the third paragraph, what makes some texts particularly hard to translate?",o:["their use of unusual vocabulary","their reliance on cultural knowledge readers do not share","the author's refusal to accept changes","their length and complexity"],a:1,x:"Texts that ‘depend on things English speakers simply do not share’: wordplay, dialect, forms of address."},{q:"How does Weiss feel about translators becoming better known?",o:["She approves, but does not want translators to draw attention to themselves.","She is indifferent to it.","She thinks it will damage sales of translated books.","She thinks it is long overdue and wants more publicity."],a:0,x:"She ‘welcomes the change but is wary of translators becoming celebrities’: ‘the reader should forget I exist’."},{q:"What is Weiss's opinion of machine translation?",o:["It is more accurate than human translators.","It will replace literary translators within a few years.","It is useless for any kind of text.","It is useful in some cases but cannot understand a writer's purpose."],a:3,x:"She uses it for technical passages, but ‘it can't yet tell you what it's for’."},{q:"What does Weiss mean by ‘living in a very small room’?",o:["Translators often feel isolated.","Without translation, our access to literature would be very limited.","Most readers only read books from their own country.","Translators are poorly paid."],a:1,x:"‘Most of the world's great books were written in languages most of us don't speak.’"}]},6:{title:"Working from home",intro:"four extracts in which experts discuss the rise of remote working",who:"experts",stem:"Which expert…",sections:[{name:"Dr Hannah Reid",text:["The shift to remote work has been one of the most significant changes to working life in a century, and on balance it has been positive. Employees save hours of commuting each week, and many report being more productive without the interruptions of an open-plan office. The risk, however, is that young employees miss out. Much of what we learn early in a career comes from watching experienced colleagues, and that is very hard to replicate on a video call. Companies must make deliberate efforts to bring junior staff together in person."]},{name:"Mark Ellison",text:["The productivity claims made for home working rest largely on what employees say about themselves, which is hardly objective. Studies that measure output directly paint a more mixed picture. More importantly, I believe that creativity suffers when people are not in the same place. The best ideas often emerge from chance conversations in corridors, and those simply don't happen online. I'd like to see most staff return to the office for at least three days a week."]},{name:"Priya Shah",text:["The debate is too often framed as office versus home, when the real question is flexibility. Different tasks suit different places: concentrated work at home, collaboration in the office. What matters is that teams agree on clear arrangements rather than leaving it to chance. I am concerned, though, about the effect on less experienced employees, who can easily feel isolated and overlooked when their managers rarely see them."]},{name:"Tom Brandt",text:["As an economist, I am struck by the effect on cities. Office districts that once buzzed with activity now stand half empty, and the cafés and shops that depended on commuters are struggling. On the other hand, smaller towns are benefiting as workers move away from expensive city centres. Whether employees are more or less productive at home is, frankly, the wrong question. The long-term geographical consequences will matter far more."]}],qs:[{q:"shares Reid's concern about the effect of remote working on younger employees?",a:2,x:"Shah worries about ‘less experienced employees, who can easily feel isolated and overlooked’; Reid says ‘young employees miss out’."},{q:"has a different view from Reid about whether remote workers are more productive?",a:1,x:"Ellison: productivity claims ‘rest largely on what employees say about themselves’ – the evidence is ‘more mixed’."},{q:"believes that the focus on productivity misses a more important issue, unlike the others?",a:3,x:"Brandt: productivity is ‘the wrong question’ – the geographical consequences matter more."},{q:"has a different opinion from Ellison about the best way to organise office attendance?",a:2,x:"Ellison wants most staff in the office three days a week; Shah wants flexible arrangements agreed by each team for different tasks."}]},7:{title:"Forty-two kilometres",intro:"an article in which a writer describes running her first marathon",paragraphs:!0,paras:["I have never been sporty. At school I was the girl who ‘forgot’ her PE kit, and for most of my adult life my exercise consisted of walking to the bus stop. So when I announced at my fortieth birthday party that I would run a marathon within a year, the laughter was not entirely unkind, but it was loud.","{1}","The training plan I downloaded began with alternating one minute of running and one minute of walking, three times a week. Even that nearly finished me. My lungs burned, my knees complained and I was convinced that everyone I passed was secretly laughing at me.","{2}","By the spring I could run ten kilometres without stopping, and I had started to enjoy it. Not the running itself, exactly, but the clear head I had afterwards, and the quiet satisfaction of doing something I had once thought impossible. I joined a local club, where I discovered that most of the members were far more ordinary than I had imagined.","{3}","Six weeks before the race, disaster struck: a sharp pain in my shin that turned out to be a stress fracture. The physiotherapist told me to stop running completely for a month. I sat on the edge of the treatment table and cried.","{4}","On race day, I stood among twenty thousand runners at the start line, feeling like a fraud. The first half went better than I had dared to hope. At thirty kilometres, I hit what runners call ‘the wall’ – a sudden, overwhelming exhaustion – and for several kilometres I alternated between walking and a slow, stubborn shuffle.","{5}","I crossed the finish line in five hours and twelve minutes, several hours behind the winner and a long way ahead of my own expectations. My daughter, waiting at the barrier, said I looked terrible. It was the best compliment I have ever received.","{6}"],options:["What kept me going was the crowd. Strangers who had read my name on my running vest shouted it as I passed, and a small boy held out a bag of sweets as though he were offering me treasure. I took two.","The truth is that I didn't know why I'd said it. Perhaps it was the wine, or perhaps it was a sudden fear that the next forty years would be exactly like the last. Either way, having said it in front of fifty people, I felt I had no choice.","Marathon running has grown enormously in popularity over the past twenty years, particularly among women.","There was a teacher in her sixties, a man who had only started running after a heart attack, and a young mother who trained with her baby in a running pushchair. Nobody asked how fast I was.","I've run two more marathons since then, both slower than the first. Speed, it turns out, was never really the point.","The club's coach refused to let me give up. She devised a programme of swimming and cycling that kept me fit without putting weight on the injury, and when I returned to running, carefully, I had lost less than I had feared.","But I kept going, partly out of stubbornness and partly because I had told so many people. After six weeks, the walking breaks had become shorter, and one morning I realised I had run for twenty minutes without noticing."],key:[1,6,3,5,0,4],x:["The loud laughter at her announcement – B: ‘The truth is that I didn't know why I'd said it… in front of fifty people’.","The painful first weeks – G: ‘But I kept going… one morning I realised I had run for twenty minutes.’","‘Most of the members were far more ordinary than I had imagined’ – D gives examples.","The stress fracture and tears – F: ‘The club's coach refused to let me give up.’","Hitting ‘the wall’ – A: ‘What kept me going was the crowd.’","After the finish – E: ‘I've run two more marathons since then.’ C (the popularity of marathons) doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"The collectors",intro:"an article in which four people talk about their collections",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Ruth, vintage postcards",text:["I started collecting postcards after finding a box of them in my late mother's attic – cards she had sent home from her travels as a young woman. I now have about four thousand, mostly from the early twentieth century. For me, the pictures matter less than the messages on the back: weather reports, complaints about hotels, declarations of love. They're tiny windows into ordinary lives. My husband thinks I'm mad, but he's come round to it; he now brings me cards from every market he visits."]},{name:"Gareth, old maps",text:["Maps have fascinated me since I was a boy. What I love is how wrong the old ones often are – coastlines in the wrong place, imaginary islands, whole regions left blank or filled with sea monsters. They show you what people believed as much as what they knew. It's become an expensive hobby, I'll admit; a good eighteenth-century map can cost more than a month's salary. I've had to set myself a strict budget, which I break about once a year."]},{name:"Mei, mechanical toys",text:["I collect wind-up toys from the 1920s to the 1960s – tin robots, dancing bears, cars that turn corners on their own. Most of what I buy doesn't work, and repairing them is the part I enjoy most. I taught myself using old instruction books and a great deal of trial and error. I've never been interested in keeping them behind glass; toys were made to be played with, and my grandchildren are allowed to wind up almost anything in the house."]},{name:"Omar, football programmes",text:["I've been collecting match programmes for fifty years – my first was from a game my father took me to when I was seven. I have every home programme my club has printed since 1965. It's not about their value, although some are now worth a fair amount. Each one takes me back to a particular afternoon, the people I was with, the weather. My son isn't interested at all, which saddens me a little. I've decided to leave the collection to the club's museum."]}],qs:[{q:"values the personal writing on items more than their images?",a:0,x:"Ruth: ‘the pictures matter less than the messages on the back’."},{q:"admits to spending more than they intend?",a:1,x:"Gareth sets a strict budget, ‘which I break about once a year’."},{q:"most enjoys fixing items in the collection?",a:2,x:"Mei: ‘repairing them is the part I enjoy most’."},{q:"began collecting after the death of a family member?",a:0,x:"Ruth found the postcards in her ‘late mother's attic’."},{q:"is interested in the mistakes in the items they collect?",a:1,x:"Gareth: ‘What I love is how wrong the old ones often are.’"},{q:"has made plans for the future of the collection?",a:3,x:"Omar: ‘I've decided to leave the collection to the club's museum.’"},{q:"encourages other people to use the items?",a:2,x:"Mei: ‘my grandchildren are allowed to wind up almost anything’."},{q:"is disappointed that a relative doesn't share the interest?",a:3,x:"Omar: ‘My son isn't interested at all, which saddens me a little.’"},{q:"learnt the skills they needed without help?",a:2,x:"Mei: ‘I taught myself using old instruction books.’"},{q:"has a partner who has changed their attitude to the collection?",a:0,x:"Ruth's husband ‘thinks I'm mad, but he's come round to it’."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t8",title:"Test 8",topics:"Noise in cities · Sleep and memory · Fast fashion · A puppet maker · The future of zoos · Cycling across Iceland · Learning from failure",parts:{1:{title:"The sound of silence",text:`Noise is one of the most {0} forms of pollution. Unlike smoke or litter, it leaves no visible trace, yet it has a measurable effect on health. Long-term exposure to traffic noise has been associated with high blood pressure, poor sleep and even heart disease, and the World Health Organization now {1} it among the leading environmental threats in Europe.

Cities are, by their nature, noisy places, and some sound is simply the {2} of urban life. The problem arises when there is no {3} from it. Research suggests that even brief periods in quiet places – a park, a courtyard, a library – can help the body to recover. Yet in many cities such spaces are becoming harder to find.

Some authorities are now taking the issue more seriously. Paris has installed ‘noise radars’ that {4} fines on drivers whose vehicles exceed legal limits, while several cities have {5} quiet zones in which loud activities are restricted. Architects, too, are paying more attention to sound, designing buildings that {6} out traffic noise and creating courtyards that act as pockets of calm. None of this will make cities silent, nor should it. But it may help to {7} the right balance between the energy of urban life and the peace that everyone needs from {8} to time.`,example:{o:["overseen","overcome","overtaken","overlooked"],a:3},gaps:[{o:["grades","scores","ranks","marks"],a:2,x:"rank something among/as… = consider it to be at a certain level."},{o:["fee","fare","price","charge"],a:2,x:"the price of something = an unpleasant thing you must accept to have it."},{o:["departure","escape","outlet","exit"],a:1,x:"no escape from something."},{o:["enforce","apply","charge","impose"],a:3,x:"impose a fine on somebody."},{o:["presented","introduced","performed","produced"],a:1,x:"introduce a new scheme, law or zone."},{o:["seal","close","shut","lock"],a:2,x:"shut out noise = stop it coming in."},{o:["knock","hit","strike","beat"],a:2,x:"strike a balance – a fixed collocation."},{o:["period","time","moment","hour"],a:1,x:"from time to time = sometimes."}]},2:{title:"Sleeping on it",text:`The advice to ‘sleep on’ a difficult decision turns out to have a scientific basis. {0} we sleep, the brain does not simply switch off. {1} the contrary, it is busy sorting through the day's experiences, strengthening some memories and discarding others. Experiments have shown that people who sleep after learning a new skill perform significantly better the next day than those {2} stay awake for the same period.

Sleep may also help us to solve problems. In one study, participants were given a puzzle with a hidden shortcut. After a night's sleep, they were more than twice {3} likely to discover it as those who had stayed awake. The sleeping brain, it seems, is able to make connections that escape us during the day.

None {4} this will come as a surprise to anyone who has woken up with the answer to a problem that had defeated them the night before. What is surprising is how {5} of us take sleep seriously. Surveys suggest that around a third of adults regularly get less than the recommended seven hours, {6} teenagers are among the worst affected. Given {7} we now know, cutting back on sleep to study or work longer may be a false economy: the extra hours awake may be worth far less {8} we think.`,example:{a:"While"},gaps:[{a:["on"],x:"On the contrary = the opposite is true."},{a:["who","that"],x:"those who = the people who."},{a:["as"],x:"twice as likely as."},{a:["of"],x:"None of this…"},{a:["few"],x:"how few of us – a small number."},{a:["and","with","while","whilst"],x:"Adding a further point: and teenagers are among the worst affected."},{a:["what"],x:"Given what we now know = considering the things we know."},{a:["than"],x:"less than – a comparison."}]},3:{title:"The true cost of fast fashion",text:`Clothing has never been cheaper, and many garments are worn only a handful of times before
being thrown away. The {0} of this model, however, is increasingly questioned.
The textile industry is a major {1} of water, and the dyes used in
production are {2} for polluting rivers in many manufacturing countries.
Working conditions in some factories have also been widely {3}.
Some brands have responded by launching ‘green’ collections, but critics dismiss many
of these as {4}, designed more to improve the brand's image than to reduce
its impact. Genuine change, they argue, would require a {5} shift in how
we think about clothes. Buying fewer, better-made items and repairing rather than
replacing them would make an {6} difference. There are signs that younger
consumers are becoming more {7}: second-hand clothing is one of the
fastest-growing parts of the market, and clothing {8} services are springing up in many cities.`,example:{w:"SUSTAIN",a:"sustainability"},gaps:[{w:"CONSUME",a:["consumer"],x:"Noun after ‘a major’: a consumer of water."},{w:"RESPONSE",a:["responsible"],x:"Adjective: be responsible for something."},{w:"CRITIC",a:["criticised","criticized"],x:"Past participle after ‘have been widely’: criticised."},{w:"MISLEAD",a:["misleading"],x:"Adjective: misleading = giving a false idea."},{w:"FUNDAMENT",a:["fundamental"],x:"Adjective before a noun: a fundamental shift."},{w:"MEASURE",a:["immeasurable"],x:"Adjective with a negative prefix: an immeasurable difference = too great to measure."},{w:"CONSCIENCE",a:["conscientious"],x:"Adjective after ‘more’: conscientious = careful to do what is right."},{w:"RENT",a:["rental"],x:"Noun used like an adjective: rental services."}]},4:{example:{s1:"Sarah is too young to drive a car.",key:"ENOUGH",pre:"Sarah",ans:"is not old enough",post:"to drive a car."},items:[{s1:"The heavy rain prevented us from finishing the match.",key:"BECAUSE",pre:"We couldn't finish the match",post:"rain.",a:[["because of"],["the heavy"]],x:"because of + noun phrase: because of the heavy rain."},{s1:"I'm sure Paul wasn't at the party – I would have seen him.",key:"POSSIBLY",pre:"Paul",post:"at the party – I would have seen him.",a:[["can't possibly","cannot possibly","couldn't possibly","could not possibly"],["have been"]],x:"Negative certainty about the past: can't possibly have been."},{s1:"Could you tell me where the station is?",key:"WONDERING",pre:"I",post:"tell me where the station is.",a:[["was wondering if","was wondering whether"],["you could"]],x:"A polite request: I was wondering if you could…"},{s1:"People don't use typewriters any more.",key:"USED",pre:"People",post:"typewriters, but they don't any more.",a:[["used to"],["use","write on","type on"]],x:"used to + infinitive for a past habit."},{s1:"It was careless of you to leave the door unlocked.",key:"OUGHT",pre:"You",post:"the door unlocked.",a:[["ought not to have","oughtn't to have"],["left"]],x:"Criticism of a past action: ought not to have + past participle."},{s1:"Mark regretted not studying harder at school.",key:"WISHED",pre:"Mark",post:"harder at school.",a:[["wished he had","wished he'd","wished that he had"],["studied","worked"]],x:"wish + past perfect for a past regret."}]},5:{title:"Bringing wood to life",intro:"an article about a puppet maker",paras:["In a converted chapel on the edge of Bristol, surrounded by half-finished heads, carved hands and hanging strings, Tom Adeyemi is teaching a wooden heron to breathe. He lifts the bird's chest a few millimetres, pauses, lets it fall. ‘The audience will never consciously notice it,’ he says. ‘But without it, they'd never believe the heron was alive.’","Adeyemi has spent thirty years making and performing with puppets, and he is weary of the assumption that his art form is for children. ‘Puppetry is one of the oldest forms of theatre there is,’ he says. ‘In many cultures it was never considered childish at all. It's only fairly recently, and mainly in Britain, that people started thinking of it as something to keep toddlers quiet.’ The success of several large-scale stage productions with puppets has, he concedes, begun to change that perception.","He came to puppetry by an indirect route. Trained as a sculptor, he spent his twenties making public artworks that, as he puts it, ‘stood in town squares being ignored’. A commission to build a giant puppet for a street festival changed everything. ‘The moment I saw people's faces as it walked down the street, I realised I'd spent years making things that didn't move, and that movement was what I'd been missing.’","The work is painstaking. A single puppet can take months to build, and every joint must be tested and retested until it moves exactly as intended. But Adeyemi insists that construction is only half the story. ‘You can build the most beautiful puppet in the world, and it will still be dead if the performer doesn't understand how the creature thinks,’ he says. He spends weeks observing animals before building them, sitting for hours in parks and zoos with a notebook.","His current project, a production about migrating birds, has brought together performers from five countries. Rehearsals have not always been easy: each tradition has its own techniques, and there have been heated disagreements about something as simple as how a wing should fold. ‘It's frustrating at times,’ he admits, ‘but those arguments are where the new ideas come from. If everyone had agreed from the start, the show would be far less interesting.’","As the light fades in the chapel, he hangs the heron back on its hook. Does he ever worry that audiences will tire of puppets in an age of digital effects? He shakes his head. ‘On a screen, anything is possible, so nothing is surprising. When a piece of wood and cloth comes to life in front of you, you know it shouldn't be possible. That's the magic, and I don't think it will ever wear off.’"],qs:[{q:"What does Adeyemi's comment about the heron's breathing suggest?",o:["Audiences are more observant than performers realise.","The heron is the most difficult puppet he has built.","Puppets should copy real animals in every way.","Small details are essential to making a puppet convincing."],a:3,x:"The audience will never notice the breathing, ‘But without it, they'd never believe the heron was alive.’"},{q:"What is Adeyemi's view of the belief that puppetry is for children?",o:["It has prevented puppetry from receiving funding.","It has been true in most cultures.","It is a fairly recent and local attitude.","It is unlikely ever to change."],a:2,x:"‘It's only fairly recently, and mainly in Britain, that people started thinking of it as something to keep toddlers quiet.’ It has begun to change (D)."},{q:"What made Adeyemi move from sculpture to puppetry?",o:["He saw how people reacted to a figure that moved.","His public artworks were criticised.","A festival asked him to perform.","He had become tired of working alone."],a:0,x:"‘The moment I saw people's faces as it walked down the street…’"},{q:"According to the fourth paragraph, what does Adeyemi consider as important as building a puppet?",o:["testing every joint carefully","understanding how the creature behaves","keeping detailed notes on each puppet","choosing the right materials"],a:1,x:"A puppet ‘will still be dead if the performer doesn't understand how the creature thinks’."},{q:"How does Adeyemi feel about the disagreements in rehearsals?",o:["He avoids them by making all the decisions himself.","He believes they have damaged the production.","He finds them frustrating but productive.","He wishes the performers all shared the same tradition."],a:2,x:"‘It's frustrating at times… but those arguments are where the new ideas come from.’"},{q:"In the final paragraph, Adeyemi suggests that puppets",o:["work best when audiences cannot see how they are moved.","will eventually be replaced by digital effects.","need digital technology to survive.","impress audiences precisely because of their obvious limitations."],a:3,x:"‘When a piece of wood and cloth comes to life… you know it shouldn't be possible. That's the magic.’"}]},6:{title:"Do zoos still have a place?",intro:"four extracts in which experts discuss the role of zoos",who:"experts",stem:"Which expert…",sections:[{name:"Dr Lisa Grant",text:["Modern zoos are very different from the menageries of the past. The best of them play a vital role in conservation, running breeding programmes for species that would otherwise be extinct. The Arabian oryx and the California condor survive in the wild today largely because of such work. Critics who call for all zoos to close overlook this. The real problem is the large number of poorly run collections that exist mainly to entertain, and these should be subject to much stricter regulation."]},{name:"Prof. Daniel Kerr",text:["I don't dispute that some breeding programmes have succeeded, but they are the exception. The vast majority of animals in zoos belong to species that are not endangered, and very few captive-bred animals are ever returned to the wild. Money spent on zoos would do far more good protecting the habitats where animals actually live. I see no convincing case for keeping large, intelligent animals such as elephants in captivity at all."]},{name:"Maria Lopez",text:["As a former zookeeper, I have seen both the best and the worst. What convinced me of zoos' value was education: children who see a gorilla up close often develop a lifelong concern for wildlife that no documentary could inspire. That said, I agree that some species simply do not thrive in captivity, however good the facilities. Zoos should concentrate on animals that can live well within their walls."]},{name:"James Whitfield",text:["The debate about zoos is often conducted as if they were all the same, but the differences between institutions are enormous. Rather than arguing about whether zoos should exist, we should be asking what standards every zoo must meet. Independent inspections, public reporting on animal welfare and a requirement to contribute to conservation in the wild would force the weakest to improve or close."]}],qs:[{q:"shares Grant's view that poorly run zoos should be more tightly controlled?",a:3,x:"Whitfield: inspections and standards ‘would force the weakest to improve or close’. Grant: ‘much stricter regulation’."},{q:"has a different opinion from Grant about how much zoos contribute to conservation?",a:1,x:"Kerr: successful breeding programmes ‘are the exception’ and few animals return to the wild."},{q:"shares Kerr's view that certain animals should not be kept in zoos?",a:2,x:"Lopez: ‘some species simply do not thrive in captivity’; Kerr: no case for keeping elephants."},{q:"places a different emphasis from Grant on the main benefit of zoos?",a:2,x:"Grant values conservation; Lopez: ‘What convinced me of zoos' value was education.’"}]},7:{title:"Into the wind",intro:"an article in which a writer describes cycling across Iceland",paragraphs:!0,paras:["The wind in Iceland is not like wind anywhere else. It does not blow so much as push, relentlessly, from whichever direction you least want it. On my third day of cycling across the island's empty interior, it pushed me off my bike twice before lunch.","{1}","The route I had chosen, the Sprengisandur, runs for more than two hundred kilometres between the north and south of the country, with no villages, no shops and very little traffic. For much of the year it is closed to vehicles. Even in July, rivers flow across the road, and there are no bridges.","{2}","Crossing them was the part I had dreaded most. The water comes straight from glaciers and is so cold that it makes your legs ache within seconds. The technique, which I had practised in a much gentler river at home, is to unclip your bags, carry them across first, and then go back for the bike.","{3}","The landscape between the rivers was unlike anything I had seen. For hours at a time there was nothing but black volcanic sand and grey stones, stretching to a horizon broken only by distant glaciers. It was, in the most literal sense, a desert – and utterly silent whenever the wind dropped.","{4}","On the fifth evening, as I was putting up my tent beside a hut that offers shelter to travellers, a car stopped. The driver, an elderly farmer, wound down his window and asked whether I needed anything. I said I was fine. He looked at my bike, then at the sky, and handed me a bag of dried fish and a flask of coffee.","{5}","I reached the green valleys of the south a day later than planned, sunburnt, windburnt and thinner than when I started.","{6}"],options:["‘You'll need it,’ he said, and drove off before I could thank him properly. That night the temperature fell below zero, and I was very glad of the coffee.","Iceland's interior is one of the most sparsely populated areas in Europe, and fewer than four hundred thousand people live on the whole island.","The first crossing took me forty minutes. By the last, I could do it in ten, although my feet never stopped hurting.","By the second time, I had stopped trying to fight it. Instead, I learnt to wait for the lulls, pedalling hard whenever the gusts eased and stopping to rest when they returned.","People have asked me since whether it was worth it. It is hard to answer. What I can say is that I have never felt so small, or so completely alive.","That silence was what I came to value most. At home I am never more than a few seconds from a notification; out there, I went four days without hearing another human voice.","There were eleven of these crossings marked on my map, and I had read enough accounts of cyclists being swept off their feet to take them very seriously."],key:[3,6,2,5,0,4],x:["Pushed off the bike twice – D: ‘By the second time, I had stopped trying to fight it.’","Rivers with no bridges – G: ‘There were eleven of these crossings’, which leads to ‘Crossing them was the part I had dreaded most.’","The technique for crossing – C: ‘The first crossing took me forty minutes.’","‘Utterly silent’ – F: ‘That silence was what I came to value most… four days without hearing another human voice’ – before the farmer appears.","The farmer's gift – A: ‘“You'll need it,” he said.’","The end of the journey – E: ‘People have asked me since whether it was worth it.’ B (population facts) doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"Learning from failure",intro:"an article in which four entrepreneurs describe their first businesses that failed",who:"entrepreneurs",stem:"Which entrepreneur…",sections:[{name:"Chloe Martin",text:["My first business, a subscription box for handmade soaps, collapsed within eighteen months. I had spent almost all our budget on beautiful packaging and almost nothing on finding customers. I still remember the day I had to tell my two employees that I couldn't pay them. It was the lowest point of my life. But I learnt that a good product is worthless if nobody knows it exists, and my current company spends more on marketing than on anything else."]},{name:"Ravi Patel",text:["I launched an app for splitting restaurant bills between friends. The technology worked perfectly, but I'd never asked anyone whether they actually wanted it – it turned out most people were happy using a calculator. I lost my savings and most of my confidence. What surprised me was how supportive other founders were; several I'd never met got in touch to share their own stories of failure. Now I always test an idea with real customers before building anything."]},{name:"Anna Kowal",text:["My café failed because I tried to do everything myself. I was the manager, the cook, the accountant and the cleaner, and I refused to let anyone help because I didn't trust them to do things my way. I burned out in less than a year. My family had warned me, but I didn't listen. Today I run three cafés, and the most important thing I do is hire good people and let them get on with it."]},{name:"Sam Okonkwo",text:["My first company, which made eco-friendly phone cases, actually did quite well at first. The problem was that we grew too fast: we took on large orders we couldn't fulfil, and when a major retailer cancelled its contract, we had no money to fall back on. I felt I'd let my investors down, although to my surprise, two of them backed my next venture. I'm much more cautious about growth now – steady beats spectacular."]}],qs:[{q:"failed to check whether people wanted their product?",a:1,x:"Patel: ‘I'd never asked anyone whether they actually wanted it.’"},{q:"failed partly because they would not let others share the work?",a:2,x:"Kowal: ‘I refused to let anyone help.’"},{q:"received support from people they did not know?",a:1,x:"Patel: founders ‘I'd never met got in touch’."},{q:"enjoyed some success before the business failed?",a:3,x:"Okonkwo: ‘actually did quite well at first’."},{q:"spent money on the wrong things?",a:0,x:"Martin: almost all the budget on packaging, ‘almost nothing on finding customers’."},{q:"ignored advice from relatives?",a:2,x:"Kowal: ‘My family had warned me, but I didn't listen.’"},{q:"was given another chance by people who had lost money?",a:3,x:"Okonkwo: two investors ‘backed my next venture’."},{q:"found it especially painful to let down their staff?",a:0,x:"Martin: telling her employees she couldn't pay them was ‘the lowest point of my life’."},{q:"now puts promoting their business first?",a:0,x:"Martin's company ‘spends more on marketing than on anything else’."},{q:"has become more careful about expanding?",a:3,x:"Okonkwo: ‘I'm much more cautious about growth now.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t9",title:"Test 9",topics:"The benefits of walking · Forgetting names · Vertical farming · A glassblower · Compulsory voting · A first flight in a glider · Moving to a new city",parts:{1:{title:"One foot in front of the other",text:`Walking is so ordinary that we rarely {0} it a second thought. Yet a growing number of doctors are now {1} it to patients with a wide range of conditions, from depression to diabetes. Unlike many forms of exercise, it requires no special equipment, costs nothing and can be {2} into even the busiest of days.

The benefits are not only physical. Numerous studies have found that walking, particularly in green spaces, can lift mood and {3} anxiety. Some researchers believe that the rhythm of walking helps the mind to wander, which may explain why so many writers and philosophers claim that their best ideas {4} to them on long walks.

Despite this, the average adult in many countries walks less than ever before. Towns designed {5} cars, long working hours and the convenience of online shopping all {6} a part. Some cities are trying to {7} this trend by widening pavements and creating car-free routes, with encouraging results. It seems that when walking is made pleasant and safe, people are only too happy to put their best foot {8}.`,example:{o:["have","give","make","take"],a:1},gaps:[{o:["describing","prescribing","inscribing","subscribing"],a:1,x:"prescribe something to a patient – what a doctor does."},{o:["matched","equipped","suited","fitted"],a:3,x:"fit something into your day = find time for it."},{o:["soften","relax","ease","loosen"],a:2,x:"ease anxiety/pain = make it less severe."},{o:["appeared","came","arrived","reached"],a:1,x:"an idea comes to somebody."},{o:["about","upon","around","over"],a:2,x:"designed around something = with it as the main consideration."},{o:["play","have","do","take"],a:0,x:"play a part in something."},{o:["overturn","upset","invert","reverse"],a:3,x:"reverse a trend = make it go in the opposite direction."},{o:["front","ahead","onward","forward"],a:3,x:"put your best foot forward – an idiom meaning make a good effort."}]},2:{title:"What was your name again?",text:`Few social situations are {0} embarrassing as forgetting the name of someone you have just been introduced to. It happens to almost everyone, and it is not a sign of a poor memory {1} such. Names are simply harder to remember than most other information about a person. We may recall {2} someone does for a living, where they live and what they were wearing, yet their name escapes us completely.

Psychologists explain this by pointing out that names, unlike most words, carry {3} meaning. Knowing that someone is a baker tells us something about them; knowing that he is called Baker tells us nothing {4} all. With no associations to hold on to, the name has little chance of being stored.

The good news is that the problem can be reduced. The most effective strategy is to repeat the name aloud as soon as you hear it, and to use it at least once during the conversation. Linking the name to a vivid image also helps, {5} if the image is absurd. {6} you do forget, though, it is usually better to admit it than to spend the whole conversation avoiding it. Most people are far more understanding than we expect, not {7} because they have almost certainly done the same thing {8}.`,example:{a:"as"},gaps:[{a:["as"],x:"as such = in itself."},{a:["what"],x:"what someone does for a living."},{a:["no","little"],x:"carry no meaning = mean nothing."},{a:["at"],x:"nothing at all – emphasis."},{a:["even","especially","particularly"],x:"even if = whether or not."},{a:["if","when"],x:"If you do forget…"},{a:["least"],x:"not least because = especially because."},{a:["themselves"],x:"done the same thing themselves."}]},3:{title:"Farming upwards",text:`Vertical farms, in which crops are grown in stacked layers inside buildings, have attracted
{0} investment in recent years. Supporters point to their {1}:
they use a fraction of the water needed in conventional agriculture, require no pesticides
and can be located in the heart of cities, reducing the {2} for long-distance
transport. Crops can be grown all year round, {3} of the weather.
Critics, however, point out that the enormous amount of electricity needed for lighting
makes many vertical farms financially {4}, and several well-known
companies have gone {5} as energy prices have risen. For the moment,
they are best suited to salad leaves and herbs; growing wheat indoors remains
{6} expensive. Nevertheless, as lighting becomes more {7},
many experts believe vertical farming will play a {8} role in feeding cities.`,example:{w:"CONSIDER",a:"considerable"},gaps:[{w:"EFFICIENT",a:["efficiency"],x:"Noun after ‘their’: efficiency."},{w:"NECESSARY",a:["necessity"],x:"Noun after ‘the’: the necessity for transport."},{w:"REGARD",a:["regardless"],x:"regardless of = without being affected by."},{w:"VIABLE",a:["unviable","non-viable","nonviable"],x:"Negative adjective: financially unviable = unable to make money."},{w:"BANK",a:["bankrupt"],x:"go bankrupt = fail financially."},{w:"PROHIBIT",a:["prohibitively"],x:"Adverb before an adjective: prohibitively expensive = too expensive to be possible."},{w:"AFFORD",a:["affordable"],x:"Adjective after ‘more’: affordable."},{w:"SIGNIFY",a:["significant"],x:"Adjective before a noun: a significant role."}]},4:{example:{s1:"I prefer walking to taking the bus.",key:"RATHER",pre:"I",ans:"would rather walk than",post:"take the bus."},items:[{s1:"The last time I spoke to Emma was in March.",key:"SPOKEN",pre:"I",post:"Emma since March.",a:[["have not spoken","haven't spoken"],["to"]],x:"Present perfect negative + since."},{s1:"They postponed the concert because the singer was ill.",key:"PUT",pre:"The concert",post:"because the singer was ill.",a:[["was put"],["off","back"]],x:"Passive of the phrasal verb put off = postpone."},{s1:"It's a shame I didn't bring my camera.",key:"WISH",pre:"I",post:"my camera.",a:[["wish i had","wish i'd","wish that i had"],["brought","taken"]],x:"wish + past perfect for a past regret."},{s1:"She speaks French very well, and so does her brother.",key:"AS",pre:"Her brother speaks French",post:"she does.",a:[["as well","just as well"],["as"]],x:"as well as – an equal comparison."},{s1:"Nobody expected the team to win the championship.",key:"CAME",pre:"The team's victory in the championship",post:"everyone.",a:[["came as a","came as a complete","came as a total"],["surprise to","shock to"]],x:"come as a surprise to somebody."},{s1:"You must hand in your essays by Friday at the latest.",key:"LATER",pre:"Your essays must be handed in",post:"Friday.",a:[["no later"],["than"]],x:"no later than = by (a time) at the latest."}]},5:{title:"The glassblower",intro:"an article about a woman who became a glassblower",paras:["The furnace in Isla Grant's workshop has not been switched off in eleven years. It burns at over a thousand degrees, day and night, because cooling it down and heating it up again would take a week and risk cracking the brickwork. ‘It's a bit like having a very demanding pet,’ she says, gathering a glowing ball of molten glass on the end of a long steel pipe. ‘You can't just leave it for the weekend.’","Grant came to glassblowing late, after a career in accountancy that she describes as ‘perfectly pleasant and completely unmemorable’. A two-day course, booked as a birthday present to herself, turned into a six-year apprenticeship with a master glassblower in Venice. ‘I was forty and the oldest student he'd ever taken on,’ she says. ‘He told me I'd started twenty years too late. Then he spent six years proving himself wrong.’","The craft cannot be rushed. Glass must be kept moving constantly, turned and reheated every few seconds, and a moment's lapse in concentration can turn hours of work into a heap of fragments. Grant estimates that she threw away more than half of what she made in her first five years. ‘The hardest lesson was learning to accept failure without taking it personally,’ she says. ‘The glass doesn't care how you feel.’","Her work now sells in galleries in London and New York, and her large sculptural pieces can command thousands of pounds. Yet she still spends one day a week making simple drinking glasses, which she sells at a local market for a few pounds each. ‘The big pieces pay the bills,’ she says, ‘but the glasses keep me honest. If I can't make a plain tumbler properly, I've got no business making anything grander.’","Like many traditional crafts, glassblowing faces an uncertain future. Rising energy costs have forced several studios to close, and the long apprenticeship puts off many young people. Grant takes on two apprentices a year, and she is unsentimental about the challenge. ‘People talk about saving crafts as if they were endangered animals,’ she says. ‘But crafts survive because people want what they produce. My job is to make things people want, not to preserve something in a museum.’","At the end of the day, she places the last piece of the afternoon, a delicate blue bowl, into the annealing oven, where it will cool slowly overnight. Only in the morning will she know whether it has survived. Does the waiting still make her nervous? ‘Every single time,’ she says, smiling. ‘That's why I've never got bored.’"],qs:[{q:"Grant compares her furnace to a demanding pet in order to",o:["show how dangerous it can be.","suggest that she is fond of it.","explain why it is expensive to run.","emphasise that it needs constant attention."],a:3,x:"‘You can't just leave it for the weekend.’"},{q:"What does the writer suggest about Grant's teacher in Venice?",o:["He refused to teach her at first.","He was surprised by how quickly she learnt.","His early doubts about her turned out to be wrong.","He had never taught a woman before."],a:2,x:"‘He told me I'd started twenty years too late. Then he spent six years proving himself wrong.’"},{q:"According to Grant, what was the most difficult thing to learn?",o:["how to work quickly","how to cope with failure","how to keep the glass moving","how to judge the temperature"],a:1,x:"‘The hardest lesson was learning to accept failure without taking it personally.’"},{q:"Why does Grant still make simple drinking glasses?",o:["They bring in most of her income.","Her apprentices need to practise on them.","Local customers can't afford her sculptures.","They help her to keep her basic skills sharp."],a:3,x:"‘The glasses keep me honest. If I can't make a plain tumbler properly…’ The big pieces pay the bills (A)."},{q:"What is Grant's attitude to the future of glassblowing?",o:["She believes it will survive only if people want what it produces.","She wants the government to fund more apprenticeships.","She is pessimistic because of energy costs.","She thinks it deserves public protection."],a:0,x:"‘Crafts survive because people want what they produce.’"},{q:"What does the final paragraph reveal about Grant?",o:["She is tired of waiting for results.","She prefers making bowls to sculptures.","She finds the uncertainty of her work exciting.","She has become more confident with experience."],a:2,x:"She is nervous ‘every single time’ – ‘That's why I've never got bored.’"}]},6:{title:"Should voting be compulsory?",intro:"four extracts in which political scientists discuss compulsory voting",who:"writers",stem:"Which writer…",sections:[{name:"Dr Emma Walsh",text:["Australia has required citizens to vote since 1924, and turnout there regularly exceeds ninety per cent. The effect is not merely statistical. When everyone votes, politicians cannot win simply by energising their most committed supporters; they must appeal to the whole population, and the result is a parliament that reflects the country far more accurately. The fine for not voting is small, and people can still spoil their ballot if they wish, so the idea that it violates personal freedom is, to my mind, exaggerated."]},{name:"Prof. Richard Hale",text:["Compulsory voting treats a symptom rather than the cause. People stop voting because they feel their vote makes no difference or that no party represents them. Forcing them to the polling station changes neither of those things. I would much rather see reforms that give votes more weight – a fairer electoral system, for example – so that people choose to take part of their own accord. A vote cast under threat of a fine is hardly an enthusiastic endorsement of democracy."]},{name:"Dr Nadia Rahman",text:["I have some sympathy with the argument that compulsion produces more representative results; in countries where voting is voluntary, turnout is consistently lowest among the young and the poor, whose interests are therefore easily ignored. However, I am uneasy about the state forcing citizens to take part in any political act. A better approach, I think, is to make voting as easy as possible – automatic registration, voting at weekends and more polling stations in poorer areas – so that people want to vote."]},{name:"Paul Mercer",text:["Much of the debate assumes that higher turnout is automatically a good thing. I'm not convinced. Voters who have taken no interest in the issues may choose almost at random, or be swayed by whichever slogan they happened to hear last. There is a case for saying that those who care enough to vote are better placed to make the decision. That may sound elitist, but a democracy is not improved simply by adding uninformed votes."]}],qs:[{q:"shares Walsh's view that compulsory voting produces more representative results?",a:2,x:"Rahman has ‘some sympathy with the argument that compulsion produces more representative results’."},{q:"has a different view from the others about whether higher turnout is desirable?",a:3,x:"Mercer: ‘Much of the debate assumes that higher turnout is automatically a good thing. I'm not convinced.’"},{q:"has a different opinion from Walsh on whether compulsory voting limits personal freedom?",a:2,x:"Walsh calls the freedom argument ‘exaggerated’; Rahman is ‘uneasy about the state forcing citizens to take part’."},{q:"expresses a similar view to Rahman about how participation should be increased?",a:1,x:"Both want people to vote because they choose to: Hale through a fairer system, Rahman by making voting easier."}]},7:{title:"Flying on air",intro:"an article in which a writer describes her first flight in a glider",paragraphs:!0,paras:["There is no engine, which is the first thing everyone asks about. A glider is towed into the sky by a small aeroplane on the end of a long rope, and at around six hundred metres the pilot pulls a yellow handle and the rope falls away. After that, you are flying on nothing but air.","{1}","I had booked a trial lesson at a club on a hill in Wales, largely because a friend had dared me to. I am not a natural adventurer; I dislike ladders and avoid lifts where possible. As the tow plane taxied into position, I seriously considered making an excuse and going home.","{2}","My instructor, Gwen, was a retired head teacher who had been gliding for forty years. She had a reassuring way of explaining everything twice, and an unnerving habit of letting go of the controls without warning. ‘You're flying it now,’ she said, about ten minutes after the rope was released. I had not been aware of volunteering.","{3}","The secret of gliding, Gwen explained, is finding rising air. Warm air rises from sunlit fields and roads in columns called thermals, and a glider can circle inside one like a hawk, climbing hundreds of metres without any power at all. The difficulty is that thermals are invisible.","{4}","We found one above a quarry. The glider lurched, the instrument that measures climb began to beep with rising excitement, and Gwen talked me through a tight turn. We circled for several minutes, climbing steadily, the fields and farms turning below us like a slowly spinning map.","{5}","The landing was surprisingly gentle: a long, quiet descent over a hedge and a short bump along the grass. When the glider stopped, it tipped gently onto one wing, and I realised I had been gripping the seat so hard that my fingers ached.","{6}"],options:["Pilots look for clues instead: a circling bird, a certain kind of fluffy cloud, a dark field next to a lighter one. Gwen could read these signs the way I might read a street map.","It is a strangely quiet sensation. Without the roar of an engine, all you can hear is the rush of air past the canopy, which rises and falls in pitch as the glider speeds up and slows down.","I have been back to the club eleven times since. I still dislike ladders.","Gliding was first developed in Germany in the 1920s, when strict limits were placed on the building of powered aircraft.","To my surprise, it was easier than I expected. The glider responded to the gentlest touch, and after a few nervous wobbles I managed to hold it level, pointing towards a distant reservoir that Gwen had chosen as a target.","Then, far too soon, Gwen took back the controls and announced that it was time to head home. We had been in the air for forty minutes. It felt like five.","What stopped me was pride. The friend who had dared me was standing by the clubhouse with her phone, ready to film my departure, and I knew I would never hear the end of it."],key:[1,6,4,0,5,2],x:["‘Flying on nothing but air’ – B: ‘It is a strangely quiet sensation.’","The writer nearly went home – G: ‘What stopped me was pride.’","‘You're flying it now’ – E: ‘To my surprise, it was easier than I expected.’","‘Thermals are invisible’ – A: ‘Pilots look for clues instead.’","Circling in the thermal – F: ‘Then, far too soon, Gwen took back the controls’, before the landing.","After the landing – C: ‘I have been back to the club eleven times since’ – and still dislikes ladders. D (the history of gliding) doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"A new city",intro:"an article in which four people talk about moving to a new city",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Elena, Berlin",text:["I moved to Berlin for a job in design, knowing nobody. For the first few months I was desperately lonely; people were polite but seemed to have their friendships already fixed. What changed things was joining a rowing club. Sport gives you a reason to see the same people every week, and slowly acquaintances became friends. The city itself took longer to love – it isn't pretty in an obvious way – but now I can't imagine living anywhere else."]},{name:"Jonah, Glasgow",text:["I'd always lived in small towns, so moving to Glasgow for university was a shock. The noise kept me awake for weeks. But I was surprised by how friendly people were – strangers chatted to me at bus stops, which never happened back home. My biggest mistake was spending too much in the first term; city prices caught me out, and I had to take a weekend job in a bookshop. That job, ironically, became the best thing about my first year."]},{name:"Mariam, Toronto",text:["We moved to Toronto as a family when my husband got a job there. I'd given up a career I loved, and for a while I felt I'd lost part of my identity. The winters were brutal, too – nothing had prepared me for minus twenty. But the city's diversity made settling in easier than I'd feared: in our neighbourhood alone, there are families from forty countries. I retrained as a teacher, and I now feel I contribute something of my own."]},{name:"Luca, London",text:["I came to London for a year and stayed fifteen. What I love is the anonymity – nobody cares what you do, and you can reinvent yourself completely. Of course, that works both ways; it took years to build real friendships. Housing is the city's great weakness. I've moved flat nine times, often at short notice, and I still can't afford to buy. But every time I think about leaving, I find another reason to stay."]}],qs:[{q:"found that a regular activity helped them to make friends?",a:0,x:"Elena: the rowing club gives ‘a reason to see the same people every week’."},{q:"was surprised by how approachable people were?",a:1,x:"Jonah: ‘I was surprised by how friendly people were.’"},{q:"felt they had lost part of who they were?",a:2,x:"Mariam: ‘I felt I'd lost part of my identity.’"},{q:"enjoys being able to live without being noticed?",a:3,x:"Luca: ‘What I love is the anonymity.’"},{q:"had difficulty managing money at first?",a:1,x:"Jonah: ‘spending too much in the first term’."},{q:"found the climate hard to adjust to?",a:2,x:"Mariam: ‘nothing had prepared me for minus twenty’."},{q:"did not find the city attractive at first?",a:0,x:"Elena: ‘The city itself took longer to love – it isn't pretty in an obvious way.’"},{q:"has had trouble finding a settled home?",a:3,x:"Luca: ‘I've moved flat nine times, often at short notice.’"},{q:"found that something they did out of necessity became enjoyable?",a:1,x:"Jonah's weekend job, taken because he'd overspent, ‘became the best thing about my first year’."},{q:"moved because of their partner's job?",a:2,x:"Mariam: ‘when my husband got a job there’."}]}}}),LZ.data.papers=LZ.data.papers||{},(LZ.data.papers.c1=LZ.data.papers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1t10",title:"Test 10",topics:"The board game revival · Why we yawn · Birdsong in cities · A perfumer · Space tourism · Learning to swim at sixty · First jobs",parts:{1:{title:"Back to the board",text:`In an age of smartphones and video games, few would have predicted that board games would {0} a revival. Yet sales have grown steadily for over a decade, and board game cafés, where customers pay a small fee to play from a library of hundreds of titles, have {1} up in cities around the world.

Part of the appeal, enthusiasts say, is precisely that board games are not digital. Sitting round a table with friends offers a kind of social {2} that is hard to find online: people laugh, argue and read each other's expressions. Many modern games also {3} away from the aggressive competition of older classics, encouraging players to cooperate against the game itself rather than compete {4} one another.

The industry has been transformed by crowdfunding, which allows designers to {5} the interest of potential buyers before a single copy has been made. This has lowered the {6} to entry, and thousands of new games are now released every year. Some fear that the market is becoming {7}, with too many games chasing too few players. For now, though, the boom shows no sign of {8} down.`,example:{o:["relish","savour","enjoy","delight"],a:2},gaps:[{o:["jumped","leapt","bounced","sprung"],a:3,x:"spring up = appear suddenly in large numbers."},{o:["interruption","interval","intervention","interaction"],a:3,x:"social interaction = people communicating with each other."},{o:["run","move","pass","fall"],a:1,x:"move away from something = change to something different."},{o:["towards","contrary","against","across"],a:2,x:"compete against one another = try to beat each other."},{o:["scale","gauge","weigh","count"],a:1,x:"gauge interest = measure or judge how much there is."},{o:["walls","fences","gates","barriers"],a:3,x:"barriers to entry = things that make it hard to start a business in a market."},{o:["saturated","dampened","soaked","drenched"],a:0,x:"a saturated market = one with more products than buyers."},{o:["quieting","slowing","lowering","sitting"],a:1,x:"show no sign of slowing down."}]},2:{title:"Why do we yawn?",text:`Everyone yawns, yet scientists are still not entirely sure {0} we do it. For a long time, the most popular explanation was that yawning increases the amount of oxygen in the blood. This theory has now largely been abandoned, {1} experiments show that breathing extra oxygen does not reduce yawning.

A more recent idea is that yawning helps to cool the brain. Supporters point out that people yawn more {2} the air is slightly cooler than body temperature, and less when it is very hot. {3} this is true, yawning may help us to stay alert during long, boring periods, {4} would explain why we so often yawn in meetings.

Perhaps the most intriguing feature of yawning is that it is contagious. Seeing someone else yawn, or {5} reading about it, can trigger a yawn in most adults. Contagious yawning is more common between close friends and family members than between strangers, leading some researchers {6} link it to empathy. Young children, interestingly, are {7} likely to ‘catch’ yawns than adults. Did you yawn while reading this? If {8}, you are in good company.`,example:{a:"why"},gaps:[{a:["because","as","since"],x:"A reason: because experiments show…"},{a:["when","if"],x:"when the air is slightly cooler."},{a:["if"],x:"If this is true…"},{a:["which"],x:"which refers to the whole previous idea."},{a:["even"],x:"or even reading about it – emphasis on something surprising."},{a:["to"],x:"lead somebody to do something."},{a:["less"],x:"less likely than – a comparison."},{a:["so"],x:"If so = if that is the case."}]},3:{title:"Singing louder",text:`Birds that live in cities face a problem that is {0} unknown in the countryside: noise.
Traffic and machinery produce a constant low rumble that can make birdsong almost {1}.
Remarkably, many urban birds have {2} to this environment by singing at a
higher pitch, where there is less competition from background noise. Great tits in
European cities, for example, sing {3} higher notes than those in forests.
Some species have also begun singing at night, when the streets are quieter. This
{4} is impressive, but it may come at a cost. Higher-pitched songs appear
to be less {5} to females, and singing at night may leave birds
{6} of sleep. Researchers now believe that noise may be as {7}
to urban wildlife as the loss of habitat. Quieter road surfaces and better planning could
make cities far more {8} places for birds to live.`,example:{w:"LARGE",a:"largely"},gaps:[{w:"AUDIBLE",a:["inaudible"],x:"Negative adjective: inaudible = impossible to hear."},{w:"ADAPTATION",a:["adapted"],x:"Past participle after ‘have’: have adapted to."},{w:"NOTICE",a:["noticeably"],x:"Adverb before an adjective: noticeably higher."},{w:"FLEXIBLE",a:["flexibility"],x:"Noun after ‘This’: flexibility."},{w:"ATTRACT",a:["attractive"],x:"Adjective after ‘less’: attractive to females."},{w:"DEPRIVE",a:["deprived"],x:"be deprived of something = not have enough of it."},{w:"HARM",a:["harmful"],x:"Adjective after ‘as’: as harmful as."},{w:"HOSPITALITY",a:["hospitable"],x:"Adjective before a noun: hospitable places = welcoming."}]},4:{example:{s1:"Can you look after my cat while I'm away?",key:"CARE",pre:"Can you",ans:"take care of my cat",post:"while I'm away?"},items:[{s1:"The archive will let researchers see the letters only if they make an appointment.",key:"CONDITION",pre:"Researchers may see the letters",post:"an appointment.",a:[["on condition that"],["they make"]],x:"on condition that introduces the requirement: researchers must make an appointment before they can see the letters."},{s1:"Maya did not realise the significance of the discovery until she read the final page.",key:"DAWN",pre:"Only when she read the final page",post:"how significant the discovery was.",a:[["did it dawn"],["on maya","upon maya","on her","upon her"]],x:"Only when at the start requires inversion: did it dawn on Maya. Dawn on someone means become clear to them."},{s1:"The new evidence makes the researcher's original explanation seem doubtful.",key:"DOUBT",pre:"The new evidence",post:"the researcher's original explanation.",a:[["casts doubt"],["on","upon"]],x:"cast doubt on/upon something means provide a reason to question whether it is true."},{s1:"The council will probably reconsider its decision following the public meeting.",key:"LIKELIHOOD",pre:"There is",post:"its decision following the public meeting.",a:[["every likelihood"],["of the council reconsidering"]],x:"There is every likelihood of + noun + -ing expresses a strong probability. The six-word answer includes the subject, the council."},{s1:"Whatever objections they raise, we will not abandon the project.",key:"MATTER",pre:"We will not abandon the project",post:"raise.",a:[["no matter"],["what objections they","which objections they"]],x:"no matter what objections they raise means that their objections will not change our decision."},{s1:"The festival's success was largely due to the volunteers' commitment.",key:"ATTRIBUTED",pre:"The festival's success can",post:"the volunteers' commitment.",a:[["largely be attributed","be largely attributed"],["to"]],x:"be attributed to identifies the cause. Largely preserves the meaning that the volunteers were the main, but not necessarily the only, reason."}]},5:{title:"The nose",intro:"an article about a perfumer",paras:["Leila Haddad can identify more than two thousand smells with her eyes closed. She keeps no notes on most of them. ‘They're filed away in here,’ she says, tapping her forehead, ‘somewhere between my grandmother's kitchen and the first rain of autumn.’ Haddad is a perfumer – or, as the industry calls its most senior creators, a ‘nose’ – and she has spent twenty-five years composing fragrances for some of the world's best-known brands.","The path into the profession is narrow. There are only a few hundred qualified perfumers in the world, and training traditionally takes up to ten years. Haddad studied chemistry before winning a place at a perfumery school in France, where students spend their first year doing little but smelling – learning to recognise individual ingredients and to describe them precisely. ‘Most people have a vocabulary of about ten words for smells,’ she says. ‘We have to invent our own language.’","Creating a perfume, she explains, is less like painting than like writing music. A fragrance unfolds over time: the first notes, which are bright and fleeting, give way to the heart of the perfume after a few minutes, and finally to the deeper base notes that can last for hours. A single perfume may contain more than a hundred ingredients, and a new creation can go through a thousand versions before it is finished. ‘Changing one ingredient by a fraction of a per cent can change everything,’ she says.","The industry has changed considerably during her career. Many natural materials have become expensive or restricted, and synthetic molecules now dominate most perfumes. Haddad has no objection to this in principle – some of the most beautiful ingredients she works with were created in laboratories – but she dislikes what she calls ‘perfume by committee’. Increasingly, she says, fragrances are tested on focus groups and modified until they offend nobody. ‘The result is often something that nobody really loves, either.’","For that reason, the work she values most is her own small collection, sold only in a handful of independent shops. These perfumes are inspired by places and memories – the spice market in Beirut where she grew up, a pine forest after rain. They are not bestsellers, and she is relaxed about that. ‘I'd rather make something that a few people adore than something everyone finds pleasant.’","Asked what advice she would give to someone hoping to follow her, Haddad does not mention chemistry or training. ‘Pay attention,’ she says. ‘Smell everything – the bus, the bakery, the sea. Most people walk through the world with their noses switched off. Once you switch yours on, you'll never be bored again.’"],qs:[{q:"What does Haddad suggest about the way she remembers smells?",o:["She finds it harder as she gets older.","She links them to personal experiences.","She relies on detailed written records.","She has trained herself to forget unimportant ones."],a:1,x:"They are filed ‘somewhere between my grandmother's kitchen and the first rain of autumn’ – memories, not notes."},{q:"According to the second paragraph, what is a key part of a perfumer's training?",o:["studying the chemistry of scent","learning several foreign languages","working for a well-known brand","developing precise ways of describing smells"],a:3,x:"Students learn ‘to describe them precisely… We have to invent our own language.’"},{q:"Why does Haddad compare creating a perfume to writing music?",o:["Perfumes are often inspired by music.","Both are best done by a team.","A perfume develops and changes over time.","Both require years of formal training."],a:2,x:"‘A fragrance unfolds over time’ – first notes, heart and base notes."},{q:"What is Haddad's main criticism of the modern perfume industry?",o:["It relies too heavily on synthetic ingredients.","It produces fragrances designed to please everyone.","It no longer values experienced perfumers.","It has made perfumes too expensive."],a:1,x:"Fragrances are ‘modified until they offend nobody’. She has ‘no objection’ to synthetics."},{q:"How does Haddad feel about her own collection of perfumes?",o:["She is disappointed that it doesn't sell better.","She finds it harder to create than her other work.","She values it more than her commercial work.","She plans to sell it more widely."],a:2,x:"‘The work she values most is her own small collection.’ She is ‘relaxed’ about sales."},{q:"What does Haddad's advice suggest?",o:["Anyone can become a perfumer with enough training.","Most people have a poor sense of smell.","Future perfumers should study chemistry first.","Paying attention to smells can enrich everyday life."],a:3,x:"‘Once you switch yours on, you'll never be bored again.’ She doesn't mention chemistry."}]},6:{title:"Tourists in space",intro:"four extracts in which commentators discuss space tourism",who:"commentators",stem:"Which commentator…",sections:[{name:"Dr Sarah Klein",text:["The criticism that space tourism is merely a playground for the super-rich ignores history. Air travel, too, began as an expensive novelty, and the money paid by early passengers funded the improvements that eventually made flying affordable for millions. The same could happen here. The industry's environmental impact is real, but it is currently tiny compared with that of aviation, and the technologies being developed could have valuable uses on Earth."]},{name:"Marcus Webb",text:["I find it hard to be enthusiastic about billionaires spending fortunes on a few minutes of weightlessness while the planet faces a climate crisis. The emissions from a single launch, divided between a handful of passengers, are enormous per person. And unlike air travel, there is no obvious route to space flights becoming cheap enough for ordinary people. Governments should step in and tax these flights heavily."]},{name:"Prof. Amara Osei",text:["Whatever one thinks of the passengers, the engineering is genuinely impressive, and private companies have already cut the cost of launching satellites dramatically. That benefits scientific research and communications worldwide. My concern is regulation: the industry is growing faster than the rules that govern it, particularly regarding safety and the growing problem of debris in orbit. Governments need to close that gap urgently."]},{name:"Jamie Ross",text:["Seeing the Earth from space has a profound effect on people; astronauts often describe a new sense of how fragile and precious the planet is. If more people could share that experience, it might strengthen support for protecting the environment. Of course, at current prices only a tiny number can, and I share the unease about the emissions involved. But I wouldn't dismiss the idea that space tourism could change attitudes."]}],qs:[{q:"has a different view from Klein about whether space flights will eventually become affordable?",a:1,x:"Klein compares it with air travel becoming affordable; Webb: ‘there is no obvious route to space flights becoming cheap enough for ordinary people’."},{q:"shares Klein's view that the industry's technology brings wider benefits?",a:2,x:"Osei: cheaper satellite launches benefit ‘scientific research and communications worldwide’; Klein: ‘valuable uses on Earth’."},{q:"shares Webb's concern about the environmental impact of space tourism?",a:3,x:"Ross: ‘I share the unease about the emissions involved.’ Klein calls the impact ‘tiny’."},{q:"agrees with Webb that governments should take action on the industry?",a:2,x:"Osei: ‘Governments need to close that gap urgently’ (regulation); Webb: governments should tax flights."}]},7:{title:"Out of my depth",intro:"an article in which a writer describes learning to swim at the age of sixty",paragraphs:!0,paras:["For most of my life, I kept a secret that I found increasingly embarrassing: I could not swim. I grew up in a city with no public pool, my parents were not swimmers, and by the time I was old enough to learn on my own, the idea of admitting it felt worse than never learning at all.","{1}","So on the morning of my sixtieth birthday, I did something I had been putting off for half a century. I walked into my local leisure centre and signed up for a course of adult beginners' lessons. The receptionist didn't even blink.","{2}","There were six of us in the class, aged between thirty-five and seventy-two. Our instructor, a cheerful young man called Dev, began not in the water but beside it, asking each of us why we had come. The answers ranged from a grandchild's swimming party to a fear that had begun with a childhood accident.","{3}","Most of the first lesson was spent simply getting used to the water: walking across the shallow end, blowing bubbles, holding the side and letting our legs float up behind us. It sounds absurdly easy. For me, putting my face in the water was one of the hardest things I have ever done.","{4}","Progress came slowly, and then suddenly. For weeks I clung to a float, kicking furiously and going nowhere. Then one Thursday evening, Dev quietly took the float away while I was concentrating on my breathing, and I swam the width of the pool before I noticed.","{5}","Last month, a year after that first lesson, I swam a full kilometre without stopping. It took me forty minutes, and a man in the next lane overtook me eleven times. I did not care in the slightest.","{6}"],options:["Hearing the others speak made me feel less foolish. For the first time, I said out loud that I was frightened of the water – and nobody laughed.","I have finally stopped keeping my secret. In fact, I tell almost everyone – partly because I am proud, and partly because I hope that somebody, somewhere, will read this and book their first lesson.","Over the years, I became expert at avoiding the subject. On beach holidays I would sit reading under an umbrella; when friends suggested a swim, I would suddenly remember an urgent phone call.","Swimming is one of the few sports that can be practised safely into old age, and many doctors recommend it for people with joint problems.","I stood up, spluttering, and burst out laughing. Dev was grinning at the far side of the pool, holding the float above his head like a trophy.","Neither, as it turned out, did anyone else. Adult beginners, I later discovered, are far more common than people imagine – and they almost always wish they had started sooner.","Dev did not hurry me. He simply stood beside me, talking calmly about nothing in particular, until I finally took a breath and lowered my head. When I came up, the whole class applauded."],key:[2,5,0,6,4,1],x:["The embarrassing secret – C: ‘Over the years, I became expert at avoiding the subject.’","‘The receptionist didn't even blink’ – F: ‘Neither, as it turned out, did anyone else.’","The others' reasons for coming – A: ‘Hearing the others speak made me feel less foolish.’","Putting her face in the water was very hard – G: ‘Dev did not hurry me… until I finally… lowered my head.’","Swimming the width without noticing – E: ‘I stood up, spluttering, and burst out laughing.’","After the kilometre – B: ‘I have finally stopped keeping my secret.’ D (swimming and old age) doesn't fit anywhere."]},8:{title:"Where it all began",intro:"an article in which four people remember their first jobs",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Karen, surgeon",text:["My first job was stacking shelves in a supermarket on Saturday mornings when I was fifteen. It was mind-numbingly dull, but it taught me something I still rely on as a surgeon: how to stay focused on a repetitive task without letting my attention slip. The manager was strict about punctuality, and I've never been late for anything since. I earned three pounds an hour and felt like a millionaire."]},{name:"Tom, architect",text:["I worked as a waiter in a busy seaside restaurant during my university holidays. I was hopeless at first – I dropped plates, forgot orders and once served soup to a table that had ordered ice cream. But the other staff covered for me, and I learnt how much a good team matters when things go wrong. The tips were excellent, and I used them to travel around Europe looking at buildings."]},{name:"Aisha, journalist",text:["At seventeen I got a summer job in a care home. I'd expected it to be depressing, but it turned out to be one of the most rewarding experiences of my life. Many of the residents had extraordinary stories – one had been a wartime pilot, another a dancer – and nobody had asked them about their lives for years. Listening to them was what first made me want to become a journalist."]},{name:"Leo, software engineer",text:["I delivered newspapers every morning before school for three years. Getting up at half past five in winter was miserable, and I was paid very little. But I became very good at planning efficient routes, which is basically what I do now with computer code. More importantly, I learnt that if you commit to something, you do it even when you don't feel like it. I was proud that I never missed a single day."]}],qs:[{q:"says their first job inspired their choice of career?",a:2,x:"Aisha: ‘Listening to them was what first made me want to become a journalist.’"},{q:"did a kind of problem-solving that resembles their current work?",a:3,x:"Leo: planning efficient routes ‘is basically what I do now with computer code’."},{q:"made a lot of mistakes at the beginning?",a:1,x:"Tom: ‘I dropped plates, forgot orders…’"},{q:"was surprised by how much they enjoyed the work?",a:2,x:"Aisha expected it to be depressing, but it was ‘one of the most rewarding experiences of my life’."},{q:"was supported by colleagues when things went wrong?",a:1,x:"Tom: ‘the other staff covered for me’."},{q:"felt rich even though they earned very little?",a:0,x:"Karen: three pounds an hour – ‘felt like a millionaire’."},{q:"has always been on time since that job?",a:0,x:"Karen: ‘I've never been late for anything since.’"},{q:"never failed to turn up for work?",a:3,x:"Leo: ‘I never missed a single day.’"},{q:"spent their earnings on an interest linked to their later career?",a:1,x:"Tom used his tips ‘to travel around Europe looking at buildings’ – he became an architect."},{q:"learnt to keep their concentration while doing routine work?",a:0,x:"Karen: ‘how to stay focused on a repetitive task’."}]}}});
